A black box in your car?
While U.S. road designers are struggling to find money to repair a crumbling highway network, many are starting to see a solution in a small black box that fits easily into your car's dashboard.
Devices that track every kilometer the motorist drives and transmit this information to the authorities are at the core of Washington’s controversial efforts and state planning authorities to renovate an obsolete system of financing U.S. major roads.
Usually the dull designing of highways at once prompted intense debate and the emergence of various associations.
Libertarians met with groups of environmentalists to lobby for the government to be able to use these small cabinets, which would record mileage and perhaps even where you are going – and then this information would be used to compile a tax acreage.
The tea party is horrified.
The U.S. Civil Liberties Union is also deeply concerned and has mentioned a number of privacy issues.
And while Congress can't agree on whether to continue, some states don't wait for anything.
They figure out how they can move over the next decade to a system in which the driver pays per kilometre of the road he's driving on.
Thousands of motorists have already taken black boxes, some of which have GPS tracking.
This is a real necessity for our nation.
“It’s not a matter where we can choose too much,” said Hasan Ikhrata, executive director of the Southern California State Administration, which plans to start mileage tracking for each California motorist in 2025.
It will change the way we pay these taxes.
This technology is used for this.
The suggestion is that tax-fundeded Road trust fund, which Americans pay at gas stations, is down.
Americans don't buy as much gasoline as they used to.
Cars will travel a lot more miles per gallon.
The federal tax itself, 18.4 cents per gallon, hasn't risen over the past 20 years.
Due to the current high price of gasoline, politicians are reluctant to raise the tax even by only one cent.
"Gasoline tax is simply not sustainable," said Lee Munnich, a transport policy expert at the University of Minnesota.
His state has recently placed tracking devices in up to 500 cars to test this mileage payment system.
"In the long run, this seems like the most logical alternative," he said.
Experts call it a user fee according to the mileage.
It is no surprise that urban liberals like this idea because taxes can be used to change driver behaviour patterns, which, for example, can help curb traffic jams and create greenhouse gases.
Californian designers look at this system when planning strategies designed to meet the goals set out in ambitious global warming laws.
But Republican Bill Shuster (representative of Pennsylvania), chairman of the Transportation Committee, said he also sees it as the most functional long-term alternative.
Representatives of the free market from Reason Foundation are also inclined with the idea that drivers will pay for mileage.
"It's not a toll that disappears into a black hole," said Adrian Moore, vice president of strategy at Reason.
People pay directly for what they receive.
The initiative is also supported by two former U.S. transportation ministers, who urged Congress in 2011 to go in the direction of paying per kilometre.
Last year, the U.S. Senate approved a 90-million-dollar pilot project, which was supposed to involve about 10,000 cars.
However, the leadership of the House of Representatives rejected the proposal and did so because of the concerns of rural legislators representing members of the population, whose daily lives often meant to travel many kilometers for work outside the city.
However, several states and cities are moving forward on their own.
The most ardent is Oregon, which included 5,000 drivers in the country's largest experiment.
These drivers will soon begin to pay tax to the state for mileage, instead of a tax on gasoline.
Nevada has already completed the pilot project.
New York is currently researching the project.
Illinois tries to do this to a limited extent in trucks.
And the I-95 Coalition, which includes 17 state ministries of transportation, along with coastal states in the east (including Maryland, Pennsylvania, Virginia and Florida) is studying how they can implement this change.
However, this concept does not have only advocates.
In Nevada, where about 50 cars of volunteers were recently equipped with equipment, drivers were concerned that the government was able to track their every move.
"Fears about Big Brother tracking and similar matters have been a major concern," said Alauddin Khan, who is leading management strategy and operations at the Nevada Department of Transportation.
It wasn't something people wanted.
As the test project progressed, Nevada's U.S. Civil Liberties Union warned on its website: "It would be very easy to turn these devices into full-fledged surveillance devices."
There is no need to build a huge, cumbersome technological infrastructure that will inevitably expand to record everyday trips and arrivals of individuals.
Nevada is one of those states that is trying to find available technology that would allow the state to track how many miles the car will travel, but not exactly when and where.
If you can do it, Khan said, the public will be calmer.
The hunt for such technology brought some state agencies to a small company in California called True Mileage.
Initially, the company was not involved in the action to assist the government in taxing drivers.
She wanted to break through in the emerging market with legal liability insurance, where drivers would pay based on how much they drive.
But this device, which tries, attracts the attention of road network designers because it doesn't use GPS and delivers only a limited amount of information that is regularly uploaded using a modem.
"People will be more willing to go for it if you don't monitor their speed and their location," says Ryan Morrison, executive director of True Mileage.
There have been a number of major errors in some of these pilot programs.
There are much cheaper and less intrusive ways to do that.
In Oregon, designers are experimenting with giving drivers a choice.
They can choose a device with or without GPS.
Or they don't have to take any facilities and instead decide to pay a flat-rate fee based on the average mileage of all residents of the state.
In other places, they hope that the prudent public will accept this concept if the device is able to do more, not less.
In the city of New York City, traffic officials are trying to develop a tax facility that would be equipped so that, in addition, it is possible to pay fees for parking hours, to provide mileage insurance and to collect data on the speed of other drivers, so motorists can avoid traffic jams.
"Motorists will be willing to get involved because of the value of the benefits offered to them," according to the city's projection paper.
However, some transport designers would like to know whether all discussions about paying per kilometre are not just a huge proxy issue.
Officials at the Metropolitan Transportation Committee in the San Francisca bay says Congress could very easily solve the problem with the bankrupt Road Trust Fund by raising the gasoline tax.
A one-off or annual tax could be levied on drivers of hybrid or other cars that don't consume much fuel, so they're going to pay a fair share.
"There's no need for any radical operation when it's rich enough to take an aspirin," said Randy Rentschler, director of the Legislation and Public Affairs Committee.
If that's what we're going to do, hundreds of millions of drivers will be concerned about their privacy and a lot of other things.
Sobotka negotiates on behalf of ČSSD.
Social democracies crumble, the people’s people and Babišs talk to each other and prepare a common course of action.
If the Social Democrats confuse within a few weeks, they will sit at the negotiating table with perfectly played partners, who will put a ready-made list of demands on the table, including ministerial posts.
ČSSD envoys will then either swallow sour coalition grapes or nod to a fickle minority government.
Bohuslav Sobotka, after four days of bearing a wave of sympathy, came across for the first time.
Yesterday, he failed to control the political committee and, most importantly, force convicted liars to resign.
The confirmed negotiator for the party is only him, the other members of the team will be allowed only after a key meeting of the Central Executive Committee (ÚVV).
I've apologised to the members of the committee, and now to the citizens.
You don't have to lie, in your personal life or in public.
"It was a mistake," Michal Hašek said after a meeting of the Social Democrat leadership.
He added that he did not cling to the position of First Vice-President.
"I'll communicate my decision on November 10 at ÚVV," Hašek said.
The vice-chairman of the ČSSD, Zdeněk Škromach, and the head of the parliamentary club Jeroným Tejc spoke similarly.
However, he pointed out that he was elected just two days ago.
Both MPs then declared that they would do everything they could to ensure that the fifty-member ČSSD club sounded one voice.
Michal Hašek gave the absurd theatre a sentence: "I have been a member of the ČSSD for fifteen years, I have always been loyal to its brand and I have respected the decisions of the partisan bodies."
Another lie live.
Or perhaps, when calling for Sobotka’s resignation and his removal from the negotiating team, he forgot that the August ÚVV with 57 percent decided that Bohuslav Sobotka was the party’s nominee for prime minister?
However, by convening the ÚVV until next Sunday, the head of the ČSSD produced a powerful army of enemies.
His opponents have ten days to flood the e-mails of 192 members of this body and the media with a bunch of gossip and rumors, eventually recalling the real mistakes of Sobotka's political career.
There will be a powerful wash, there will be signs of dark alliances between coal baroon Zdeněk Bakala and Sobotka's wing.
A friendly relationship between Bohuslav Sobotka and the influential lawyer Radek Pokorný will be discussed.
Disputes in the South Moravian or Moravian-Silesian CSSD organization will erupt.
The words of the mayor of Ostrava and Sobotka ́s ally Petr Kajnar ́ ́ ́ ́ ́ ́ ́ ́ ́ ́ ́ ́ ́ ́ ́ ́ ́ ́ ́s
If ÚVV had met already this weekend, liars would probably have been forced to sacrifice their functions ritually.
Traitors sprinkle their heads with ashes and remain.
And at the moment, the victoring head of the ČSSD will end up with the nickname Bohuslav Pyrrha.
Martin the Doctor on Problems with OH in Sochi: Communication with Russians is challenging
Corners of his mouth suggest a slight smile and then just exhale deeply: "It's challenging."
Cooperation with the organizing team of the Olympics in Sochi 2014 is a little more complicated, according to Martin Doctor.
We can forget about what happened at last year's Olympics.
"Nothing works the same way," claims the chief of the Czech Olympic mission
Specifically, what's going on?
Many things work more slowly and not so accurately.
It's so confusing.
From the point of view of invoices, they are relatively fast, in terms of supplying information about crucial things not so much.
For example, for three months, we've been leveraging to tell us how individual villages will be officially marked, so that we can do a number of things accordingly.
We're still waiting, and they're still saying they're communicating that thing.
In a number of things, they're still communicating.
Is there at least an Olympic village yet?
But it's no longer a disastrous state.
A month ago, we were walking in empty rooms.
Luckily, the houses stand.
What does it mean they're empty?
Do you need ready-made floors?
Only basic concrete, such was the state a month ago.
But they still have time.
Even though there's a danger that when we get there on January 26 as a van, there's still going to feel the glue.
And if they didn't make it, what?
You're thinking President Putin is chasing an army there?
That's exactly how it's gonna happen.
Just so it doesn't happen that the Olympic village = Potemkin's village.
Rather, there's a risk that they won't be able, for example, to adjust paths, they won't finish the details.
They might have roads in such a state that we'll have to bring our boots from the London Games with us.
But I believe they'll put it together somehow.
How many Czech athletes could go to Sochi?
There are now roughly 78, although depending on the nomination criteria, that number changes.
For example, figure skaters are likely to ride in the trio they're riding.
No one has to doubt the speed skater Martina Sáblíková either, but otherwise it is in development.
Are you still on your way to Sochi?
No, if nothing happens, I shouldn't fly there until January 26.
We know what houses will look like in the Olympic Village.
We've even measured some things ourselves by subway so that we wouldn't just believe sketches.
We are also preparing a number of other things, including transportation.
And what's the mission chief doing a hundred days before the Olympic Games?
I am preparing for the annual evaluation of individual Olympic sports plus I am doing a common agenda in the sense of communication with the organizing committee in Sochi.
Putin vows environmental Olympiad, plan to be hacked by giant secret landfills
Fans of winter sports are already cutting the meter until the start of the Olympic Games in Sochi, others are looking at their preparation with increasing fears.
The Russian Olympics, although not starting until February, are facing another scandal.
Apparently, the Russians are not succeeding in fulfilling the promise that the preparations and holding of the Games will not in the slightest disrupt nature in the Krasnodar region at the foot of the Caucasus, where thousands of athletes and tens of thousands of visitors will head in four months.
Indeed, after the Vancouver Olympics, the Russians had the bar built very high, the Canadian Games have made their mark in the history of the Olympics as the most environmentally friendly.
It seems, however, that the commitment of Russian President Vladimir Putin to organize games with "zero waste," has long since taken over.
Last week, AP reporters discovered a giant landfire of building material in the village of Akhstyr near Sochi.
Thus, a landfill full of building material, tires, canisters and insulating foams was found by ecologists in August, which was immediately reported.
Russian authorities have disappeared.
But only for a moment.
Last week, reporters got a look at a landfill the size of three football fields.
It's not the only one, there have been severals in the area of such sites, British newspaper The Times
Russian railways, a state monopoly that builds a roughly sixty-kilometre journey between Sochi airport and a sports resort, are working on the site.
The business, which has already swallowed 280 billion rubles, was led by one of Putin's close collaborators, Vladimir Yakunin.
In August, railways defended that the landfire was set up by one of the subcontractors.
The locals are already desperate.
According to Achshtyr residents, trucks full of waste material have been driving around the village for three months day and night.
According to experts, waste threatens to contaminate the local Mzymta river, other streams and eventually the Black Sea.
"Water from here contaminates Soča water springs for the next ten to fifteen years," Vladimir Kimayev of the environmental non-profit EWNC told the British newspaper.
Illegal landfills are not the only problem of the Russian organizing committee and the government.
The most expensive Olympics in history, according to the opposition, are one big tunnel.
By comparison, the Sochi Games will cost $51bn, while the 2008 Beijing Summer Olympics came to £43bn.
Last year's games in London cost only 19 billion and the aforementioned Vancouver nine billion.
In addition to corruption scandals, project delays and concerns about whether there will be enough snow in the end, there have also been reports that human rights are not respected when working on the exhibition of sports venues and infrastructure.
Last month, one of the workers sewed his mouth in protest that he had not been paid for for two months.
Many are also worried about how Russian organizers are dealing with a new law that prohibits the promotion of homosexuality.
Some visitors are preparing to support Russian gaxies at the Olympics.
Young couple matured to live with their parents, the new house offers privacy for both generations
Japan's Tai and Associates has been given as an assignment to design a house on a plot that lies on the corner of two streets, with the land itself sloping somewhat.
The requirement was that two generations would live in the house, which would like to live together, but at the same time each should have its own clearly separated space.
The younger couple also needed a small office.
The house stands on the corner of two streets and on a slightly sloping property.
Therefore, the architects decided to place each household on one of the floors of the house.
"When creating the design, we respected the family bonds, history and memories associated with a given place."
"Three different functions of the house are interconnected through several paths through the exterior of the building, so everyone is aware of the presence of others, but they have their own privacy," the designers explain.
The household of parents is on the level of a garden with an old pine tree, to which family traditions and memories are related.
The individual parts of the house are easily recognizable from the outside, because they differ in the different final modification of the facades.
While the main segment of the building is made of concrete, the surface of which has been sculpted using panels from planks, the part where the office is is plastered white, and the remaining elements remained in a clean concrete design without further modifications.
Many walls of the house are completely glazed, thanks to which the interior of the house is very intertwined with the exterior.
The household of parents, more precisely the kitchen, dining room and living room, surrounds a small garden with a tree, which is of great importance to the family.
The interconnection of the interior with the exterior is underlined by the side wall, which begins in the garden and proceeds to the living room.
Kitchen space in the home of a young couple.
The young couple enters the house through the main entrance of the hall and from there rises up the stairs to their premises.
The stairs are the main dominant feature of the interior of the house.
Their design allows a lot of natural light to fall through them cascadingly.
It was in this way that the architects tried to distinguish the house from others and imprint something uniquious.
The house of the young couple is treated as one open space with a kitchen area, a dining room and a living room together.
From the terrace it is separated only by a glazed wall with sliding doors.
A living area with a dining and kitchen area in the home of a young couple.
The second floor is composed as one large open space, which freely passes to the terrace, from where there is a nice view of the Tokyo bay.
The office itself on the top floor is conceived in a pure combination of white and black.
Staircase is the dominant element of the interior of the house.
The young couple enters the house through the main entrance, finds themselves in the lobby, and from there they climb up the staircase.
Defend academic freedoms, this applies to Zeman and the ODS.
In recent months, I have repeatedly called on students, educators and the management of secondary schools and universities in several counties to defend academic freedoms from the election campaign carried out by President Miloš Zeman at schools.
I've encountered more or less success.
The Rector of Masaryk University in Brno, who rejected the lecture of the honorary chairman of SPOZ - Zemanovci in the pre-election period, even received retaliation in the form of non-invitations to celebrate the national holiday of the Czech Republic.
Here is a list of representatives of schools who were no longer so brave and the visit of Miloš Zeman was made possible: Brno University of Technology, Gymnázium Hladnov in Ostrava, University of Business in Ostrava, Business Academy in Kolín, J.E. Purkyně University in Ústí nad Labem.
Now in Pardubice we are faced with the continuation of the enforcement of politics at universities.
The newly elected MP for the ODS, dean of the Faculty of Electrical Engineering and Informatics of the University of Pardubice, Simeon Karamazov, refuses to resign from his academic office and, moreover, it seems normal to him.
Professor, it's really not the same as if, for example, you stayed as mayor of a smaller city.
Do you want to join the governors of the ČSSD, who forge at the same time the position of MP and governor and, like them, to degrade both professions?
This tells people that you can handle both of these demanding functions on the left rear.
I know, we have an example of Michal Hašek, who manages even several dozen functions at once.
But that’s probably not exactly an example of the person you should follow.
We will also find the opposite and positive example of MUDr. Štěpánka Fraňková, who preferred to leave the parliamentary post so that she could fully devote herself to the management of the city of Pardubice.
However, the main reason for my recommendation to leave the dean’s office is that by concurring functions, you violate the apoliticality of academia of the University of Pardubice.
The management of the Pardubice ODS sees your simultaneous influence in both positions as an advantage for the University.
However, you will be able to help "your" school even without remaining a dean.
I think your students will have a similar opinion as I and Rector Miroslav Ludwig.
Therefore, consider your resignation and keep your face.
Otherwise, it may happen that the electorates count for you in the next election.
And it doesn't have to last long.
There's cabbage bubbling in Kunovice, they don't add chemistry
More than a thousand tons of sauerkrauts want to be produced in the coming months in Slovácká Fruta Kunovice, which belongs to the Hamé food group.
The season started here in October and will last about half a year.
Since 1925 he has been producing a healthy delicacy here in the traditional way, i.e. by pedaling directly in big tanks.
In modern industrial times, this is a rarity.
Our worker walks almost a ten-kilometre route per shift.
Fifteen to thirty tons of cabbage can fit into each of the ten tanks, the fulfillment of one takes three shifts," said David Kladroba, director of the Slovácká Fruta plant.
Cabbage heads are transported to the company’s premises directly from their fields by farmers mainly from Ostrožsko and Břeclav.
Fruta employees then clean the vegetables, remove the broomsticks and throw in the gag.
From here, already chopped cabbage falls into swimming pools with a depth of six-meter, in which hookers dressed in white clothing and boots work tirelessly.
This displaces unwanted air.
The last trampled layer of cabbage is covered with a tarp and loaded with water.
"Cabbage is then aged six to eight weeks under spontaneous lactic fermentation without any addition of substances supporting fermentation," emphasized director Kladroba.
The natural process corresponds to production in domestic conditions, the individual layers of the workers are filled with salt and cumin.
Cabbage after fermentation travels to the filling line.
It is packaged in sweet-sour brine in bags or jars and undergoes sterilization.
Hamé supplies sauerkraut mainly to the domestic and Slovak markets.
It also registers interest in some other countries, such as the UK, Ireland and Canada.
Snowden is willing to "cooperate" with Germany in connection with U.S. surveillance
Edward Snowden, a snitcher from U.S. intelligence agencies, announced that he was willing to go to Berlin to give evidence to the German parliament if the U.S. National Security Agency and its director, Keith Alexander, were unable to provide answers to questions about their activities.
German MP Hans-Christian Sträbele meted with Mr Snowden in Russia on Thursday, where he was granted asylum, and negotiated with him about the possibility of testifying in Germany.
"Although the outcome of my efforts has been demonstrably positive, my government continues to treat the opposition as defectors and tries to criminalize political speeches with serious lawsuits that do not give room for defense."
Speaking the truth, however, is not a crime.
In that letter, Mr Snowden said he believed support for the international community could persuade the U.S. government to withdraw criminal charges against him.
Allegations filed by the U.S. Department of Justice include espionage and theft of state property.
Hans-Peter Friedrich, Germany's interior minister, told Zeit Online magazine: "If Mr Snowden is ready to speak with German officials, we will find a way to make it possible."
Tensions in the relationship between the U.S. and Germany began to rise when claims emerged that the NSA was eavesdropping on Chancellor Angela Merkel's phone.
Thomas Oppermann, a member of parliament who heads a parliamentary panel overseeing news coverage, said that if there was any opportunity to interview Mr Snowden as a witness "without putting himself in danger and that relations with the U.S. would be completely devastated," it should be done.
Mr Sträbele, a member of parliament on behalf of Germany's Green Party, posted on his Twitter post a photo of himself with Mr Snowden.
On his trip to Russia he was accompanied by two German journalists.
Mr Sträbele said that according to a former NSA lawyer, Mr. Snowden would not be able to return to Russia if he left once.
If Mr. Snowden testifies in Germany, he would need assurances that he would be "safe" there, a member of parliament said.
In his letter, Mr Snowden said he was facing a harsh and persistent campaign linked to persecution that forced him to leave his home.
However, he said that the world's response to his "act of political expression" gave him courage.
Citizens throughout and senior officials – including those in the United States – evaluated the disclosure of an unjustified system of ubiquitous surveillance for service to the public.
The letter includes an offer to cooperate with the German authorities, "when the difficulties of this humanitarian situation will be resolved."
We're sorry to kill, but we're innocent
Nuon Chea was number two in the Khmer Rouge hierarchy and Khieu Samphan is the former head of state.
During the reign of the Khmer Rouges, which relied on a mixture of Maoism and nationalism between 1975 and 1979, more than three million Cambodians lost their lives as a result of exhaustion, hunger, disease, torture and executions.
He was sentenced to the commander of the infamous S-21 Prison by the International Criminal Tribunal for Cambodia in 2010.
Nuon Chea and Khieu Samphan are allegations of genocide and crimes against humanity including torture, enslavement and homicide.
Nuon Chea and Khieu Samphan defended themselves by saying they believed they were acting in the nation's best interests and had no idea what the true extent of the killing was.
"It's easy to say that I should have known everything, understood everything and therefore intervene and correct the situation," Khieu Samphan told the court on Thursday.
Do you really think I wanted this to happen to my people?
The reality is that I had no authority," he added.
Nuon Chea said he had never ordered Khmer Rogue cadres to "mishandle people or kill them, to deny them food or commit genocide."
However, he acknowledged "moral responsibility" for the dead.
"I would like to sincerely apologise to the public, to the victims, to their families, to all the Cambodian people," he said.
Fifty-five-year-old Bin Sivla, who lost eleven relatives under the Khmer Rouge regime, told AFP that she could not accept Nuon Che's apology."
"He lied to us."
"He just wants to be acquitted."
"She doesn't care about others," she said.
"If he hadn't handed out orders, his subordinates wouldn't have killed," she added, noting that Khmer Rouge leaders were "worse than monsters."
Khmer Rouge Rouge Minister Ieng Sara was also scheduled to appear on trial, but he died in March this year.
His wife Ieng Thirith, the then minister of social affairs at the time, in September 2012 the court found an incompetent trial for dementia.
Khmer Rouge leader Pol Pot died in 1998.
Frontier Airlines is about to charge hand luggage
Frontier Airlines plans to charge an amount of up to USD 100 on its flights for storing hand luggage on board.
Frontier Airlines plans to introduce a fee of up to USD 100 for hand luggage and USD 2 for coffee or soda, although they said in their announcement on Wednesday that passengers would be able to keep the entire can.
The new carry-on baggage fee relates to luggage in the upper storage compartment, so the small luggage under the seat will continue to be free.
Frontier says it will charge USD 25 if the fee will be paid in advance, and 100 USD, if passengers with payment will wait until arriving at the gate.
Frontier spokeswoman Kate O'Malley said the hundred-dollar fee was intended to encourage passengers to take care of the payment in advance.
"We don't want to levy this fee," she said.
Airlines began charging for the first and second handed baggage in 2008.
Passengers tried to avoid these fees and stuffed hand luggage hidden in storage space as much as they could, so there was often no space in that space.
Fees are one of the ways to teach passengers to take fewer things on board.
O'Malley said the point of this new fee really isn't to raise more money.
The point is that Frontier’s most loyal customers have made it quite clear that finding a place in the upper storage space is becoming increasingly difficult.
Passengers who buy tickets on the company's website will not pay.
This means that one passenger queued at Frontier’s gate can bring luggage for which they don’t pay anything, while the other queue queue for the same luggage pays USD 100.
O'Malley said websites and check-in procedures will be adjusted to ensure passengers learn about the fee before they arrive at the gate.
Frontier's new carry-on baggage fee will not yet be in force until the summer, but the date has not yet been set.
Passengers often complain about baggage fees and other fees, but airlines love them.
They argue that the handling of luggage costs money, and passengers requesting the service should pay for it.
Many on Wall Street perceive an additional baggage fee as a sign that airlines are trying to withdraw enough money to cover the cost of air traffic after years of losses.
However, most of them did not agree on carry-on baggage charges.
Dispir Airlines was the first to charge hand luggage three years ago, and another low-cost airline, Allegiant Air, later followed its example.
The only company that also has such fees is Hungary's Wizz Air, says air travel adviser Jay Sorensen, which closely monitors additional fees.
In his December 2011 report, he estimated that Spirit's carry-on baggage fee would bring US$50 million annually.
Sorensen, a former Midwest Airlines chief executive, recently flew with Spirit and marveled at what he had seen at the gate when passengers met Spirit's unusual carry-on baggage fee.
“Getting on the plane was the smoothest I’ve ever seen in my aviation career,” he said.
I expected to see gnashing of teeth and quarrels at the gate.
The plane was full, he said, "and the boarding took place in a blink."
Frontier will also apply fees for coffee, tea, soda and juice.
Frontier said passengers who get lemonade or juice could keep the entire can and the coffee would be replenished for free.
The water will continue to be served free of charge.
U.S
Frontier's last move to charge for hand luggage if a passenger doesn't buy a flight directly from that airline is its latest effort to direct customers directly to their website.
Airlines pay online travel sellers, such as Orbitz, from USD 10 to USD 25 for each ticket sold.
This has prompted all airlines to lead passengers to purchase directly from them, instead of doing so through online travel agencies.
However, Frontier has gone the furthest in this area.
In September, it began giving customers who bought through an internet travel agency, just half the miles within the loyalty program.
On Wednesday, she cropped a reward for flying miles to 25 percent of the miles from that trip.
So traveling with Frontier 1000 miles long purchased through an internet travel agency means a reward of 250 miles.
It also allows customers to choose their seat in advance only if they make the purchase directly through Frontier’s website.
Frontier has a loyal customer base in its domestic city of Denver, but its business is starting to shrink and losing money.
According to the financial results given on Wednesday by corporate partner Republic Airways Holdings, sales fell by 9 percent and transport capacity shrunk by almost 13 percent in the first quarter
By selling this airline, Republic is also trying to solve Frontier’s financial problems.
The crash of an NSA website does not accuse hackers, but an “internal error”
The mysterious National Security Agency said last Friday that its website fell for several hours last week because of an internal error, not because of an attack by hackers, as some claimed on the Internet.
"The NAS.gov site wasn't accessible for several hours this evening because of an internal error that occurred during a regular update," the spy agency said in an e-mail statement.
The problem will be eliminated during this evening.
The claim that the outage was caused by a DDoS (distributed denial of service) is not true.
Earlier this evening, online-server scanners reported that the NSA's website didn't work for at least six hours, and sites remain unavailable to some users.
Earlier, an NSA spokesperson told ABC News that the agency's internal sensitive networks were not "at all" threatened.
A spokesman said no classified information was compromised.
At least one group of hacking activists on the Internet claimed it was responsible for decommissioning the NSA's sites via a DDoS attack.
DDoS attacks are designed to flood target websites with information until servers overload and the page crashes.
This cyber tactic is relatively simple, and the purpose of the attacks is not to penetrate the internal network of the target system.
In recent months, the former super-secrecy NSA agency, which has once been nicknamed No Such an Agency, has become the focus of public attention in recent months after a string of revelations of foreign and domestic surveillance programs, and a biting critique of the NSA’s alienation of the agency’s secret files and their disclosure by a disenchanted former employee, Edward Snowden.
This growing controversy surrounding the agency was prompted by earlier speculation that today's accident was the result of a targeted cyber operation.
Bombardier's profit plummeted as a result of decline in deliveries and orders
Canada's aircraft and train maker Bombardier reported a 15 percent drop in net profit on Thursday, which is caused by a smaller number of orders and deliveries of aircraft in the third quarter and contractual problems in the train unit.
Bombardier, headquartered in Montreal, has also not released any aerial test data for its new CSeries aircraft, nor has it offered up-to-date information on whether this aircraft will comply with an ambitious plan to start commercial operations from next September.
After the opening test flight about a month and a half ago, the plane flew only three times, raising the question of whether the test phase was progressing well.
The results did not meet estimates and shares on the Toronto stock exchange declined 8 percent.
State bank analyst Cameron Doerksen downgraded his rating from "higher profitability" to "sectoral performance" on Thursday, with expectations that the exchange would experience limited growth within the next quarter or two.
"While we were mostly expecting smaller aircraft deliveries, the profit margins in the field of transport significantly disappointed us," Doerksen said in a report to clients.
As the test program continues, we believe Bombardier will receive new CSeries aircraft orders.
However, if no new orders are announced in the coming months, we anticipate that market skepticism will increase relative to this programme.
Bombardier hopes that CSeries aircraft will fight their way into the lower region of the market, now dominated by Boeing and Airbus.
The first test aircraft was unveiled in March, and after several months of delays, the first flight took place in September.
However, the number of CSeries aircraft binding orders reaches a modest 177, as potential buyers are waiting for the results of the test flight to verify the company's claims regarding low consumption and savings on the airline's cargo.
There are currently a total of 403 orders and commitments from 15 customers and operators.
CEO Pierre Beaudoin had expected 300 binding orders to be achieved by Bombardier when the first aircraft was put into commercial use.
Executives also on Thursday assured analysts and media that the program was proceeding as planned.
"The testing plane didn't stay on the ground longer than expected," Beaudoin said in a conference call, noting that examinations on the ground and software updates were scheduled during the plane's shutdown.
Each manufacturer plans them differently.
We decided to take the first flight and then focus on updates and that's what we did.
This will happen throughout the flight schedule.
According to Bombardier, the second of the five test planes is expected to take off in the coming weeks and the others not long after.
Analysts are nevertheless skeptical that the first customer could start operating a CSeries aircraft 12 months after its first flight.
Bombardier claims to have evaluated its plan for commissioning the aircraft and will provide updated information in the coming months.
“This slow pace of flight tests – although consistent with Bombardier’s internal plan – is likely to strengthen our view that commissioning will be postponed to the first quarter of 2015,” Doerksen said.
From US$172 million, or US$9 cents per share in the previous year, Bombardier’s net profit fell to US$147 million, or 8 cents per share, in the third quarter ending on September 30.
Modified returns per share remained unchanged at 9 cents.
Turnover marginally decreased from US$4.2 billion to US$4.1 billion.
According to Thomson Reuters' IBES, analysts had expected a yield of 10 cents per share and a turnover of ££4.56 billion.
The fourth-largest aircraft manufacturer said it had delivered only 45 planes during the quarter compared to 57 aircraft a year before.
Net orders fell from 83 planes to 26.
Unfulfilled contracts in the aviation division amounted to 32.9 billion as of September 30, which does not change as of December 31.
"The results in aviation were in line with our outlook, but the low receipt of orders and the overall state of the market disappointed us," Beaudoin said.
Yield in the aerospace industry fell 13 percent to US$2 billion.
Bombardier, the world's largest trainer, said the revenue in that division rose nearly 11 percent to US$2.1 billion.
Unfulfilled transport contracts accounted for 32.6 billion as of 30 September, which is marginal growth compared to 31 December.
Profitable margins in the transport division were affected by problems with the implementation of several large contracts.
Executives said new prospects would be granted in the fourth quarter.
Bombardier's shares, which also announced the arrival of Google's CFO Patrick Pichette, fell 8.5 per cent during trading on Thursday afternoon to US$4.83.
Brazil's Embraer SA, Brazil's third-largest manufacturer of commercial aircraft and Bombardier's closest competitor, reported a 10 percent slump in quarterly gains on Thursday.
Cocaine-addicted lawyer who warned mobster against police investigation is in prison
Basharat Ditta (42) was supposed to give information to the underworld king Neil Scarbrough.
The lawyer was concerned that his drug addiction would come out.
The Liverpool Crown Court sentenced him to three years in prison.
A top-of-the-line defense attorney who briefed a drug mobster about a major police investigation because he feared his drug addiction would come out, was sentenced to three years in prison.
Basharat Ditta (42) was supposed to hand over sensitive information about the investigation of his activities relating to drug smuggling after he became suspected of using cocaine.
The lawyer, whose nickname was "Bash" (party) and which was hailed by criminals as the best attorney, was arrested at his home in 2011 after police surveillance of Scarborow, whom he had represented in previous narcotics proceedings.
Police officers saw Sarborough (32) leaving three bags of cocaine at the lawyer's house in Blackburn, Lancashire, who had just been having dinner with his colleagues at the Legal Society.
Based on exams that found traces of cocaine in his hair, on his wallet and his credit cards, the investigation showed that Ditta was a regular drug user of Class A.
During a period of eight months, from January to August 2011, he sought to obtain information about the arrest of two men in favor of Scarborough and his companion.
All four suspects were tracked at the time as part of a major investigation into heroin and cocaine trafficking in Lancashire, Cumbria, Merseyside, Berkshire and West Yorkshire.
After police withdrew heroin and cocaine worth about GBP £1.5 million along with GBP 200 000 in cash during a series of raids, another 32 people ended up in prison along with them.
Ditta (42) passed information to criminals for fear that his addiction to cocaine would come.
Ditta, who works at Blackburn-based Forbes Solicitors, had a disgraceful moment before the Liverpool Crown Court when, after a three-week trial, he was found guilty of obstructing the administration of justice on two occasions.
During an earlier hearing, he pleaded guilty to possession of cocaine.
The lawyer's fall came after Scarborough was found to have been in regular telephone contact with Ditta in February 2011.
Two detectives tracked the suspect, recorded his arrival at Ditt's home, and spotted him placing 60 percent purity drugs under the lawyer's basket in a black golf glove.
Subsequently, Scarborough with Ditta, who was at dinner at Blackburn Rovers football stadium in Ewood Park, was in regular telephone conversation.
The lawyer returned home, where he found the drugs, and there were nine conversations between the two.
It was heard in court that traces of Class A drug had been found in his hair, wallet and on his credit cards and that Ditta was a "regular user" of cocaine.
Ditta was later arrested, but denied using cocaine, and said he spoke to the suspected dealer because he was his client, arguing that their conversation fell into "confidential communication."
During his arrest, Ditta took his wallet and tried to take out several credit cards from it, but they were all withheld and his hair samples were taken.
During the police interrogation, he said he operated the office both at his home address and the workplace, and that clients also visited him at home because of work matters.
However, the court heard that after key arrests he contacted major players in the drug supply chain, some of whom he had previously represented, to tell them what detectives knew about them.
Prosecutor Anne Whyte said: "Who else is more aware that they are not allowed to violate the law than a legal representative in a criminal field."
Mr. Ditta is accused of misusing his position as a lawyer and getting into too close contact with a particular client.
The relationship we're talking about here doesn't just concern a drug dealer, but a drug dealer who supplies his own lawyer with drugs.
Some of his conversations were undoubtedly legitimate because he was his lawyer.
But it has gone far beyond the normal lawyer-client relationship.
He was obstructing police investigations as much as he could to allow them to continue their criminal activity.
Mr. Ditta did not bring a good reputation to his profession, on the contrary he disgraced it.
He bonded too much with some of his clients, especially Scarborough, and allowed his independence to be undermined.
Ditta declined, declaring: "If I were a corrupt lawyer that I am not, and I would like to give information to Mr. Scarborough, I wouldn't wait 15 hours, but I would do it immediately."
After the hearing, however, police inspector Lee Halstead of Lancashire said: "Mr. Ditta himself became a criminal from a lawyer in the area when he began obtaining drugs from organized criminals."
His addiction to cocaine made him hopelessly weakened and vulnerable to the intentions of leading representatives of organized crime groups, which gave him the task of gathering valuable information about the police investigation.
Legislators must adhere to the highest principles of integrity and inspire confidence in the public and feel that they can rely on them.
Mr Ditta betrayed this trust and tried to hide behind the cloak of his profession.
The Serious and Organised Offences in Lancashire has conducted an investigation against Mr Ditt, on the basis of which he has also been convicted for three cases of cocaine possession and now for obstructing the administration of justice, demonstrating our commitment to bring criminals to justice.
This case should serve for all criminals as a warning that no one is out of the hand of the law.
We'll find you and put you before our courts.
Scarborough himself was sentenced to 14 years in prison after being convicted of collaborating to supply heroin, cocaine and cannabis.
Another thirty-five persons involved in trafficking were jailed for a total of 153 years for drug offences.
On his website, Ditta held an online interview about himself, in which he said that his dream job would be to legally represent clients from the death block in America, as a guest at dinner he would like Mohammed Ali and as a motivation for the job cited inequalities.
He negotiated the government with both Klaus and Špidla.
Now Jan Kasal's people have sent against Babiš.
The People's Party won fourteen deputies in the elections, i.e. only one-third the number of Social Democrats or YES representatives
However, they enter into government negotiations with at least one advantage.
In the team, which has already beginned to guess the outlines of a possible coalition, Jan Kasal - a man who tuned the conditions of government participation in 1992, 1996, 2002 and 2006.
Neither the Social Democrats nor Andrej Babiš have such a matador on their side.
It will probably commemorate the first negotiations in 1992, when the ODS was also a completely new party.
"The experiences were minimal back then," Kasal estimates of how this year's talks will unfold.
Kenyan press outraged by controversial media law
“It’s scary news, and it’s appropriate to ask what prevents parliament from simply sweeping the independence of the judiciary tomorrow,” the newspaper said.
"This law is strict and very repressive, and we reject it," said Cyrus Kamau, chief executive of the Capital Group, including CapitalFM, one of the most respected independent radio stations and news sites.
He said the new media tribunal "will always be prejudiced because it is an extended hand of the government" and that restrictions on content and advertising will damage Kenya's position in the global economy.
"I hope the president will listen to us, and we will ask him to reject this bill and return it to MPs," he said.
According to Star, the bill effectively passes "complete control of the media" to the government, and the Standard newspaper reports that democracy and freedom of expression in Kenya have "taken a hard blow," criticizing the bill as "strict."
Approval of the bill comes in the midst of a number of measures aimed at boosting national security immediately after September's attack by an Islamic shooter in Westgate's shopping centre.
Kenyan media voiced the anger of the authorities when it aired footage of a security camerace on which soldiers sent to the scene of the attack allegedly looted a luxury goods store.
The police director reacted by summoning two journalists and the head of the media worker for interrogation, but the summons was later withdrawn after pressure from the media.
Under the new bill, media companies can be fined up to 20 million Kenyan shirks and individual journalists with up to one million, along with the risk of "decommissioned from the list" or can be denied official journalistic accreditation.
The tribunal also has the right to seize the offender's property if the fine is not paid.
According to the Daily Nation, "even one fine is enough to dispose of most of the radio stations."
It is also reported that these measures can have a liquidating impact on what is called Kenya's "living blogosphere."
Politicians know that when they silence the media, they can do whatever they want with impunity.
"You never know what might happen," Nation journalist Mutuma Mathiu wrote, describing Kenya's media as a key source of a system of mutual control and balancing in public life.
“If we leave it up to the politicians, they’ll bring the country to bankruptcy and throw us back to hunting and collecting,” he wrote.
Legislators in Kenya have been the target of public criticism in the past.
In May, they voted to repeal cuts ordered by the national pay agency and to restore their fat salaries of around 532,000 untaxed shillings per month, making them among the tallest in the world.
They have to save themselves, the young Czechs know.
Most of them don’t even know what interest
More and more Czechs put their money aside.
However, they do not reach the champions in savings in the region.
Most of the most money is deposited by Austrians, who save an average of 181 euros a month.
A little more than the Czechs also save people in Slovakia.
This is according to the Erste Savings Barometer survey in Central and Eastern European countries.
It has also shown that the mood of savers is improving, and two-thirds of the respondents are planning to put aside more money.
While Czechs are roughly in the middle of the ranking of savers, in Romania and Serbia, people still use savings products very little.
For example, almost every second Serb, according to the survey, saves at home "under the pillow."
In the Czech Republic this year, the number of those who save higher sums per month has risen.
Last month, between a thousand and 2.5 thousand crowns, 26 percent of respondents postponed themselves to all sorts of savings every month, this year it is five percent more.
However, the average monthly deposit in the year-on-year comparison fell slightly to 2078 crowns.
The biggest hit among savings products is retirement insurance.
This is preferred by 54 percent of Czechs, while three years ago it was the most popular way to evaluate savings for only 36 percent of people.
On the contrary, the popularity of building savings is declining, compared to last year, the interest in investment funds has also declined significantly.
Only 16 percent of respondents use them, compared to almost a quarter of people last year.
The survey also showed that although people consider it important to save, most don't know what returns they achieve on individual products.
In particular, young people under the age of 30 do not know how interest-bearing their money is.
On the contrary, the oldest group of respondents has the best awareness of the profitability of their saved money, 73 percent of respondents know this among people over fifty.
In international comparison, Erste Czechs lead in the use of online banking, which is regularly used by 44 percent of people.
They are followed by Slovaks and Austrians with 12 percent distance.
The U.N. celebrates the new goals of combating poverty
The United Nations is beginning to work without delay on a new set of goals to replace the Millennium Development Goals (MDGs), which were introduced 12 years ago to deal with global poverty.
Prior to the U.N
They were included in the final concept of the document, which will be endorsed by world leaders, including Ms Gillard, during the summit.
U.N. Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon said overnight at the summit that now is the time to "rise above national interests."
"I am pleased that the Member States have agreed to launch and take over the process to establish a general Sustainable Development Goal - the SDG," he said.
The SDG will build on the progress that has been made under the Millennium Development Goals and will be an integral part of the post-2015 development programme.
I will make every effort to fulfill the mandate given to me by the Member States and to implement our vision of the Sustainable Development Goals that build on the success of the MDG.
Worries about Mozambique's security are escalating because of a conflict of strong personalities
Under the statue of Samora Machela, the founder's Mozambique president, thousands of people gathered in downtown Maputo to proclamate peaceful slogans at a rare public demonstration.
"We want peace back; we want stability," said Vanessa de Sousa, chief executive of an investment firm.
Out of fear for her country's future, she exchanged corporate clothing for a T-shirt adorned with "we demand safety" in Portuguese and joined the crowd at Independence Square in the capital on Thursday.
For two weeks, there have been almost daily reports of conflicts between government forces and Renamo, some of the worst skirmishes since the peace deal more than 20 years ago.
Renamo was once an innocent-known insurgent movement, originally backed by the White government in Rhodesia, and then by the apartheid government in South Africa as part of efforts to destabilize the country's independent government.
After a peace agreement in 1992, it became an opposition party.
Analysts argue that it is unlikely that an open conflict will break out in the country again, but recent events have startled foreign investors and locals.
A fast-growing economy is at stake, as the discoveries of large-scale coastal oil reserves and coal deposits in the northwest may bring more than $50bn worth of investments from companies such as Brazil's Rio Tinto and Vale, Italy's Eni and U.S. Anadarko over the next few years.
The ruling Frelimo party, the dominant political force since 1975, and Renamo accuse each other of tension.
Renamo party says the government sparked the latest conflicts by launching an attack on their members in Sofala province on September 17, a traditional Renamo Party bastion.
Violent attacks by former rebels subsequently culminated when government forces attacked Renamo's base and tried to kill Afonso Dhlakama, the group's leader, Fernando Mazanga, a spokesman for the Renamo party, told the Financial Times.
The government blames Renamo for triggering the conflicts because it is said to have attacked soldiers.
President Armando Guebuza tried to alleviate fears of instability.
Mr Guebuza told the French press office of AFP on Wednesday that Mr Dhlakama had seened himself as a "wrench" who wanted to use "anything of his strength to try to prove that he could ordain his own decisions to the government."
Both Frelima and Renamo say they want to avoid war.
However, fears rose when Mr Mazanga's words were quoted as saying that Renamo's party was withdrawing from the 1992 peace agreement.
He told the FT that he meant that the Frelimo party no longer respects the deal.
"Our goal is to get back to negotiations, but with seriousness," Mr. Mazanga said.
Previous conversations between these parties have done very little to ease tensions between these parties, stirred up by a number of skirmishes this year.
It's a skirmish between two great personalities (Guebuza and Dhlakama), said lecturer at the Open University and Mozambique expert Joseph Hanlon.
Neither of them is a good negotiator, and neither of them is ready to make the necessary concessions.
Renamo party, which sought electoral changes, has already commented that it would boycott municipal elections planned for November.
Presidential and parliamentary elections are scheduled for next year.
Some commentators have interpreted the use of force as an effort by a dying movement to obtain benefits and financial proceeds from the government.
Renamo's electoral stake has declined since 1992, while the newer party, the Mozambique Democratic Movement (DHM), which was founded by a former Renamo party, is expected to improve its position in elections.
Mr Mazanga says that Mr Guebuza, whose second term ends next year, wants to destroy this country's democracy.
"He doesn't want a democratic system of multiple parties, he doesn't want transparent elections, he doesn't want peace because he doesn't want to leave the presidency," Mazanga said.
It is unclear what options the Renamo party has, but carried out divisive terrorist attacks on police stations and vehicles on a key road between north and south.
Most skirmishes occurred in Sofala province, which is several hundred kilometers away from the capital Maputo, but where the city of Beira is located, a port relied on by mining companies, including Rio Tinto and Vale, when exporting coal.
In June, Rio suspended for about a week the use of the railroad when Renamo party threatened to attack the line.
Mr Mazanga did not want to comment on whether Renamo would repeat his threat.
Renamo wanted to "warn the international community that the situation in Mozambique is not good," Mazanga said.
Instability has heightened frustrations from the government, says Fernando Lima, head of independent media company Mediacoop, and many people are also concerned about corruption, the slow pace of development and the recent flood of kidnappings.
"People think that the responsibility for the future of the country is borne exclusively by the government and the president, and that he is supposed to be the one who finds solutions to the problems," he says.
Protester Omar Sultuane says people just want stability.
“No one cares about Renamo or Frelimo, people just want peace again, they want to have free access to the roads,” he says.
Children should learn myths and legends as “models for a way of life,” says the author.
Stories about Thór can show that “gross power can’t compare to clever cunning,” while Arthurian legends reveal how important it is to have a dream.
A large part of the myths “would be too wild for teaching at school, all too scandalous, and, in some cases, too obscene,” said Crossley-Holland, interceding for a careful selection of works corresponding to age.
"I think it's great that myths and folk creation are already part of education," he said.
I've defended this plan for twenty years.
He added that "openly lecturing" authors and teachers are, with eye-catching stories with a "subliminal" message for children as a "switch."
Crossley-Holland, who translated Beowulf from Anglo-Saxon and who also writes Norwegian Myths (Penguin Book) and the British National stories, says: “You may have good intentions, but you’ll do better to keep them to yourself.”
Probably the biggest difference between an adult author writing for adults and an adult author writing for children is the need for some sense of hope.
Not that everything has to be simplified or end with a happy ending, but there must be a natural sense of good and evil.
And it must be subconscious; exposed through a story, not openly established.
The old truth is that it's better to show than describe.
Brno observatory takes visitors into space in a new digitarium
At first glance, it is clear that the hall of a large planetarium has changed in the last six months.
There is a new stage, visitors can bury themselves in new comfortable seats, and above their heads added a projection area with a diameter of 17 meters in the shape of a dome from the American company Spitz.
In the middle of the hall is its most important part, a new projector from Japan.
In order for the picture to be perfect, other smaller projectors are scattered around the hall.
Another important part of the whole device is hidden behind the walls of the hall.
"It's twenty-one computers that generate an image," said Jiří Dušek, director of the observatory in Brno.
The computers ensure that the image from several projectors does not overlap, but gives a comprehensive impression.
"The performance of computers is thirty to forty times higher than that of classical personal computers," said Tomáš Hladík, a technician at the observatory.
Digitarium can conjure up an extremely realistic and plastic model of the night sky, the human body or the bottom of the ocean.
Audiences find themselves right in the middle of the universe or a person's brain.
Another similar facility is closest in Warsaw or Hamburg.
The management of the observatory plans to screen the purchased shows and their own work in the hall.
There have been two shows on the agenda since Saturday.
The first is called Astronaut and it is designed for adults.
In it, people will learn about cosmonaut training, fly into space, spin on centrifuges, and then on illustrative examples will see what challenging conditions astronauts face in space.
The second show is for children and has the name The Magic Reef - Kaluoka'hina.
It tells the story of a cliff whose colorful inhabitants lived a peaceful life until a nearby volcano erupted.
The entire digitarium cost about fifty million crowns, which was paid mainly by the Ministry of Education.
The city of Brno and the observatory also contributed smaller amounts from their budget.
The projection area cost ten million crowns, the projector twelve million.
"The rest were swallowed by audiovisual technology and computers," Dušek explained.
The installation of the digitarium followed the reconstruction of the entire building, which the observatory underwent two years ago.
According to Director Dušek, between 25 and 30 thousand visitors could arrive by the end of the year.
We've studied 11 THOUSAND ADS!
We know where they want you to work!
In November last year, the analysis captured more than 11,000 job advertisements, of which 4300 were published in daily newspapers and 6800 ads (62 percent) online.
Therefore, the Internet is becoming the most important medium for the publication of job offers.
Even in 2005, when the survey took place for the first time, its share was 26 percent.
In the last two surveys (2007, 2010), it held steady at about 60 percent.
According to the analysis, the advertising of job opportunities significantly reflects the current situation on the labour market.
An example could be a simple comparison of the number of published advertisements in periods of economic prosperity and in times of economic crisis.
For example, in 2007 almost 25 900 advertisements were published in the same reference period, compared to less than half in 2012.
The offer of jobs on the Internet is oriented to a narrower, in particular a more qualified group of professions.
By contrast, the offer of employment in the daily press covers a wider range of professions, including workers, according to Pavlína Št'astová, the author of the analysis.
The degree of representation of selected professional groups varies for both media.
On the Internet, advertisements looking for managers and executives, sales representatives, programmers and financial experts took the top spots.
The position of programmers or heads of small businesses was only on the Internet.
In the daily press, the share of these professional professions is significantly lower, on the contrary, a large representation belongs mainly to service and working-class professions.
Among the most frequently mentioned requirements that employers express through an advertising offer are, of course, education.
Other monitored areas included the knowledge of foreign languages, especially English.
In the daily press, the requirement for knowledge of a foreign language was given in three percent of advertisements, on the Internet in 37 percent.
In only two percent of advertisements, employers demanded an active knowledge of two foreign languages.
Claims for 'computer literacy' appeared in 40 percent of adverts (in the daily press at 11 percent, on the Internet in 57 percent).
Experience in the industry has been demanded in 54% of advertisements.
The requirements for practice are generally associated with qualified professions.
These are mainly production managers and other executives.
For working-class professions it is mainly about mechanics and repairers or manufacturers and processors of food products or repairers of electrical equipment, added the author of the study Jiří Tillner.
Employers also indicate in the middle of their advertisements the requirements for the characteristics and skills of potential applicants.
As the most frequently required skills, communication skills have long appeared in advertising in the first place.
Furthermore, employers demand flexibility, adaptability and dynamism, independence, responsibility, organizational skills and reliability.
Ben Greenman: The 10th Anniversary of the New York Comedy Festival: New Yorker
It could be argued that the city of New York is the place where solo comic performances were born in America: almost a century ago, variety artist Frank Fray, who worked as the chairman of ceremonies at the Palace Theatre on Broadway, began telling jokes to the assembled people using the interview method.
Fay's innovation has expanded over the years, most recently through a New York comedy festival.
Founded and managed by Caroline Hirsch, the founder of solo comedy Carolines, she celebrates her 10th anniversary this year – along with more than sixty performances in small clubs as well as major theatres.
"Most of those titles appeared in Carolines and became successful to the extent that they are too big to be played at the club," Hirsch said.
We created this festival as an opportunity to continue working with them.
This year's event includes performances by Wandy Sykes, Kathy Griffin and Bill Maher, as well as "Stand Up for Heroes", an annual musical comedy benefit performance at Madison Square Garden, featuring Bruce Springsteen, Jon Stewart, Roger Waters and Bill Cosby.
Just as this festival expanded, so did the world of comedy.
Several comedians participating in this year's festival emerged from non-traditional channels, such as shows in smaller radio and television companies, such as Comedy Central, FX and Spike.
Nick Kroll has risen with his sitcom (happily spicy “League” on a fantastic-football theme on FXX) and now has his own comedy show on Comedy Central.
Jenny Slate was a member of both “Saturday night live” and “Parks and recreation”, but she became most famous for her fast-spreading series of “Marcel the shell with shoes on”.
Both Kroll and Slate, as well as other young comedians with clearly recognizable voices (surreally pessimistic Anthony Jeselnik, sarcastic, racially-facing W. Kamau Bell), are the product of the decentralized world of American comedy.
One of the festival's biggest draws will be an interview: David Steinberg will speak with Larry David.
Steinberg began as a solo comedian but became a successful television and film director, as well as an unofficial comedy historian.
From 2005 to 2007, he hosted a show called “Sit down comedy with David Steinberg.”
The meeting takes place at Town Hall in downtown Manhattan.
"This city is definitely in the comedy DNA of Larry's work," Steinberg said.
He told me that when he's here, he sometimes walks down the aislane between two buildings and thinks: man, if I lose all my money, maybe I'll live here.
The price of oil fell further to 96 dollars per barrel
On Friday, the price of oil continued to fall, as fears of high inventories were balanced by a report that showed the strengthening of China's energy-fighting manufacturing sector.
The U.S. oil indicator for December deliveries fell 14 cents to 96.24 dollars per barrel in European e-trading on the New York Stock Exchange.
There was a decline of 39 cents on Thursday and lagged 5.8 per cent for the month of October.
Rich oil inventories have weighed on prices in recent weeks.
The Department of Energy said Wednesday that U.S. stocks have risened by 4.1 million barrels last week.
In five weeks, inventories have risen by more than 25 million barrels.
On Friday, however, out of two reports on Chinese production, came an impetus for greater demand, which showed growth in activity.
This suggests China's economic recovery could continue to strengthen after growth after the previous two weak decades at 7.8 percent.
Brent crude oil, an indicator for international oil also used by U.S. refineries, fell 26 cents to 108.58 dollars per barrel on the International Trade Exchange in London.
Well, no, Mr. Hašek.
We have a unique opportunity to say to politicians that this is not the case.
It is necessary to exert such pressure that Martin Hašek is deprived of all political functions and leaves the parliamentary mandate.
Any other solution will reduce the level of political culture that is already slim.
Martin Hašek is shielded by the promise he has made, but everyone and the politician in particular must be aware of the consequences of the promise and consider very much what can be promised.
Confidence is the only capital that a politician has (Mr Babiš also has a material one, but the question is whether he is a politician).
