Take responsibility and retire from politics.
The court blocked regulations regarding the procedure of stopping and searing persons by NY police departments
A federal appeals court on Thursday blocked court orders demanding changes to the cessation program and the search of people at the New York Police Department and dismissed the judge from the case.
A second U.S. appeals federal court said Judge Shira Scheindlin's decision would be postponed until an appeal decision filed by the city.
In August, the judge ruled that the city had violated the Constitution by implementing its program of stopping and questioning people.
The city challenged its conclusions and its corrective regulations, including the decision to allocate oversight, which would help the police department change its procedures and its training program associated with them.
The appeals court heard arguments on Tuesday for the required postponement.
The Court of Appeal said the judge had to be dismissed from the case because it came into conflict with the rules of conduct for the judge, by saying that, as a judge, she did not act according to the requirement of avoiding the appearance of bias, partly because of a series of interviews with the media and a public statement by which she openly responded to criticism of the court.
The judge ruled that police officials violated the civil rights of tens of thousands of people when they wrongly targeted blacks and Hispanics as part of their stopping and search program.
It established independent supervision to oversee major changes, including reforms in procedures, training and supervision, and ordered the launch of a pilot programme to test body-worn cameras in some areas where the most frequent stopping of people is taking place.
In August, the city of New York agreed to end the method of retaining the names and addresses of people whose cases were dismissed after a police search.
The oral justification for the city's appeal is scheduled sometime after March 14, 2014.
Tactics of stopping and searching people have criticised a number of civil rights defenders.
In some form, there have been stopping and searching people for about many decades, but recorded tours under the administration of independent mayor Michael Bloomberg have dramatically risen to a record number of 684 330 in 2011, in most cases black and Hispanic.
In 2004, four men, who are all among minorities, filed a lawsuit that became the case in a group lawsuit.
Proponents of changes to the cessation and search program conducted by the NY police department say changes will end unfair practices, increase the credibility and effectiveness of the police force, and can influence how other police departments use those procedures.
Opponents argue that the changes will reduce police morale, but not crime, and that this will waste money, but a broader problem will not be solved, when the police force is under pressure after its numbers have decreased by several thousand people over the past decade.
The judge noted that she did not end the practice of halting and searching persons who are constitutional, but reformed the way in which the NY police department's tours introduced.
One-fifth of shopping centres are struggling with low attendances
That's according to data from real estate consultancy Cushman & Wakefield and Jones Lang LaSalle.
The Krakov Shopping Centre is open to the public in Bohnice in Prague, it will be the 19th shopping centre in Prague.
There are now a total of 90 large shopping centres in the Czech Republic.
Unsatisfactory performance affects about 20 percent of medium-sized and medium-sized centers.
The main problems are an inadequately large center towards the locality and bad layouts, i.e. too many floors or a complicated and confusing arrangement of passages, or dead places of centers," said Michal Soták from Cushman & Wakefield.
Also, according to Jones Lang LaSalle's head of retail properties, Sylvie Samadi, about a fifth of shopping centres are struggling with low attendances.
The main reasons include, among other things, poor transport accessibility, a low income category of local residents or market oversaturation in a given location.
Some of the projects opened almost fully occupied, but their results were mainly unsatisfactory at first.
Nowadays, customers are somewhat conservative and don’t like to change their shopping habits.
New shopping centres need to try harder to 'entice' their customers to new brands in the market, entertainment, services and marketing events," Samadi added.
The occupancy of shopping centers is generally stable, according to Soták, it even rises at some centers, because landlords were willing to discount rents in crisis and lease some units.
"However, it is true that some projects that are not fully booked have recently opened and the average occupancy is thus reduced (e.g. Breda & Weinstein in Opava, Šantovka in Olomouc)," Soták added.
Shopping centres opened in the past two years have been promoted very slowly on the market, according to Sylvia Samadi.
However, there is a place on the market for new projects.
The number of places suitable for the construction of a successful project is very limited.
For example, in Prague we see space for the construction of a project that would serve the inhabitants of Prague 6.
There is certainly room on the market for the construction of smaller local projects, so-called 'convinience centres', focused on purchases and services of daily necessities for the population living in the immediate vicinity," Samadi said.
Also, according to Soták, there is a place on the domestic market for other shopping centers.
Both for small "service" centers and for large projects in Prague or medium-sized projects, or extensions in regional cities.
"Cities such as Kladno, České Budějovice, Brno or Pilsen have room for further growth," Soták added.
In Prague, they see space for the upcoming shopping center on Vítězné náměstí, according to him, they also have a clear potential of Na Příkopě streets and Wenceslas Square.
"The expansion of retail parks, which allows traders to cover and expand to increasingly smaller cities at favorable rents and operating costs, there is also space in some locations for smaller local or residential shopping centers, respectively their reconstruction, such as the reconstruction of Prior department stores, which benefits from a good location," added Dita Kubánková from DTZ.
There are currently 90 modern shopping centers in the Czech Republic with a total of more than 2.2 million square meters of rude rentable areas.
A quarter of all these shopping centres are located in Prague.
Among the 20 largest shopping centers, according to the number of tenants, nine are in Prague, according to data from Incoma GfK.
In the number of shopping parks, the Czech Republic is below the EU average, compared to values from Britain and France, the Czech Republic lags by about 50 square meters per 1,000 inhabitants.
Coulson hacked into his phone to check the tip
Former News of the world editor Andy Coulson allegedly used methods of “hacking into the phone, tracking and confrontation” to confirm the fraudulent tip for a love affair involving then-Interior Secretary Charles Clarke.
Attorney General Andrew Edis told Old Bailey that the News of the World newspaper had intercepted fake news in May 2005 that Clarke had been seen with his "attractive special adviser" Hannah Pawlby.
The newspaper commissioned private investigator Glenn Mulcaire to hack into Pawlby's voicemail and penetrate her privacy, but it was rumored in court that Coulson, too, had called her and left her with voicemails.
"The prosecutor suggests that Mr. Coulson, who is now editor of NotW, is not a person standing in front of the house hoping to catch people outside, but a person who likes to tell people stories to see what they have to say," Mr. Edis said in a statement.
