A recording with voicemails taken from her phone on at least three occasions was obtained at Mulcaire's house in August 2006.
Investigators also found records on a private investigator's computer, where Ms Pawlby and her sister were referred to as "Projects."
During Ms Pawlby was under investigation, her grandparents were receiving anonymous phone calls asking for information about Pawlby, Mr Edis said.
In the meantime, former Neville Thurlbeck and former correspondent James Weatherup oversaw Ms Pawlby's movements.
Coulson left her with a voice on June 18, 2005, telling her: "I've been given a story we're going to publish tomorrow that I'd be happy to talk to Charles about."
Mr Edis says Coulson's involvement in the story was, according to the same model as that of other prominent personalities such as former Home Secretary David Blunkett.
It was heard in court on Thursday that Coulson confronted Mr. Blunkett because of a love affair with a married woman, while he himself said with co-defendant Rebecca Brooks, who was married at the time.
Coulson and Brooks have denied collaborating with others in order to hit phones between October 3, 2000 and August 9, 2006.
Mulcaire, Thurlbeck and Weatherup have pleaded guilty to hacking on the phone.
The NSA's revelation increases the social paranoia of surveillance by the state
One peaceful day towards the end of August, a German police helicopter buzzed over a U.S. consulary office in Frankfurt, Germany's capital.
At the behest of the Federal Office for the Protection of the Constitution (BfV), a German domestic intelligence agency, the purpose of the mission was to take photographs of the roof of the U.S. forward patrol, which is located less than 5 km from the European Central Bank and the Bundesbank.
German media say BfV had hoped to be able to identify the presence of eavesdropping antennas, and this action triggered an exchange of views between the U.S. and German foreign minister in Berlin.
James Clapper, the U.S. director of national intelligence, insisted once again in September that the United States did not use its foreign intelligence capabilities “to steal trade secrets of foreign companies for the benefit of U.S. companies and thus strengthen their international competitiveness or increase their profits.”
But since Edward Snowden, who has become an informant from an collaborator, has begun revealing his valuable collection from classified surveillance by the United States, European governments and leading entrepreneurs no longer know if they should trust the director's words.
After reports that the U.S. National Security Agency conducted espionage at a Brazilian oil company and gained access to data available by U.S. cloud providers, including Google and Yahoo, achieved general paranoia about state monitoring of new dimensions.
The last straw came with the revelation that Chancellor Angela Merkel's phone might have been wiretapped for perhaps a decade.
If the most powerful person in Europe can be the target, then even leading entrepreneurs are certainly a potential target.
Snowden has made intensive cooperation between U.S. intelligence agencies and companies more transparent.
I'd say it's possible that this data is being used to each other.
"Germany has to wake up," said Oliver Grün, president of BITMi, which represents small and medium-sized IT companies in Germany.
According to a survey released in July by EY's advisory service, German companies are convinced that when it comes to industrial espionage and data theft, the U.S. now poses as much of a risk as China.
However, there is no evidence that the United States has handed over the trade secrets of foreign companies to its own companies.
Politicians have expressed concerns that the EU lacks certain IT and internet capabilities, and said it should try to reduce its dependence on the US:
Leading entrepreneurs are sceptical about this.
There are voices from the German parliament saying that we should make a German Google.
I'm just able to close my eyes and slowly open them again...
That's not the way, says Hasso Plattner, chairman of the German software company SAP.
If someone wanted a strong European IT industry, they shouldn't have let it die 20 years ago.
Everything in Germany is subsidized, from coal to cars and farmers.
Everything but the IT industry.
The scale and technical sophistication of U.S. spy agencies uncovered by Snowden is shocking for some of the companies who have so far believed that the biggest threat of surveillance is China.
There is a big turnaround in cloud computing, where European officials realized to a wider extent that data stored in the U.S
According to a survey conducted by the cloud security alliance, about 10 percent of non-U.S. members, after revealing U.S. Prism’s data mining program, canceled plans to use a U.S.-based cloud provider.
Jim Snabe, one of SAP’s CEOs, says, “Do we have a new question for customers who haven’t been here a year ago—where my data is stored, and can you guarantee they’ll physically stay in this jurisdiction?”
Many German high-profile officials argue that the latest reports are just confirmation of what they already knew: that powerful states want to alienate their most precious secrets, and this data must be protected at all costs.
That economic espionage is carried out is not a surprise.
It's always happened.
“It’s been a crucial topic for many years now, and it hasn’t changed significantly even during the current discussion,” says Kurt Bock, CEO of agri-cheming company BASF.
The Americans are espionage on us at the commercial and industrial level, just as we do espionage on them, because it is in our national interest to defend our businesses.
In general, business leaders do not want to boast of the countermeasures they have made in order not to give the attacker an advantage.
In the case of large companies, it has long been reminded that taking a free memory USB drive at the fair or leaving an unprotected laptop in a hotel room is minimally unreasonable.
Ulrich Hackenberg, a member of Audi's board of directors, says it's been a common practice for many years to collect mobile phones prior to a board meeting so that they can't be used as listening devices.
The German BfV authority advises executives to consider whether not to use simple prepaid mobile phones on foreign trips due to the risk that smartphones can be embezzled.
Prepaid mobile phones are then thrown away.
However, there is concern that small and medium-sized enterprises will continue to be vulnerable towards hacking and surveillance.
In Germany, many of these companies are leaders of the global market in their specific areas.
“Small and medium-sized enterprises often lack experience, staff and financial resources to effectively protect corporate secrecy against an unauthorised approach,” BfV warns.
The U.S. warns its own companies against economic espionage by other countries.
According to the Washington post, U.S. National Intelligence Estimate reported France in February, alongside Russia and Israel, in the second series of perpetrators who engage in hacking activities for economic correspondents, right behind China.
A member of the council in a German risk-free company concluded that when it comes to economic espionage, "the French are the worst."
Bernard Squarcini, the former head of France's internal intelligence agency DCRI, said in an interview this month: "Services know perfectly well that all countries, even if they are cooperating in the fight against terrorism, are conducting espionage on their allies."
The parents of an underage boy from Georgia who died in a strange accident believe he was murdered
The parents of an underage boy from Georgia, whose body was found inside a wrestled wrestling chird in the gym of his high school, believe their son was murdered, the family's lawyer said on Thursday.
Kendrick Johnson of Valdosta, Georgia, was founded on January 11 in a straightened spirit behind grandstanders in a high school gymnasium where he wented.
Sheriff's Lowndes County investigators concluded that Johnson died accidentally, but the family of a seventeen-year-old rejected it.
