I'll keep an eye on the facts, wherever they lead me.
My goal is to find out the truth.
"I'm of the opinion that there is a sufficient basis for a routine investigation," he said.
Moore told reporters that the original autopsy had hinted that Johnson had died as a result of "positional asphyxiation."
The second autopsy, however, according to Moore, cited a different cause of death.
"There are a few questions that must be answered or confirmed," he said.
Moore added that if, in Johnson's death case, he reveals enough evidence for an order to investigate a crime or violation of civil rights, he will ask the FBI and its implementation.
A representative of the sheriff's office from Lowndes precinct wasn't immediately available on Thursday for possible comment.
A South Georgia judge on Wednesday ordered the authorities to make available all the videos from the surveillance cameras, which investigators inspected.
The juvenile's parents hope that the video footage will contain a clue leading to the revelation of how their son died.
CDC has issued guidelines for schools on allergies in children
On Wednesday, the Centre for Disease Prevention and Control (CDC) issued a set of directives designed to aid in children's food allergies at school.
This is the first set of such directives issued by the U.S. government, due to the fact that the number of school-aged children suffering from food allergies is rising.
One of the 20 children in the United States is allergic to food.
The CDC found that the incidence of food allergies in children increased by 18 percent between 1997 and 2007.
The guidelines include information for schools on how to inform the teaching staff and staff about children's food allergies and how to respond to an allergic reaction if it occurs.
It also advises schools to have adrenaline available – most often using the EpiPen automatic injector – to be able to respond to potentially dangerous hypersensitivity.
State legislators have recently updated rules that allow schools to store adrenaline more easily.
The report also contains a list of typical symptoms appearing in conversations with children who have an allergic reaction.
The child can say, “It’s like someone’s sticking in my tongue,” “I feel like I have hair on my tongue” or “Itches my tongue.”
Parents of intersex children may choose “indeterminate gender”
Germany has become the first European nation to recognize the third gender in children born with an ambiguous genital tract.
Newborn babies will no longer be assigned to either male or feminine.
The new law does not require parents to indicate any sex for such children, and allows them, on their birth certificates, to list gender as “indefinite” or “unspecified.”
The aim of the law is to stop putting pressure on parents to make a hasty decision on their child in order to determine their sex, and to combat discrimination against those who are intersexual.
According to the BBC, after many years, an intersex person said: “I am neither male nor female.”
I'll remain a crevice created by doctors, bruised and scarred.
It is estimated that every year, out of 2000 babies, one is born, which is neither a boy nor a girl.
They are intersexual, part of a group of about 60 facts that fall within the diagnosis of genital development disorders, an overarching term for those who have atypical chromosomes, sex glands (ovaries or testicles) or unusually developed genitals.
Intersex may have been Wallis Simpson.
Determination of gender is not yet fully understood, but most experts in the United States say that when gender cannot be determined, it is better to use the best available information and determine it instead of waiting and following the psychological and physical development of the child until the operation is carried out, if at all.
New York Psychiatrist Dr Jack Drescher, who specializes in sex-determination issues, said Germany's new law "looks like a good thing."
Intersex children represent an ethical dilemma.
"Some persons are in a life-threatening condition that requires surgery, but that's not the case for most children," he said.
Determinating the sex can be done without surgery, and then it can wait as the identity develops.
Knowing how a child develops sexual affiliation is not very accurate.
No one can answer the question of why this is happening.
It's like the mystery of why some people are homosexual.
The report handed over to the European Committee in 2011 described intersex people as different from transsexual or transgender people because their status is not tied to gender, but to their biological structure, which is not exclusively male or female, but is typically composed with both and cannot be clearly defined as neither.
These traits can manifest themselves in secondary sexual characteristics such as muscular volume, hair growth, bust and figure, in primary sexual characteristics, such as propagating organs and genitals, or in the structure of chromosomes and hormones.
This report also provides an overview of the discrimination experienced by intersex and transgender people in the field, as well as levels of harassment, violence and prejudice.
Boys not matching any sex now have a special camp.
Australia and Nepal already allow adults to be labeled male, feminine or "third-sex" in official documents.
Norrie May-Welby, a 52-year-old Australian woman, became the world's first recognized "sexless" person in June when she won a judicial appeal so that she could retain "indefinitely" gender status for the rest of her life.
According to the Ministry of the Interior, in addition to M (male) and F (female), German passports will also have a third designation, X, for intersex persons.
In neighboring France, according to news coverage at France 24, gender issues are still controversial.
In 2011, dozens of French lawmakers from this strongly Catholic country signed a petition to remove "gender theory" from school textbooks.
America’s Catholic online website has also railed against German law, writing: “As the world is dragged towards a new state, where gender is a matter of choice, but sexual activity is not, two other pillars of civilisation are upside down.”
One of Maryland's newborn's mothers also told the Baby zone she'd preferred to see children be assigned gender at birth.
“Parenting alone is stressful even without further restrictions, especially if you don’t know your child’s gender,” she said.
Children need stability and security.
Historically, children with both male and female sex were called hermaphrodites, according to the handsome Greek, who had a dual sex.
And about a decade ago, the medical community thought that gender was something like a blackboard that could be erased and redrawn again.
But now many question the ethical basis of the operation, saying that sexual affiliation is a complex matter, and doctors can sometimes decide incorrectly because they don't know how children will feel about their gender when they grow up.
"In the middle of the 20th century, it was called a psychiatric emergency," Drescher said.
When these babies were born, you didn't call a psychiatrist but a surgeon.
A persistent theory about how to treat children with an ambiguous sex was presented by Dr. John Money at Johns Hopkins University, who took the view that gender was malleable.
He created the new concept of “sexual affiliation” and argued that the social causes and influences of the environment – as parents raised their child – affected childhood genes and hormones and shaped the child, regardless of whether she was identified as male or female.
But in one case from 1966, known as "John/Joan," his theory became contentious.
He advised the parents of the boy, whose penis was damaged due to ill-executed circumcision, to have his child completely neutered, remove his testicles and raise him as a girl.
"The media presented this case as a successful transformation case, but the reality was different," Drescher said.
When the boy was 15 years old, he allowed himself to be transported back to the boy and married a woman.
At the age of 38, however, he committed suicide.
Drescher said some doctors "use this model" to this day.
But in the 1990s, with the arrival of the Internet, survivors of these operations appeared "dissatisfied with the outcome."
It was also the case of Jim Bruce, a 36-year-old writer from Montana who was born with male chromosomes but an ambiguous genitals.
The doctors were unsure of whether he had a large clitoris, or a small penis, and were convinced that as a man, he could never live a “satisfactory life.”
And so shortly after his birth in 1976, Bruce's external organs and testicles were surgically removed, and he was raised as a girl.
At the age of 12, he got female hormones.
“I knew I wasn’t a girl,” he told ABCNews.com.
I've been unhappy, but it's been very difficult to ask.
At 18 he was sent for vaginal surgery.
But because he was depressed and he knew something wasn't right, he asked for medical records.
What he found was scary.
I was sterilized after I was born and no one ever said anything to me.
Bruce was born with a gender disorder (DSD), which prevented the body from producing enough testosterone so that its genital tract could develop properly.
After learning the trust, he turned back into a man, took doses of testosterone, and had his breasts removed,
Surgeries caused his infertility.
Today he represents others in an organization called Interface Project and tries to normalize the perceptions of those who are intersex.
But Anne Tamar-Mattis, director of Advocates for informed choice (proponents of an informed choice) based in California, fears that German law is "callinging for branding and stigmats."
“Many activists are worried that German law will encourage parents to make a quick decision and choose ‘indefinitely’ for the child,” she said.
We're afraid it's going to encourage intervention.
We think it's better to assign male or female gender and then wait.
But we don't know how the law will sound in the end, so all we can do is speculate.
Tamar-Mattis said her organization supports Australian law because it "allows adults to choose to be included in the third gender."
"Adults should be able to make their own decisions about the legally valid gender," she said.
German law is about to allocate it at birth.
This is a battle that a small child should not seduce at this moment.
When they grow up, they can make their own decisions about their own bodies.
But Dr. Arlene Baratz, a Pittsburgh chest radiologist who has a daughter with impaired sexual development and helps hundreds of others in the support group, said the German law will "award powers" to both parents and children.
Baratza's daughter, Katie, was born with male chromosomes, but has a DSD called androgen number syndrome.
Because its androgenic receptors are defective, Katie's feminine traits have evolved.
She has a vagina, but she doesn't have uterus and ovaries.
Now 29-year-old Katie is married and as part of her studies at the University of Pennsylvania she works as an intern at children's psychiatry.
Although infertile, she hopes to become a parent through adoption or surrogacy.
"The law will give parents some space not to rush their decisions," Baratz said.
It gives them time to perform certain tests and think about it and gives them time to write “man” or “woman”.
Everything is fine in this regard – you raise and love your child.
You've got an amazing child and you're rejoicing
You don't have to rush an operation that's irreversible.
"It brings about the opportunity to involve the child in decision-making, taking away the anxiety that binds parents because they don't have the certainty that they're doing the right thing," she said.
In the end, the child decides what gender he identifies more with – and that’s a great thing.
It gives children the power to make their own decisions.
Drunker poured gasoline on the whole house: He threatened to burn it down with his own family!
