Report: Obama's campaign considered swapping Biden for Hillary Clinton
According to the New York Times, President Barack Obama’s closest adviser was secretly considering replacing Vice President Joe Biden for Hillary Clinton.
This revelation is the most significant sensation of the eagerly awaited 2012 campaign book by Mark Halperin and John Heilemann “Double down: Game Change 2012.”
The Times magazine obtained a copy of the coming book and announced Thursday night that the president's top collaborators had held intense group meetings and polls in late 2011 to estimate whether getting rid of Biden would help revive Obama's fading hopes of re-election.
According to a national-political correspondent for the Times magazine, Jonathan Martin, the book offers a thorough recording of the efforts of senior officials inside the campaign and in the White House, namely former White House chief Bill Daley, to measure what effect the former Secretary of State Clinton's move to the post of vice-presidency would have.
Potential change has been a closely guarded secrecy within Chicago's campaign infrastructure and within the Oval Office.
Just a few of the president’s closest advisers – including Daley, former Obama campaign manager Jim Messina, and former White House lead advisers David Axelrod and David Plouffe – knew this change was being considered.
“Double down” claims that Daley was a representative of efforts to replace Biden, despite their close personal relationship, until they finally decided not to make that move, when the data showed that adding Clinton to a nominee would “not substantially increase Obama’s chances.”
In an interview with Martin Daley, he confirmed that the administration was indeed considering replacing Biden Clinton.
“I’ve researched a number of things intensively, and this was one of them,” Daley said.
Don’t forget that the president had terrible prospects at the time, so we thought, “Christ, what are we supposed to do?”
While Daley characterized the research as "proper care," Martin told CNN's Anderson Cooper that the re-election campaign had made considerable investments to determine whether this move would pay off in the election.
“The campaigns don’t spend that kind of money on elections and qualitative research, unless it’s really seriously considered,” Martin told AC360.
However, it is unclear whether Obama knew that his team was considering this exchange.
Martin told CNN he had asked Daley if his then-boss knew about this potential shuffling of cards.
While Daley said he didn't think the president "was aware of the potential change," the former head of the office admitted it was "possible" that Obama knew about it.
Martin added that the book “Double down” does not definitively answer the question of whether a political probe has made it all the way to Obama’s table.
Cooper asked Martin if he seriously thought Obama didn't aware of the research regarding removing Biden from the nominee.
"Perhaps," Martin replied.
Fantastic ice cream that shines in the dark?
A British businessman has produced the first ice cream that shines in the dark – using jellyfish.
Charlie Francis took advantage of the fluorescent properties of this marine animal and developed a luminous treat.
He came up with the idea when he read an expert treatise on jellyfish, and persuaded scientists in China to chemically produce a protein that causes luminousness.
This ice cream reacts with the consumer’s tongue – it increases the pH level in the protein and subsequently causes its radiation.
Chris says the ice cream shines when it reacts with the heat of your mouth, and therefore the more you lick, the brighter it shines.
Charlie, the founder of the ice cream company “Lick me I’m delicious”, says: “It’s an amazing substance, but when it comes to producing, we’re at the beginning, so for 200 GBP you’ll get about 2 g of this substance.”
The protein we use in ice cream reacts with your tongue at neutral pH.
So as your mouth heat the protein, the pH level increases and the ice cream begins to glow.
We've tested it over the last few months and it seems to be a great idea to celebrate Halloween because it's causing this fantastic radiant effect.
It's probably the most expensive ice cream I've ever made, because jellyfish lighting is four times more expensive than gold.
And so every scoop costs me about 140 GBP.
But it tastes great.
Charlie's Bristol-based experimental company is known for its unconventional flavors of beer, cheese, beef and gold leaves.
However, his other product should be even more ambitious.
He says, “I really want to make an invisible ice cream.”
In essence, it's impossible because of the refraction of light caused by the crystals of ice that make up the ice cream, but I'm counting that we'll find some way to do it.
Ice ice cream uses the fluorescent properties of the jellyfish, artificially made by Chinese scientists.
