Firefighting troops arrived to rescue the missing puppy, who was stranded 15 meters above the ground on a dangerous protrusion in a quarry
Cocker Spaniel Ruby escaped after a small car accident.
Three days later he was seen trapped in a quarry by a man who was walking a dog.
Firefighters rappelled along a rock wall to save the dog from certain death.
The puppy was rescued by fire units, who came to rescue him after somehow self-imprisoned himself on a dangerous protrusion in a quarry of 15 meters above the ground.
Ruby's nine-month-old cocker spaniel escaped after a small car accident on Sunday afternoon, and survived himself for three days until he was extricated from a quarry on Wednesday.
Its owners Scott Alderson (25) with his girlfriend Becky Hall (20) were at Flappit quarry in Denholme, West Yorkshire, to meet Ruby again and to thank West Yorkshire fire and emergency services.
After he escaped when he got involved in a small accident, they frantically searched for their missing dog and placed calls on social media.
On Wednesday at about 14.15 he saw a bright-eyed doggier stranded Ruby on a protrusion in a quarry of 15 meters above the ground.
A technical rescue team rushed to the site from Cleckheaton's fire station and rappelled down to free Ruby, using an animal transport bag to pick it up on the rock.
Andy Clayton, the expert commander of the technical rescue team, said: "The dog was in a dangerous situation."
It was right in the middle of the rock wall – 15 meters from the top and from the bottom.
He didn't move his eyebrows during the rescue - he was totally frozen.
But he's fine now.
He got biscuits.
It was a very unusual exit.
Seeing a dog is incredible.
The expert commander of the technical rescue team, Peter Lau, said: "Ruby was very lucky during his escape."
There was a possibility that he would be very seriously injured or even worse.
Ruby was taken to the vet for a check-up, and in addition to exhaustion and dehydration, he was fine.
Miss Hall, from Halifax in West Yorkshire, said: "I was scared at the sight of the rescue operation."
Above all, I couldn't believe how he got there.
It was amazing to hold him in your arms again.
The veterinarian said that if he exhausted and collapsed too much, he'd probably fall.
Firefighters have done a great job.
What they did was very courageous.
We're very grateful to them, they've all just been huge.
Mr Alderson of Keighley added: "We were afraid he might fall, but he stayed there."
Firefighters were great.
We just don't understand how he got there.
