George Kerevan: Divided Europe gives Scots a choice
The next day, another scary story of independence.
This time there is a warning that the condition for the membership of an independent Scotland in the EU would be its entry into Schengen.
This led to stories of passport checks in Berwick and barbed wire along Hadrian's wall.
The truth is that Strathclyde newspapers mentioned the possible economic benefits of free movement with the rest of Europe, but – as expected – this was not reflected in the headlines.
No one has also mentioned that EU Member States spend a lot of time adapting their official laws if it suits them.
Given that Scotland is not in Schengen right now, the offer of continued mismatch would be a cheap concession for Brussels in return for whatever they really want from the Scots.
So it's a pretty weak story.
Moreover, so old that it has become an excavation. I heard the journalistic duck that “independency means passport controls” at least 40 years ago.
Still, there's an interesting thought in this bearded tale.
Why should it even be expected that independent Scotland will listen to Europe?
Why replace the London chomout for the Brussels one, especially now?
This is a true European story: the great post-war plan to unify Europe has finally failed.
Due to the euro crisis, the so-called Project Europe is officially dead.
Across Europe, insider-indicated parties gain influence towards the EU or the abolition of the euro as a common currency.
Even in Germany, the Eurosceptic Alternative for Germany party – founded this year – came out of nowhere and had already captured nearly five million votes in September’s federal elections, essentially “sweping out” the Free Democrats (the equivalent of Britain’s Liberal Democrats).
On home soil, there has always been resistance to a plan to create a federal Europe.
However, the current economic crisis has become a defining moment.
The austerity dictated by Germany and the European Central Bank, together with a straining jacket strung to national economies through clinging to the common currency, has led many people to believe that Project Europe has gone too far.
The euro crisis has very little to do with national governments managing a high budget deficit – this was only true in the case of Greece.
Rather, it is a system of the euro, which blocks its members at exchange rates favorable for German exporters – and that’s what German politicians want to keep.
Without the possibility of devaluation of the domestic currency, southern Europe has a disadvantage in the field of domestic production compared to Germany.
The only way is to cut revenues and public expenditures – to which Germany encourages.
There is a deeper cause of Europe's malaise performance behind the current problems with the budget and the currency.
As a result of the “green” energy policy backed by Brussels – a code to support French and German companies at the consumer’s expense – European industry pays twice as much for electricity and four times more for gasoline than is the case in the United States.
This is a paralysing disadvantage in terms of cost, as we have already seen in Grangemouth.
Nor would all salary freezes in the world avert the trampling of Europe's petrochemical industry cheap shale oil from the U.S.
The result is a smoldering riot, especially in France, an once enthusiastic supporter of the EU.
After the war, the political elite in France saw the EU as a tool that will keep Germany at bay and allow Paris to stand in the world to the level of Washington.
But Berlin no longer needs Paris as a political legitimacy card and has enforced its own economic policy in Europe, leaving the worn-out French economy to its fate.
Result: Marine Le Pen's right-wing, the anti-European National Front, has just won a major additional election and pushed the ruling Socialists to third place.
The queue is now, with 24 per cent of the vote, the most popular party in France - an early warning to Britain's Labors that they can't conclude that splitting the right will automatically have a positive effect on the left.
How does Le Pen handle her newly acquired popularity among the white working class?
It would like to use the European elections next year to create an anti-European bloc operating against the common currency across the European Parliament.
If anti-European parties do well in these elections, which is likely, such a bloc could dominate the European Parliament for the first time in history.
In doing so, it occurs to me that, in the foreseeable future, the growing anti-European feeling and stance against the common currency will come together and destroy the euro.
The EU will not disappear, but it will rather be transformed into something looser in the sense of “Europe (Sovereign) peoples” that General de Gaulle preferred.
Germany and several of its satellite economies may retain the euro, but France and southern Europe will restore their own currencies.
I expect the United Kingdom to distance itself from this project and cling to the United States.
But Washington's increased interest in the Pacific suggests that Britain will be left to an atlantic chill.
And where will Scotland be?
We can choose to be an area (in principle) of Little England.
Or we can defend our own economic interests – this includes the possibility of announcing to Berlin and Brussels when to leave us alone.
I suppose Scotland would do well well in a relaxed order of Europe, provided it retains its own currency.
Collaboration with other like-minded countries would be easier in a non-federal Europe of nations.
Otherwise, we should take an example from Norway and retain our economic independence.
The SNP government in Scotland is – oddly enough – the most successful political movement not austeritizing measures in Europe, which won by a large majority in 2011 thanks to an opposition stance against cuts planned (and implemented) by Labour Chancellor Alistair Darling and the subsequent coalition of Conservatives and Liberal Democrats.
It would be ridiculous if Scotia now chose independence only to adopt the austerity measures dictated by Berlin and Brussels.
Early Puberty: Advanced Earlier
Research has shown that African-Americans and Hispanics reach puberty earlier than their white peers.
Physical changes do not mean puberty is approaching
It has not been proven that hormones or other chemicals are behind it
Experts say one of the triggers of earlier puberty may be an obesity epidemic.
The trend of earlier puberty is not so evident in boys.
Former CNN correspondent Pat Ethemidge is a journalist who specialises in children's health and family issues.
Should the mother be horrify when her daughter's breasts begin to grow and pubic hair at 7 or 8?
At the annual conference of the American Academy of Pediatric Physicians this week in Orlando, Florida, pediatric endocrinologist Dr Paul Kaplowitz explained that these physical changes are quite common among U.S. girls and represent a new standard.
“I’ve spent a lot of time assuring my parents that it’s usually not a sign of rapid development towards full puberty,” Kaplowitz said
Visible signs of development, such as breast growth, hailing and armpit and body odor, appear earlier in girls.
In the last forty years, however, there has been only a small shift at the age when the first period occurs.
In the United States, the average age is now 12.5 years compared to the age of 12.75 in 1970.
“When their breasts begin to develop, at least two to three years have passed before the first menstruation takes place,” said Kaplowitz, who is also the author of “Early Puberty in girls: The Essential Guide to Coping with This Common Problem.”
The most reliable test of how puberty will continue is time.
It discusses what activates the actual beginning of puberty, but if breast growth is accompanied by a growth spurt before the age of 8, the girl is considered “premature.”
In most cases, the process slows down or stops – which children’s doctors can closely monitor.
Faster growth can be a reason for an endocrinologist to conduct tests in order to rule out serious problems such as a tumor or cyst.
There are treatments that delay early menstruation and prevent further consequences: premature aging of the bones, which can eventually lead to a halt to growth and dwarfism in adulthood.
Recommendations regarding drugs and hormonal treatment are dependent on the age of the child, the speed of development, the rate of growth and emotional maturity.
Psychosociological aspects are also important.
When it comes to the deployment of drugs, Kaplowitz is cautious but acknowledges that “suppressing puberty can alleviate the girl’s behavior problems and her feelings that they are different from their peers.”
Understandable is another big problem: Parents simply don’t want their very young daughters to have their period.
"They're worried that he can get pregnant, or how he's going to handle hygiene," Kaplowitz said.
“It was a shock,” recalls a woman whose daughter began her period at the age of 10.
Although there were indications and we talked about menstruation, it wasn't ready emotionally.
She came home scared and angry that she's the first of her friends.
There are many well-known theories about the causes of premature puberty.
However, there is still no contiguous set of evidence that hormones in milk or other foods, chemicals in the environment or a sexual message in the media are to blame.
Boys, like girls, achieve puberty sooner.
Kaplowitz promotes the assumption that the most serious reason is an epidemic of obesity.
In 2001, he assisted with a study on girls between the ages of 6 and 9, which combines body fat with the timing of puberty.
Other findings support this conclusion, but there are many other contributing factors.
In this country, African-American and HispaniCS tend to reach puberty earlier than their whitish counterparts.
The explanations are different.
From a global point of view, early puberty patterns seem to be influenced by everything from economic conditions through climates to genes.
Another riddle: Although in boys at a young age there is hailing in the face and on the shame, the trend towards the full flourish of early puberty is not as noticeable as is the case with girls.
Other doctors attending the Conference of the American Association of Pediatric Physicians (AAP) have added further complexity to this topic.
Acne and hailing is common even in infants and toddlers.
"We have to be careful when it comes to how we identify the true onset of puberty," said Lawrence Silverman, a pediatric endocrinologist at Goryeb Children's Hospital in Morristown, New Jersey.
Parents should not hesitate to get instructions from their child's doctor on how to talk to their child.
“It may mean having to talk to your baby sooner than you expected,” Kaplowitz said.
If you stay calm, your child is likely to react well.
Girls developing earlier need assurance that even if this happens prematurely, this process is a normal part of life.
