Aircraft manufacturers argue over seat width, large orders are at stake
A dispute over the width of second-class seats for long-haul flights flared up between leading aircraft makers, giving rise to the cause of a bitter exchange of views on the Dubai air show this month.
The subject of the dispute is the width of the seats provided on long-distance long-haul flights for economy class – not exactly the one that airlines would normally precede, but whose allocated space is the key to the efficiency requirements of the last aircraft offered by Airbus SAS and Boeing.
Airbus this week called for industry standards that would offer at least 46 cm-wide seats in economy class, but chief U.S. rival Boeing says airlines are supposed to decide.
The dispute arised at a time when aircraft manufacturers are competing in sales of the largest version of their dual-engine long-haul aircraft with a potential record number of orders expected during the event from November 17-21.
The way in which the rear part of the aircraft is decomposed – especially whether there are 9 or 10 seats next to each other – is the basis of the economic performance requirements for the designs of the new “mini-jumbo” aircraft.
Boeing says the improved "777X" model will transport 406 people in economic seats with a width of more than 43 cm spread over 10 in each row.
Airbus says rival versions of its A350 will deliver 350 people in 46 cm wide economic seats spread over 9.
Giant aircraft often attack each other on technical issues through advertisements in professional media.
Airbus is now appealing directly to the public ahead of the air show in Dubai, where 777X is expected to prevail with more than 100 orders.
He recently preliminarily showed what might be the start of a new advertising war when financiers showed a picture depicting three people crowded in a restaurant headlining “Would you like it?”
"Boeing promotes long-haul flights in narrower seats than in regional turboprop aircraft," Airbus sales manager John Leahy said.
As the eating swabs change, people are larger, but the seats on the planes have not changed radically.
From the early 1970s, when the Boeing 747 jumbo defined modern long-distance air travel, by the turn of the century, according to U.S. Department of Health data, the weight of an average American between the ages of 40 and 49 has increased by 10 percent.
According to U.S. health statistics, the average American in the 21st century has 100 cm at his waist.
Airbus argues that its rival adheres to the seat concept from the 1950s, when the average width of the seats of the newly baptized aircraft was lower.
Airbus says it has carried out research that suggests extending the seat by 2.5 cm will improve sleep quality by 53 percent.
Airbus refuses the dimensions of Airbus' seats, saying it is not within the authority of aircraft manufacturers to decide how airlines export fees and equipment.
The company also says the research shows that an experience in the cabin depends on more matters than the width of the seat.
“It’s really a matter of providing airlines with flexibility and allowing them to carry out things they’re convinced they have to do to be successful,” said Kent Craver, a cabin space expert.
They don't want us to dictate to them what yields their profit.
They know their business better than anyone.
There is more elbow space essential for passengers, but for suppliers it is a growing problem that can affect yields.
Behind this controversy is a plant in aircraft orders with an estimated trade in the coming decades worth at least $700 billion in catalogue prices, enough to deflect the dagger of the scales of U.S. and European exports.
As Reuters reported in July, the layout of the seats is exactly what drives the battle between the last planes.
Both Airbus and Boeing claim to achieve 20 percent better efficiency in their latest designs of twin-engine long-haul aircraft than market leaders in this segment, the Boeing 777-300ER with 365 seats.
Boeing's performance demands depend in part on the 777X's 10-seat design comparison and the 777 9-seater in-row concept.
Profit in unit costs is weakened compared to the 10-seater concept.
"The reason Boeing is doing this is to pack more seats, and make their planes more competitive than our products," said Kevin Keniston, head of passenger comfort at Airbus for Europe.
On the other hand, analysts say cabins with 10-seaters in a row for existing 777 models are consistent with the fact that many passengers are for a denser layout that can go hand in hand with lower fees.
"A forty-five-centimeter-wide seat would be great for passengers, but the reality is that from Airbus's business point of view, the design is motivated by the threat of the 777 model," said cabin interior expert Mary Kirby, founder and editor of Runway Girl Network.
Airbus and Boeing don't deliver seats, but they offer airlines a catalogue of suppliers so they can choose.
Deparrated plane vendors even carry a measuring zone with them to check a competitive layout.
While it boasts comfort, all manufacturers also offer aircraft with a highly dense layout for low-cost companies and regional travelers.
Airbus offers the A350 with 10 seats in a row, but says it hasn't sold it yet.
Until recently, Airbus emphasized the need for a greater modification of the cabin by offering wider aisle seats for some of its aircraft.
Experts say that without the support of a single other manufacturer of large modern aircraft, the call for new industry standards will remain unheard, but may deter 777X sales.
The number of people infected with HIV has exceeded 1,000 in Prague
In the Czech Republic, the number of people infected grows every year.
For example, in 2002, tests confirmed the infection in 50 people, five years later there were 121, and in 2010 there were 180.
Last year, 212 people were infected and it was a record number.
"In my opinion, there is no doubt that the total number of newly diagnosed cases this year is no longer in doubt," commented AIDS expert Miroslav Hlavatý, who for years led the Prague House of Light for Patients.
According to him, from January to September, there were 165 HIV patients in the country, this year there were 176 in the same period.
From the beginning of follow-up until the end of this September, tests confirmed HIV in 1710 men and 353 women, with the most infected in Prague.
Their number reached 1005 in September.
There were 206 infected people in the Central Bohemian Region.
156 people from the South Moravian Region, 130 from Moravian-Silesian and 116 from Ústí nad Labem also listened to the diagnosis.
The fewest infected came from the Highlands, there were 19.
Overall, 88 percent of patients became infected during sex.
Fourteen people have become infected with blood transfusions in the past.
However, since the beginning of testing, more than a quarter of a century, such a case has not appeared in the Czech Republic.
Six children have also been born with HIV, transferred from their mother.
Nonsenses in Gravity are, but the spectacle is beautiful, experts say
A fantastic film from space Gravity some time after the premiere selled cinemas in the Czech Republic.
According to the Union of Film Distributors, almost 150 thousand viewers saw it in October and earned more than 26 million crowns.
Most of the reviews evaluate the image positively.
However, there are some inconsistencies.
News server Lidovky.cz asked experts from astronomers and astrophysicists about how they liked the film and whether they noticed any nonsense that appeared in the film.
Jiøí Dušek (Director of the Observatory and Planetarium Brno): I like science and fantasy films, which I consider to be modern, dreamy fairy tales.
Gravity, not to mention it, even though it looks like a "physically real" performance.
It's not, but that doesn't mean he's bad.
I've enjoyed it too much.
For example, you may notice Sandra Bullock's hair, which somehow does not float in a weightless state.
Hubble's Space Telescope and the International Space Station are moving along significantly different orbits, so they can't be seen in one shot.
Some viewers of the film blame the unrealistic effect and the whole behavior of the fire in a state of weightlessness.
According to them, suits would not withstand such wounds either.
The heroes in Gravity often get into a collision with different objects, and suits without much hassle can withstand everything.
Criticism wasn't spared even the plot.
Such a large-scale tragedy is unrealistic, according to some debaters.
In addition, she wonders why the doctor was sent to space to repair the technique.
People argue in forums about "unreal" rope behavior.
It feels like it’s designed for bungee jumping.
Marcel Grün (Director of the Observatory and Planetarium of Prague): I have only seen it on a wide screen so far, but I can clearly say, I have been enchanted.
I'm glad it wasn't a documentary film, it was science fiction.
And for such a story, the author has the right (almost an obligation) not to adhere consistently to reality.
"More than the inconsistencies you mentioned, I was touched by how three times the cosmic station with the degrading level of technology (last Chinese) is shown."
“Chinese won’t be until the end of the decade and it’ll probably look better than it is in a movie.”
Vladimír Karas (Director of the Astronomical Institute of the Academy of Sciences of the Czech Republic): In computer-generated scenes, the authors are very successfully trying not to violate the laws of physics, and so the naturally oriented spectator will appreciate that during various cosmic collisions and collisions, the momentum is quite rightly preserved, in the dictionary of physics, also the view of the Earth from space is very realistic, including the thin shell of the atmosphere and the aurora
There are certainly inconsistences during a more detailed examination.
In addition, for example, while reading the comments, I found that the preferred direction of circulation of various cosmic debris in orbit appears reversed in some scenes than it should actually be (from west to east).
Jiří Štěpán (employee of the Astronomical Institute of the Academy of Sciences of the Czech Republic): Some things have been adapted by the creators to their needs, but I did not record any fundamental and heaven-calling offense against logic or natural laws in the film, and in comparison with other films of similar focus it can be seen that the creators cared about the credibility of the depicted events.
From moments that don't make sense, it's worth naming the fact that Hubble's space telescope orbits an otherwise inclined orbit than the ISS.
The consequence is a significantly different mutual speed, and the rescue operation would not actually be able to take place as it was shown in the film.
Furthermore, at the moment of Kowalsky (Clooney) and Stone (Bullock), I didn’t notice that the entire ISS would rotate, so there’s not even a clear origin of the force on Kowalski, which forces the two actors to let go when they’re no longer moving towards each other.
New nicotine vaccine can take away the pleasure of smoking
Scientists have developed a nicotine vaccine that can take away the pleasure of smoking a cigarette.
A single dose of the vaccine could protect the mouse from nicotine addiction for a lifetime.
There is a need to conduct additional tests before human trials, which will take several years, but Professor Ronald Crystal of the Weill Cornell Medical School in New York says the first indications are good.
"We firmly hope that this kind of vaccination strategy can finally help millions of smokers who have tried to quit and exhausted all methods available on the market today, but have found that their nicotine dependence is so strong to overcome all current approaches," Professor Cornell said.
The new vaccine contains a harmless virus that has been assembled to carry genetic information to produce antinicotine antibodies.
The virus selectively infects liver cells, which then begin to produce a smooth stream of these antibodies.
Antibodies chase all nicotine molecules in the bloodstream and neutralize them before they reach the brain, preventing the smoker from getting nicotine hit.
In vaccinated mice tests, by which nicotine was subsequently supplied, continued their normal activities.
But mice that weren't vaccinated "calmed down," the researchers said, which is a sign that nicotine got into their brains.
The experiments are described in the journal Science Translational Medicine.
Previous tobacco vaccines have failed because they contained antibodies.
Injections had to be given as often as to maintain a sufficient level of antibodies, so they proved expensive and impractical.
The cost of the new vaccines should be much lower because they are converting liver cells into antibody factories.
Professor Crystal said that if future vaccines are completely safe for people, they could be given to children before they want to try a cigarette, preventing nicotine dependence.
However, it is more likely to be used by smokers to stop.
"They're going to know that if they start smoking again, they won't enjoy the nicotine vaccine, and that's going to help them get rid of this habit," he said.
British scientists said the results were interesting, but warned that further research was needed.
Tripodi denies being influenced by Obeid
The former Labour minister of New South Wales (Australia) will be investigated by the State Committee for the Oversight of Corruption.
Joe Tripodi, the former New South Wales minister, has denied changing coastal rental policy at the request of his political advisor Eddie Obeid, who had hidden interests in three properties on government-controlled property.
The Independent Anti-Corruption Committee (ICAC) on Friday extended its search on whether Mr Obeid had lobbied with several state ministers to be rented at Circular Quay, where Obeids owned two restaurants and a cafe, without having to take part in the selection process after it expired in August 2005.
The committee is now investigating claims that Mr Tripodi was aware of Mr Obeid's secret interests on those properties, based on evidence presented on Thursday by Mr Tripodi's former deputy chief of staff, Lynne Ashpole.
During years of talks launched in 2005, the government pushed for leases to go to public tenders.
The tenants were against it and also wanted longer-term contractual terms.
In 2009, leases for businesses in Circular Quay, which brought US$2.5 million a year to Obeid, were resumed without the need to take part in a tender.
Mr Tripodi, who was port minister from February 2006 to November 2009, was originally a supporter of public tenders.
But he denied that the changes were made at the request of Mr Obeid, who, as Mr Tripodi acknowledged, urged a shift in the government's rental policy.
The transcript of the calls submitted in the ICAC showed calls from August to September 2007 between Lords Obeid and Mr Tripodi and Steve Dunn, a senior official who came to the port resort after working under Mr. Obeid in the Fisheries Department.
"Was the subject of conversations during these phone calls the development of the policy of commercial leases?" asked Assistant Commissioner Anthony Whealy Mr Tripodi.
"No," Mr. Tripodi replied.
I can't remember what we were talking about, but definitely not about this.
Definitely not between me and Mr. Obeid.
Israeli military jets attacked a target in Syria, a responsible official said
Israeli military jets hit a target in the Syrian port of Lazikiya on Thursday night, a responsible administration official confirmed to Fox News.
This responsible official did not specifically say what the target was, but said there was at least one.
The Associated Press reported that the targets were SA-125 Russian-made missiles.
At least twice before this year, Israel carried out an air attack on missile deliveries in Syria.
Many things have moved, and it's different.
For example, the commentator Pečinka spoke in such a way that it was possible to agree with it, while Chairman Štěch stirred discord.
Governor Chovanec was suspicious to me as soon as he was elected to the bureau, and he confirmed it.
President Sobotka has always been, and continues to be a symbol of the misery of the ČSSD.
The other gentlemen mainly demonstrated their weaknesses.
Senator Dienstbier is principled and legible and unusable, Vice-Chairman Zaorálek has made it impossible to do the most with an interview on television.
