Foreign workers with a visa licence may undergo a “legitimacy” test
As the government intends to expand police crackdowns, it is considering a "legitimacy" test for foreign workers with a 457 visa.
The test, if adopted, will be applied through criteria to prevent the 457 programme from being used to fill unqualified jobs, or as a backdoor for moving family and friends to Australia.
When a government debate article was published today, former Labour MP Maxine McKew condemned the government's rhetoric about foreign workers, saying it could offend Australia's neighbours.
“Vocum statements by ‘foreigners at the end of the queue’ and ‘works first for Australians’ are a very unpleasant remnant from when unions demanded a protected labour market,” she told the Australian-Indian Institute.
Historically, this meant that a white workforce should have been protected – and I wouldn’t be surprised if anyone in this region noticed an echo of this historical artifact.
The discussion article discusses 12 measures previously considered by former Immigration Secretary Chris Bowen.
Immigration Minister Brendan O'Connor, who was in Sri Lanka yesterday, where he is in talks with officials over human smuggling, has implemented five of the recommended changes and the others remain to be considered.
If ‘legitimacy’ criteria are adopted, visa applicants may be examined in detail whether “the nomination is justified in situations where the candidate is in a relationship or personal connection with the owner or the relevant person of the sponsoring business.”
After previous experience with businesses that intended to sponsor a small number of workers but then employed hundreds, businesses may also be required to justify the number of visa holders 457.
Meanwhile, a 35-year-old man from Sri Lanka seeking asylum has died with a suspected heart attack after arriving this week on an asylum boat for the Christmas Islands.
The man's nine-year-old frightened son travelled to Australia along with him, and after his father's death on Wednesday, he was comforted by an adult cousin who was also on a boat.
Australian authorities with the man hurried to a hospital in the Christmas Islands, where he died.
The British government has announced a study that aims to increase the benefits of the HS2 railway project for Scotland.
HS2's work suggests that high-speed services for Scotland and northern England will begin once the first phase is triggered in 2026.
Transport Secretary Baroness Kramer said the project "will bring the UK together."
Scotch Transport Secretary Keith Brown said he was "excited" by collaborating with the British government on the plan.
The first phase will consist of a new high-speed train line between London and the West Midlands.
After the completion of Phase Two, he will lead from Manchester to Leeds.
In June, the government reconsidered the estimated cost of building a high-speed line between London and northern England from GBP 32.7 billion to GBP 42.6 billion.
The British government, which has held talks with national transport firm Transport Scotland, has instructed HS2 to identify additional rail capacity and the possibility of improving travel times for northern England and Scotland.
This includes the possibility of eventually travel times from Glasgow and Edinburgh to London corresponding to three or fewer hours.
Baroness Kramer said: "Our goal for HS2 is really a national network that will better connect the UK and its cities."
We've given SH2 the green light because the benefit it will bring is huge.
Without this, we are facing a crisis in the capacity of our rail network.
But it’s also about interconnection – 18 cities, including Glasgow and Edinburgh, will be better interconnected across the UK thanks to HS2.
Scotch Minister Alistair Carmichael added: "Today's announcement is good news for Scotland."
On behalf of the Scottish Government, Keith Brown called on Mr Carmichael to "unequivocally" promote Scottish integration into the HS2 network.
Mr Brown said: "The high-speed track has the potential to bring immeasurable economic benefits to Scotland, but it also adds the economic weight to Scotland in the overall case of high-speed rail across Britain."
Therefore, we are enthusiastically working with the British government to explore the possibility of bringing high-speed railways to Scotland, bringing benefits to everyone and supplementing the Glasgow-Edinburgh line, which the Scottish Government is planning anyway.
I look forward to reviewing the report on this study, together with British ministers, over the next year and on joint decision-making on the next steps.
Together with Bárta, the inspection and the anti-corruption police analyst were also arrested
Information about the arrest of Vít Bárta began to spread on Thursday evening, when the report was carried by news server Prvnízpravy.cz.
MF TODAY soon discovered that the case had been handled by the General Inspection of the Security Forces (GIBS), its people were to arrest Bárta in the center of Prague.
It took place in front of the Four Seasons Hotel.
The inspection deals with cases where there are police officers among the accused, but its authority also applies to civilians.
According to MF DNES, one of the officers of the anti-corruption police - the head of analytics and informatics Jan Petržílek - is among those arrested.
In the past, he worked for the Security Information Service.
Jaroslav Ibehej, a spokesman for the anti-corruption police, declined to comment on the information, addincing that the media had to turn to GIBS in this matter.
However, Radka Sandorová, spokesperson for the inspection, did not want to say anything about the case, immediately referring to the Municipal Public Prosecutor’s Office in Prague.
"We will not comment on this question with regard to the protection of classified facts," said Štěpánka Zenklová, a spokeswoman for the prosecutor's office.
It can also be inferred from this vague reaction that the event took place, but it is in secrecy mode, which does not allow the speakers to say anything specific.
Bárta's lawyer Oldřich Chudoba is in a similar situation.
He told the media that he represents Bárta "even in this matter," but since it is classified information, he cannot confirm or deny Barta's arrest.
Some media initially speculated that Bárta's arrest might be related to former police president Petr Lessy.
Bárta, together with the former chairman of VV Radek John, who sat at the head of the Ministry of the Interior.
The dispute, which because of this flared between former Prime Minister Petr Nečas and R&D, had to be resolved even by then-President Vaclav Klaus.
I don't know where this connection came from.
No one has contacted me, I haven't been interrogated.
"I don't really know anything about things," Lessy told iDNES.cz.
In January 2011, Lessy became head of police.
It was officially promoted by Radek John, but the real head of the party was Bárta at the time.
Bárta has now run for elections for the Úsvit movement, his parliamentary mandate has escaped him only narrowly - by 175 votes.
Dawn leader Tomio Okamura said he had no idea what it was.
"Vít Bárta was one of the many candidates of many parties on our candidate, and as I have repeatedly said before the elections, he was not or was not a member of the Dawn of Direct Democracy Tomio Okamura," said Bárta Okamura, who said before the election that he had given Bárta the space to run for Dawn because he helped him solve the problem of bailiffs.
Vít Bárta found himself in court in 2012 with his former co-worker from VV Jaroslav Škárka.
The rules on the use of electronic devices on aircraft will remain in force in Australia for the time being
Australian airline passengers will have to keep switching off their tablets and smartphones while launching and landing, regardless of changes in the U.S. in order to ease regulations on those devices.
The U.S. Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) has given U.S. companies space to change their procedures, so passengers will be able to read e-books on their devices, watch videos, or play games during the critical phases of the flight if devices remain in “aerial” mode.
Passengers can already do this during the bulk of the flight, but it annoys many people that they cannot have access to their e-books during takeoff and landing.
Australia's carriers are looking into this decision, which requires a lot of work from U.S. carriers to meet those requirements, but have indicated that they don't have plans to change their procedures anytime soon.
The Civil Aviation Safety Authority (CASA) also said it had examined the announcement, but stressed that restrictions on the use of electronic devices at critical phases of flight are still in force in Australia.
"CASA currently has no specific regulations regulating the use of electronic devices on an aircraft," he said.
This issue is covered by regulations requiring aircraft operators to ensure that safety is maintained at all times and that passengers act in accordance with the safety instructions they receive from crew members.
Virgin, which has already spoken to CASA about extending the use of its on-board wi-fi entertainment system, has been inclined by the change, but has announced that it will submit to the regulator's decision.
"We'd like CASA to carry out an analysis of permission to use electronic devices, because we really think it would improve customers' experiences now that we're (wireless on-board entertainment," the spokesperson said.
Qantas has announced that it will stick to current regulations for the time being.
"Our current policy is that electronic devices must not be used during take-offs and landings, and we have no intention of changing that anytime soon," he said.
FAA regulations apply to U.S. airlines.
But we are constantly interested in developments in the regulatory area, which can be useful for our passengers, and we will certainly thoroughly study the FAA’s decisions and the reasons behind it.
In the case of air carriers from the U.S
Carriers will have to prove that their planes can tolerate radio interference of mobile phones, as well revise manuals, training materials, hand luggage programs and passenger information.
"Once the airline confirms its fleet's tolerance, it can allow passengers to use portable and lightweight electronic devices such as tablets, e-readers and smartphones, at any altitude," the FAA said.
In rare cases of low visibility, the crew orders the passengers to switch off during the landing of the device.
The group also recommended that heavier devices be safely stored under the seats during take-off and descent, or in the storage compartment above their heads.
With the growing misery of the middle class, pawnshops in Singapore are flourishing
Janani Amirthalinga stops a gold bracelet, ring and earrings at a pawn shop in the Bendemeer shopping centure in Singapore to pay tuition.
“My husband and I just bought a house and I gave all my money for it,” says Ms Amirthalinga.
Although she receives 2400 dollars (3000 SGDs) each month as an administrator, and her husband also works, the family's monthly income is inadequate, she says.
The demand across areas in Southeast Asia – where household debt is increasing – is indeed such that ValueMax, where a woman conducts her transaction, became the third paworker on the list this week on the Singapore stock exchange.
Stopping jewelry isn’t just a quick way to borrow cash – 1300 SGD in the case of Mrs Amirthalinga – but it’s also almost as cheap as unsecured bank loans.
Pawnshops in Singapore routinely apply an annual percentage rate of 17 percent, just above the 15.4 percent rate offered by United Overseas Bank, a local bank with a branch in the same shopping centre.
However, pawnshops have the advantage that they do not require verification of a person (payment history) or proof of income, and they can provide a loan faster than banks.
And that's why, as families experience pressure because of rising living costs and rising household and consumer debt, millions of people across the region are turning to pawnshops.
After five years of massive growth since the global financial crisis and inexpensive loans backed by loose monetary policies in advanced economies, low and middle-income families are turning to pawnshops to deal with the deteriorating financial situation.
This week, rating agency Standard & Poor's cited the increasing use of household loans primarily due to rising rents as a risk factor for the creditworthiness of Asian banks.
She said Malaysia, Thailand and Singapore, due to gross domestic product, have the highest household debt in Asia.
Malaysia is at the forefront of the ranking with a ratio of 80 percent of GDP, an increase from 60 percent in 2008.
Economists were also worried about the high level of consumer debt in Thailand, which this week had just come out of a technical recession.
On Thursday, the data showed continued weak exports and attenuations of consumer demand.
“The bottom line is that, with increasing costs, people from medium to low [income scale] will try to supplement their income wherever they can,” said Song Seng Wun, an economist at Malaysia’s CIMB bank.
Historically high gold prices over the past two years have contributed to the fact that people are hastily stopping their personal possessions and taking the opportunity to exchange their family jewellery for cash.
About 70 percent of the items stopped in 200 city pawnshops make up gold.
People are saying: the price of gold looks good, let's stop Grandma ́s gold necklace and get it back next month.
EasyMoney's largest pawnshop operator, EasyMoney, has seen up to a 20 percent increase in the number of customers using its pawnshops in recent months.
The growth in the pawn industry is so great that ValueMax, a branch operator in downtown Bendemeer and 15 similar in Singapore, plans to expand not only to neighboring Malaysia – where it has four offices – but also outside of Asia, says ValueMax Executive Director Yah Lee Ching.
The company will fund it using 60 percent of the 66 million SGD it acquired this week on the Singapore Stock Exchange.
While some discount creditors have come under fire for criticism over high interest rates, Ms Yeah says the pledge not only offers lower rates than other lenders, but it also doesn't directly increase debt.
"Customers stop items they already own, and monetizing personal property doesn't increase the household's debt," he says.
In society, the pledge of property is increasingly accepted as a means to ensure short-term, secure financing.
Moreover, among those using pawnshops, there are not only people in financial distress.
Wealthy people from Singapore also take advantage of ValueMax branches when they stop golden rods or Rolex watches and can get up to 60 percent of the purchase price in cash.
Our customers are out of all possible circumstances.
“They include wealthy individuals who need to borrow in the short term because of their daring deals or investments, or small businesses that need help resolving problems in their cash flow,” Ms Yeah said.
Sometimes they just need money as quickly as possible.
325-million-dollar rescue package for Tasmanian health
The federal government insists that a $325-million rescue package for a dimiled health service in Tasmania includes tough conditions that ensure the state government doesn't waste funds.
Federal Health Secretary Tanya Plibersek announced that the Australian Union is taking "expedited measures" to avert a crisis caused by the island's aging population, a higher number of chronic diseases and systemic disorders.
Financing for four years was decided after government consultations with independent Tasmanian MP Andrew Wilkie.
“The government has come up with an emergency rescue package that, we believe, will solve the specific problems faced by the state,” Ms Plibersek said today.
The $325-million package includes $31 million for surgeries at the patient's request.
Another 2600 operations, including orthopedic operations and cataract operations, will help remove indebtedness.
The money is also for freely accessible clinics in Hobart and Launceston, better post-operative care, training of health specialists, mental health services and the introduction of personal electronic health records systems in local hospitals.
"These investments are responding to ideas that leading clinicians have told me to help the Tasmanian health system in the best way," Ms Plibersek said.
The minister stressed that the Tasmanian government must require a strict reporting regime and registration obligations.
The state will have to maintain the current level of funding in order to receive money from the Australian Union, and report monthly on where additional funds have been spent.
A three-member committee will be appointed to ensure that the state supplies services as efficiently as possible.
Mr Wilkie said today that $325 million would be useless "until true reform is followed in order to lay a more sustainable basis of the Tasmanian public health system."
Still, he praised the government for responding to his request for accelerated aid, which he first raised in talks with the prime minister in early May.
"I believe the federal auxiliary package can extricate the state's public health service from critical condition," Mr Wilkie said.
According to the state government, these additional chosen procedures will significantly reverse the recent cuts.
Peter Dutton, a spokesman for the federal health opposition, believes today's statement is just a "patchmark."
"The reason I'm here is because the Labour government has taken $430 million out of our health system," he told ABC TV.
You can't have a state government that takes nearly half a billion dollars, and an Australian union that donates $300 million, and pretend it's good new.
Mr Dutton urged Ms. Plibersek to guarantee that not a single dollar from the bailout package would be extradited for higher bureaucracy.
Guillaume Nicloux has adapted Denise Diderot's novel, which brings an exceptional film quest and a portrayal of the times, but it's also more boring than it should be.
It unfolds in the 60's of the 18th century in France and tells the grim story of Suzanne, a young noblewoman who is sent to a monastery by her family.
As he rebels, he experiences extremely cruel treatment from a criminally degenerate mother superior and, for others, becomes an object of sexual passion.
The film never slips into lust or sensationality, and that's its problem.
The ceremonial character of this story leads to the fact that even viewers will experience torment similar to the wearing of a sackcloth shirt.
Ski resorts want to make it cheaper for families with children
Syria has destroyed its means for the production of chemical weapons, the supervisory authority says
Syria has destroyed essential equipment for the production of chemical weapons and venoum gas munitions, the Committee for Global Oversight of Chemical Weapons said on Thursday, while fierce conflicts broke out in the north of the country, not far from one of the sites where poisonous substances are believed to be stored.
On Thursday, the Syrian activist group also said that more than 120,000 residents had been killed since the start of the civil war nearly three years ago.
Announcement of the Organization for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons came the day before
by a date set by that organisation based in The Hague to destroy or prevent the operation of all equipment for the production of chemical weapons and machinery for the mixing of chemicals in order to create poisonous gas and fill munitions.
The completion of this essentially initial phase of destruction is an important milestone in an ambitious timetable that aims to destroy all Damascus chemical weapons by mid-2014.
Destroying the equipment means that Syria will no longer be able to produce new chemical weapons.
Damascus, however, has yet to begin the destruction of existing weapons and supplies.
This country is believed to have about 1,000 tons of chemicals and weapons including pyperite and sarine nerve gas.
The announcement came as fighting raged on Thursday in the city of Safira, which experts refer to as the headquarters of both chemical weapons factories and storage facilities, the Syrian human rights observatory based in Britain said.
A group of activists monitoring the total death toll through a network of activists in Syria announced on Thursday that 120 296 people had died.
Of these, 61 067 are civilians, including 6,365 children.
On the governing side, 29,954 members of Bashar al-Assad's armed forces, 18 678 pro-government fighters and 187 militants from Lebanon's Hezbollah.
Among those killed will also be 2,202 government defectors and around 5 375 opposition fighters, many of whom are foreigners.
On July 25, the U.N. estimated that 100 000 people had died in the conflict since March 2011.
This number has not been updated since then.
The conflict forced about 2 million residents to leave the country.
Assad's forces have fought in Saphira against the insurgents, many of whom are affiliated with Al Qaeda, for weeks.
Observatory said on Thursday that the losses were on both sides, but did not have specific numbers.
The fighting has underscored the dangers faced by inspectors for chemical weapons, who have to deal with close deadlines in their mission to get rid of a chemical arsenal in the midst of an ongoing civil war.
In a statement from the OPCW, which is working closely with the U.N
He added that "no further control activities are currently planned."
Earlier this week, inspectors said they had completed the first round of verification work when they visited 21 of the 23 sites declared by Damascus.
The inspectors said they were unable to visit two places because of safety concerns.
On Thursday, the OPCW said the two sites had been "abandoned, according to Syria, and ... items of the chemical weapons program it contained were moved to two other declared sites that had been inspected."
It wasn't immediately clear whether the facility in Safira was one of those spots that OPCW inspectors couldn't visit.
Syria has approved a plan for the complete destruction of its chemical weapons, which must be approved next month by OPCW's executive committee.
“I bow to the courage and bravery you’ve shown in fulfilling this most challenging mission this organization has ever had,” said observer director Ahmet Uzumcu, in a commentary issued by the OPCW.
Now, in its third year, Sunni Muslim rebels and Assad's government, with its security forces full of members of the Alawite sect, an offshoot of Shiite Islam, are facing each other.
In terms of further developments, Observatory chief Rami Abdul Rahman said there had been a powerful explosion on Wednesday inside an air defense facility in Syria's Lazikiya coastal province.
He said the cause of the explosion was unknown.
Anger over the verdict handed down over the plotter of the bombing in Bali
Survivors and relatives of 202 people killed in the Bali bombing in 2002 reacted angrily to the verdiculation handed down over the last of the outlaws who were brought to justice and said Umar Patek should have been executed.
Patek, who spent nearly 10 years on the run as one of the most wanted individuals in Southeast Asia, was sentenced yesterday to 20 years in prison for putting together explosive devices that were used in a bomb attack.
In 15 years, he could be paroled.
This 45-year-old man was found guilty of mass murder in an attack on two nightclubs in the popular Kuta tourist area, after which 202 people remained dead, including 88 Australians, and many wounded.
He has also been found guilty on several other counts of terrorism-related indictments, including a series of bombings on churches in Indonesia on Christmas Eve of 2000.
The prosecution demanded a life sentence, even though it may have insisted that a man nicknamed "Demolition man" (the crasher) was given the death penalty thanks to his reputation as an explosives expert.
The decision revived the painful memories of June Corteen, a mother from Perth, who lost her 39-year-old twins Jane and Jenny in a destructive attack triggered by Patek and other plotters nearly a decade ago.
With tears in her eyes, she said Patek should have been sentenced to death.
I really think he should follow in the footsteps of the others.
Ms. Corteen told AAP: "He should be put in front of an execution squad."
Every day I have to live with the fact that I won't see other grandchildren and my daughters.
Club Sari was leveled when a heavy charge placed outside a parked van was detonated shortly after 23 o'clock on October 12, 2002.
Peter Hughes was at Paddy's bar, where just 20 seconds earlier, a suicide bomber detonated a backpack filled with explosives.
As a result of the blast, he fell into a coma for a month, and while he was kept alive, he "died" three times.
Mr Hughes said Pateka should meet the same fate as the three other members of the Jama Islamiya terrorist cell responsible for the massacre – Amrozi, Muchlas and Imama Samudra – who were executed four years ago.
This guy should get the death penalty like nobody's.
Keeping him alive... there's just no reason to keep him alive.
There's not that much getting 20 years for killing 202 people and wounding hundreds more.
Patek was the last bomber in Bali to stand trial.
He avoided arrest for nearly a decade, but was eventually captured in January 2011 in the Pakistani city of Abbottabad, where less than four months later U.S
During the trial, an FBI agent said in his testimony that intelligence reports had uncovered that Patek had been in Pakistan to meet with bin Laden in an attempt to re-establish links between terrorist groups from Southeast Asia and Al Qaeda.
"He didn't give up voluntarily," Ms. Corteen said.
To this day, he has not felt sorry for how much grief he has caused others.
The verdict comes before the 10th anniversary of the attack, which will be later this year and to which ceremonies in Bali and Australia will belong.
"We'll see a lot of tears this year," Ms. Corteen said.
Patek can still appeal the verdict.
FAA: Air passengers can now use technological gains on aircraft (but they are not allowed to call from their mobile phones)
Airline passengers will be able to use their electronic devices "from the gateway" - in addition to calls from their mobile phones - to read, play games, watch movies and listen to music based on eagerly awaited new rules issued by the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) on Thursday.
However, passengers should not expect changes to occur immediately.
How quickly the change will be introduced will vary by individual airlines, FAA administrator Michael Huerta said at a press conference.
Airlines will have to prove to the FAA’s administration to what extent their aeroplanes comply with the new rules and that they have updated cabin crew training manuals and equipment storage directives to comply with the new rules.
The FAA said it has already received plans from some airlines to expand the use of portable electronic devices on aircraft.
Among the companies that have already handed over the plans are Delta and JetBlue.
"Depending on the status of such a plan, we can improve the widespread use of electronic devices very quickly," the FAA said in a statement.
Currently, passengers are demanded to turn off their smartphones, tablets and other devices as soon as the aircraft's doors are closed.
They can't turn them on until the plane reaches an altitude of 3 kilometers and the captain doesn't issue permission.
Passengers are expected to turn off their devices again when the plane is dropping to land and not turn them on until the plane is on the ground.
Under the new rules, airlines whose planes are properly protected against electronic interference can allow their passengers to use their devices during take-off, landing and driving, the FAA said.
Most new transport aircraft and other aircraft that have been modified so that passengers can use Wifi at a higher altitude are expected to meet these criteria.
Laura Glading, president of the Association of Professional Stewards, welcomed the changes.
"Once the new rules are safely introduced - and we will work closely with transport companies - it will be a success," Glading said in a statement.
We’re tired that we look like “guardians.”
However, surfing on the Internet, sending e-mails and sms or downloading data will continue to be banned below 3 kilometers of altitude, the agency said.
Passengers will be notified that their smartphones, tablets and other devices will be switched to air mode.
So still no Words with friends, an online scrabble-type game played by actor Alec Baldwin on his smartphone in 2011 when, as is well known, he was kicked out of an American Airlines plane because he refused to turn off his device when the plane was parked at the gate.
And heavier devices like laptops will have to continue to be stored due to fears that they could injure someone if they fly around the cabin.
Calls from mobile phones will continue to be banned on board.
The rules for the use of mobile phones are not determined by the FAA, but by the U.S. Federal Telecommunications Committee.
The FAA may lift the ban on some electronic devices during take-off and landing.
Last month, Mark Rosenker of the State Transportation Security Committee, a national transport safety expert at CBS News, said mobile phones continued to consider themselves a risk.
"Mobile phones are really a problem, not only because they can potentially create interference with navigation devices, but we also know from FCC data that in the air they can cause interference to broadcast towers," Rosenker said.
An expert advisory committee created by the FAA's administration to investigate the issue suggested last month that the government allow wider use of electronic devices.
There has been pressure on the FAA in recent years to ease restrictions on their use.
Critics such as Senator Claire McCaskill, a Missouri Democrat, say there's no rightful security reason for the ban.
Restrictions are also becoming increasingly difficult to enforce, because the use of such devices is ubiquitous.
Some studies suggest that up to one-third of passengers also forget the obligation to shut down their devices or ignore it.
The FAA began restricting the use of electronic devices by passengers in 1966 in response to the interference of navigation and communication equipment, when passengers began carrying FM radios with them, technical achievements of that time.
New airlines are much more dependent on electrical systems than those of previous generations, but they are also constructed, and approved by the FAA's administration to be resistant to electronic interference.
For several years now, airlines have offered passengers to use Wi-Fi at travel altitudes.
Plans adapted for Wi-Fi systems are also more resistant to interference.
The vast majority of airlines are expected to adapt to greater use of electronic devices under the new rules, Huert said.
Current electronic devices generally radiate much less intensive radio broadcasting than was the case with previous generations' devices.
For example, e-readers emit only minimal broadcasts when turning the page.
But broadcasting is stronger when devices download or send data.
Among those pushing to ease restrictions on the use of these devices by passengers is Amazon.com.
In 2011, representatives of this company loaded the airliner with their Kindle electronic readers and conducted a test flight, but there was no problem.
Members of the FAA's advisory committee have expressed mixed feelings regarding whether these facilities pose any risks.
Douglas Kidd of the National Association of Airline Passengers said he is convinced that interference from these devices is real, even if the risk is minimal.
Other members of the committee declared that there were only fragmentary reports from pilots designed to support the fact that such devices could cause the interference of aerospace systems, and most of these reports are very old.
However, the committee recommended that the FAA allow pilots to order passengers to shut off their devices while flying by instruments in low visibility.
The travel industry has welcomed these changes and it is a reasonable procedure for the travelling public, which uses the technology to an abundant extent.
"We are delighted that the FAA has acknowledged that a pleasant travel experience does not conflict with safety," said Roger Dow, chief executive of the American Travel Association.
The bird was airlifted from a drilling platform in the North Sea and released back into nature
The bird was transported to the coast after it was founded on an oily rig in the Black Sea, and discharged back into the wild.
One rattler was transported by helicopter to Aberdeen last month after it had previously been treated by Scottish company SPCA at the rescue centre until it recovered.
Colin Seddon said: "This rattling probably migrated from northern Europe when strong winds caught it over the North Sea."
Apparently, he'd run out of strength and found refuge on an oil rig.
Mr Seddon added: "He was unable to take off again, so they contacted us with a request for help."
By the time the rattler was discharged, he was in good shape and healthy.
Is the European elite ready for trade with Britain?
The Business for Britain campaign was launched in April with the promise of bringing businesses together and defining what changes in the relationship with the EU they want to see British entrepreneurs and job-creating companies.
To this end, we have commissioned the largest and most comprehensive survey, in which we have asked leading British entrepreneurs about their views on Britain, trade and the EU.
YouGov interviewed 1,000 leading entrepreneurs representing British businesses of various sizes and from different sectors and regions.
The results of the survey will come as a surprise to many.
We have found that the vast majority of businesses are now trying to export outside of Europe and focus on countries that modernise and grow, while EU states are stagnant.
They want to see how the government favors new trade ties with countries such as China, India and Brazil, rather than to bogged down in the long and strenuous process of reforming mysterious European institutions.
When we asked about their views on specific political areas – from monopolistic regulations to product laws – most entrepreneurs expressed the view that controlling these key competences should be returned to Westminster.
There was general dissatisfaction with the single market, and entrepreneurs said that the costs of Brussels regulations now outweigh the advantages of being part of the European business area – 40 percent of large enterprises, traditionally the most pro-European companies, agreed.
Finally, our survey of business leaders revealed the most eloquent knowledge, namely that the unambiguous majority wants Britain to seek to change the treaty and relationship with the EU, which should be based on trade, not politics.
The findings, identified by companies of different sizes and major business groups, show that the business sector is pushing for a “meaningful change” that will bring influence back to Britain.
There's a lot at stake - reaching a treaty change and making a better deal for Britain can make a gain of up to 16 per cent in a referendum to remain in the EU.
The Prime Minister should not hesitate: this survey shows that Britain's trade supports his plan to amend the terms of British EU membership.
It also shows that the business sector expects this amendment to mark a significant shift away from the current balance of forces back towards Britain.
A better deal for Britain is possible and, given how the eurozone is embarking on a path for a narrower economic and fiscal union, increasingly necessary.
The priority must be jobs and growth in Britain, and as the results of our survey show, for entrepreneurship, this means re-focusing on trade and a fundamental change in Brussels's regulatory approach.
John Kerry admitted: Spying has gone too far!
The United States has gone "too far" in spying at times, U.S. diplomacy chief John Kerry admitted during a video conference between Washington and London.
According to the minister, however, the collection of information was used to avert terrorist attacks.
According to AFP, the "first confession from Washington" was heard in the midst of fierce controversy on both sides of the Atlantic over scandalous revelations about mass data surveillance by the U.S. National Security Agency (NSA), the target of which have also become U.S. allies.
"In some cases, I admit, just as President (Barack Obama did), some of these actions have gone too far and we assure that this won't happen in the future," Kerry said.
In the interview, however, he defended the previous practice.
According to him, the collection of information is necessary for the fight against terrorism and the prevention of assassinations, such as the attacks of September 11, 2001 in the U.S
"We have prevented planes from falling, the buildings have exploded and people have been murdered because we have been able to be informed in a timely manner about the plots," Kerry stressed.
However, he again acknowledged that the collection of information "in some cases has gone disproportionately far."
An affair around U.S. intelligence that allegedly tracked hundreds of millions of phone calls erupted in Europe this month.
Among other things, it turned out that the Americans were also tracking the mobile phone of German Chancellor Angela Merkel.
European leaders did not hide outrage, and some of them urged the European Union to try to reach an agreement with the United States that would foresee or significantly restrict surveillance.
Alexei Miller of Gazprom says the pipeline in Bulgaria is launching a new gas era
The opening of the South Stream pipeline in Bulgaria marks the launch of one of Europe's largest energy projects, Gazprom's director said.
"Today we are witnessing a landmark: Construction has begun on the Bulgarian part of the South Stream pipeline, the largest and most important project in Europe," Gazprom President Alexei Miller said in a statement.
This project is a key element of the energy security of the entire European continent.
The South Stream pipeline should add diversity to the routes of Russian exports through Europe.
The contractual dispute between Gazprom and its partners in Ukraine, which brings most Russian gas to Europe, adds to the risk of conventional routes, officials say.
Miller says the direct connection to Bulgaria, a member of the European Union, means that the geopolitical risks associated with transit countries are "forever" eliminated.
Once the entire project launches its operations in 2015, Bulgarian consumers will get gas from the South Stream pipeline at a discounted price.
Gazprom says construction is due to begin in other countries in the direction of the flow at the end of this year.
The pipeline is designed for an annual capacity of 2.2 trillion cubic feet of natural gas.
Hezbollah has sent thousands of militants to Syria.
A decisive battle awaits them.
Lebanon's Hezbollah movement has deployed 15,000 of its fighters north of Damascus in preparation for a decisive battle over the mountainous Kalamun region.
According to ever-clarifying reports, Israel destroyed Hezbollah missiles during Wednesday's airstrikes on two sites in western Syria.
This Lebanese Shiite organization is fighting in Syria on the side of the local army.
Its agenda is also the fight against Israel, which does not meddle in the Syrian conflict, but has made it clear that it will prevent the transfer of armamamence to Hezbollah's hands to Lebanon.
