The SA-8 are obsolete missiles that the Syrian army has had since the 1980s.
They are located on a movable chassis.
Although they are quite primitive, thanks to mobility and small dimensions, they can be easily concealed.
They are equipped with radar, but switched off after firing a missile, so it is difficult to target them.
Latakiah lies in western Syria, an area inhabited by ruling Alawites.
It is a well-guarded area and Hezbollah has been involved in fighting in western Syria in the past.
It says it is helping to protect Syria's Shiite community, to which Alawites belongs.
Now reportedly 15 000 of his fighters are ready to help the army back Kalamun.
Along with Hezbollah, the Syrian army is also strengthening its troops there.
Kalamun lies between Damascus and the country's third-largest city, Homs.
According to an al-Arabian source, the battle will be retaliation for the opposition's recent attack on the headquarters of Abu Fadl Abbas' Syrian Shiite Brigade in the Shiite part of Damascus.
Hezbollah helped the Syrian army this year to win back the city of Kusair near the Lebanese border.
Many Kusair rebels have just gone to Kalamun.
