The Royal Commission learned that two employees of the Christian Young Men's Association (YMCA) had been charged prior to lawsuits against Jonathan Lord
The Royal Commission on Child Sexual Abuse has learned that before allegations against child educator Jonathan Lord of Caringbah in 2011, two employees of the Christian Young Men Association (YMCA) in New South Wales were indicted for child sexual abuse.
But it was said before the commission that in its initial statement to the commission, the association said they had never dealt with a case of sexual abuse of a child in their organization.
Executive Director Phillip Hare was asked about a case where one of YMCA's employees was charged with misdemeanors related to child pornography, and another when a gym teacher at Caringbah from the YMCA was sentenced in 1991 for sexually molesting children he had in his care.
Mr. Hare told Gaili Furness, the commission's adviser, that he knew about the first case, but he didn't.
He admitted that YMCA's introductory statement was also inaccurate, stating that external audits were carried out at YMCA, appreciating the organisation as a leading advocate of children's rights.
Evidence submitted by the commission indicates that YMCA has been notified that it received the second lowest of four possible evaluations in an audit by the Ministry of Education and Society of August this year.
Mr. Hare, who began working with YMCA when he was 21, acknowledged that the management had failed from him alone when he accepted Mr Lord without making sure that the staff were aware of the obligation to report violations of children's safety rules.
Earlier this year, Lord was convicted of sexual harassing by 12 boys he committed during his two years working at the YMCA association.
He's been jailed for at least six years.
Mr. Hare, however, dismissed claims that YMCA had a cultural problem that prevented the staff from reporting child safety breaches by Lord.
Staff members gave evidence that they witnessed such violations, including that the Lord was alone with the children, guarding them privately, letting them sit on their laps, telling them he liked them, and letting them play with his mobile phone.
Danielle Ockwell, who was Lord's subordinate and asked for child safety training because she was concerned about his behavior, testified that Jacqui Barnat, head of services to children at YMCA in Caringbah, who was Lord's supervisor, was "very intimidating and often had a hard time talking to him about something."
The chief executive said he did not accept evidence from the staff that they felt uneasy if they were to contact their supervisors and report the deeds.
Instead, he said that their friendship with the Lord obscured their judgment and they did not report the problem.
Mr Hare said he had communicated to the YMCA board in New South Wales his view that lessons from Jonathan Lord's "case" for the organisation was that the staff "did not give a report," and the council agreed with him in doing so.
Mr. Hare said the decision to let the staff sign the confidentiality agreements shortly after allegations surfaced were made by the general manager for services to children at YMCA Liam Whitley.
He said the goal was to avoid breaching evidence, but it was "overably zealous" and poorly executed.
Professor Stephen Smallbone of Griffith University told the commission that the YMCA in New South Wales was not an organisation safe for children between 2009 and 2011.
He said there were serious problems in recruiting, vetting, incorporating, training and conducting personnel.
Hearing was adjourned to December 20.
Tony Blair said he would take the chance to return to the post of prime minister, but admitted the return was unlikely.
In a late-nature interview to mark the fifth anniversary of his departure from office, a 59-year-old politician expressed his views on various areas of domestic politics.
