When asked if he would return to the post of Prime Minister, his reply was quoted by the London-based newspaper Evening Standard: “Yes, of course, but that’s probably hard to come by, right, so...”
While there were crowds of horse-drawing experts gathering in Cardiff to fight for the Horse of the Year Award, it was clear to everyone that the competition wouldn't be easy.
But no one was ready for three-year-old Fenton Kirkland.
Although he doesn't go to school yet and just a few months ago he took his first steps, this little boy and his pet, Shetland pony Toffee, trotted through three rounds smoothly to eventually win the top prize and leave behind 30 protracted adult rivals.
This inseparable pair – with the same height – was praised for their appearance, behaviour and style at an annual competition run by Sunnybank’s equestrian centre in the village of Rudry near Cardiff.
He confronted the masters and ladies in elegant hats, tilted his beret to a cheerful angle, and let his two-year-old Toffee promenade around the circuit.
Fenton was praised by the judges for his natural ability to control the horse, which far outweighs his age.
And Toffee earned the highest marks for insight and personality.
Fenton got Toffee in March for his third birthday and has been practicing daily with this Shetland pony ever since.
His mother, Donna (30), said, "Fenton and Toffee are working well together."
They didn't have high hopes, but in the end they left with a golden cup and bouquet.
It was only the second time he competed with Toffeo, and when he won, we were thrilled.
Everyone considered him phenomenal, and complete strangers wanted to take a picture with him.
A boy from the village of Nantyglo near Ebbw Vale in South Wales follows in the footsteps of his aunt Sharon Howells, who has been showcasing horses for more than 10 years.
Ms Howells says: "There was an exciting atmosphere and everyone cheered and applaused."
He ran along the sand along the length of the arena, and although he seemed so small, he gave a great performance.
Fenton is obsessed with animals – he loves horses, tractors and a farm and was in charge of two chickens.
Considering how he started, he'll be back at the Horse of the Year competition again, and I'm sure he'll do well.
A spokesperson for the annual horse-drafting competition said: "Fentons are only three, but they already know how to control the pony."
They form a great team together.
The jurors picked up Fenton and Toffee for their outstanding speech and for the way they performed on the competition circuit.
They're always watching how good the team's work is between the pony and whoever's leading it - and Fenton and Toffeo were the best on the oval.
I'm sure Fenton's adorable clothes have helped, he'll really fit in with him.
Too inquisitive newspapers are to be investigated
The Chinese newspaper, which on the front page asked for the dismissal of a reporter accused of defamation, will be thoroughly investigated, the administrator told the press.
The New Express newspapers, based in Guangzhou, have made a rare public request for the release of journalist Chen Yongzhou.
However, Mr. Chen later admitted on television that he was taking bribes to make up stories about a society partly owned by the state.
The administrator said the New Express newspaper would now make a "full correction."
The "remedial" regulation came from the State Administration for Press and Publications, radio, film and television in Guangdong Province.
A preliminary investigation showed that between September 2012 and August 2013, a New Express newspaper from Yangcheng's Evening News group released several false reports on the aforementioned Zoomlion.
"The editorial guidance of the New Express newspaper has been disordered," the administrator said in a statement.
He said he had to decide to "impose Chen Yongzhou's administrative punishment in the form of abolishing the journalist's license."
He also "ordered the yangcheng group Evening News to make an overall correction to the New Express, and recommended that they investigate the relevant those responsible in the New Express and immediately change the management of the New Express newspaper."
Mr. Chen has written several articles for the New Express concerning financial irregularities at a company with construction equipment called Zoomlion.
After being detained, his newspaper issued two requests for his release on the front page, claiming he was behind his journalistic work.
However, Mr. Chen appeared on state television and confessed to giving untrue stories for payment.
"In this case, I caused damage to Zoomlion and also to the entire media industry and its ability to gain the public's trust," he told state-run CCTV news station.
I did it mainly because I craved money and fame.
I realize I've acted poorly.
After Mr Chen's apology, the New Express issued an apology on the front page, stating that he had failed when he did not carry out sufficient scrutiny of his reports.
More recently, several high-profiled suspects have made confessions on television.
Experts say confessions are still regularly enforced, regardless of a change in this year's law that prohibits authorities from forcing anyone to blame themselves.
Drug smuggling tunnel between U.S. and Mexico with its own railroad was found
It managed to discover one of the most ingenious drug smuggling tunnels between the U.S. and Mexico, complemented by its own lighting, ventilation and electric rail.
U.S
The tunnel, which winds into the length of nearly six football fields, connects warehouses near Tijuana, Mexico and San Diego, USA.
This area is full of indeterminate warehouses, where it is easy to hide trucks before being loaded with drugs.
Authorities said the tunnel was closed before drugs may have been undetected.
Along with the discovery of the tunnel, authorities also detained eight and a half tons of marijuana and about 150 kilograms of cocaine, according to judicial records.
Three men who, according to authorities, worked as drivers, have been charged with possession of marijuana and cocaine in order to distribute them.
If convicted, he faces prison sentences ranging from 10 years to life imprisonment.
In Nogales, Arizona, smugglers penetrate vast drainage channels.
Tunnel is the eighth-largest corridor discovered in San Diego since 2006.
