The researchers explained more about how tail movements were related to the dog's mood
Previous research has shown that joyful dogs wag their tail more to the right (from the dog's point of view), while nervous dogs wave their tail mostly to the left.
Now, however, scientists are saying that other dogs are able to notice these subtle differences in the tail movement and respond to them.
Professor Georgio Vallortigara, a neurobiologist at the University of Trento, said: "In humans, it is well known that the left and right sides of the brain have a different link to stimuli that arouse positive or negative emotions."
We tried to observe the same in other species.
He added that, as is the case in humans, in dogs, the right side of the brain is responsible for left-handed movements and vice versa, and that these two hemispheres play a different role in emotions.
In an effort to get more information about how dogs react to the disproportionate wagging of other dogs' tails, researchers monitored animals that watched movies about other dogs.
They measured the heart rate of these animals and analyzed their behavior.
It probably won't be long before we understand why their tail sometimes goes to one side, and other times to the other.
Professor Vallortigara said: "We've screened dog films about dogs - either in the natural version or in the form of silhouette - to get rid of any confusing stimuli, and we've also been able to adjust the tail's movement and illustrate it more to the right or left."
When the animals saw an otherwise bland dog moving its tail to the right (from the point of view of a dog wagging its tail), it remained completely calm.
But when she noticed filming the tail mostly to the left (again from the point of view of a dog wagging its tail), their heart rate increased and the animals seemed insecure.
Professor Vallortigara said he didn't think dogs would consciously communicate with each other through these movements.
Rather, he believes that dogs from experience have learned which movements they have to have and do not have to look out for.
He said: "If you encounter dogs several times and the movement of their tail in one direction is repeatedly associated with rather friendly behavior, and the movement of traffic breeds less friendly behavior, you will respond based on this experience."
The researchers say the findings can help dog owners, veterinarians and trainers better understand their animals' emotions.
Dog Behavior Expert John Bradshaw, a veterinarian at the University of Bristol, said this wasn't the first study to examine whether the left and right-hand side were important to dogs.
Last year, a team from Lincoln University discovered that dogs avert their head to the left as they observe an aggressive dog, and on the right as they look at a hilarious dog.
And in another expert treatise from the University of Victoria in Canada, he said: “Dogs were more likely to be familiar with the dog’s robot when it wagged its tail to the left than to the right, when they were becoming more precarious – exactly the opposite of an Italian study.”
According to him, the difference can be due to the fact that dogs in these various studios did not treat animals in movies or dog robots completely like dogs.
Explained that a study on how dogs react to real dogs could help.
“While in the case of many different mammals there are strong evidence that both sides of the brain are used for different purposes, many details still need to be clarified – and dogs are no exception,” he said.
But given how easy it is to record their behavior, it probably won't be long before we understand why their tail sometimes goes to one side and, other times.
Arctic Monkeys postpone Glasgow concert due to Alex Turner's illness
Rock band Arctic Monkeys moved a concert in Glasgow after the lead singer was diagnosed with inflammation of the larynx.
The Sheffield group was due to perform Friday at City's Hydro complex.
However, Alex Turner’s lead singer’s illness forced her to move the performance.
The group's announcement came after it was similarly forced to postpone Thursday's appearance at the LG arena in Birmingham.
In a statement on their official website, Arctic Monkeys says: "After the decision to postpone today's performance at the LG arena in Birmingham and based on a medical recommendation, the Arctic Monkeys group must also postpone the performance at the Hydro complex in Glasgow planned for Friday, November 1."
“Alex Turner has been diagnosed with laryngeal inflammation and unfortunately he won’t be able to perform.”
Performances at the LG arena in Birmingham will take place on November 20 and performances at the Hydro complex in Glasgow on November 21.
All tickets for these speeches remain valid.
We'd like to apologise to all those who bought tickets for the inconveniences it's caused them.
If you need further information, please contact customer service at the ticket office where you bought your tickets.
Pope Francis appoints first cardinals in February
On Thursday, the Vatican announced that Pope Francis would appoint new cardinals of the Catholic Church for the first time on February 22.
Cardinals have the highest position in the clergy of the Catholic Church right after the pope and are those who elect the pope, so Francis appoints his first group of men, who will eventually help choose his successor.
There are now 201 cardinals.
However, once the cardinal reaches the age of 80, he can no longer take part in the election of the pope – the group thus shrinks to 120 electant cardinals.
In a statement announcing this report, Father Federico Lombardi, a spokesman for the Vatican, said that before the ceremonial promotion of the new cardinals, a gathering of current cardinals known as the constorium would be held.
"Pope Francis has decided to communicate his decision to convene the February consistory in advance in order to facilitate the planning of other gatherings that require the participation of cardinals from different parts of the world," Lombardi said.
Jack Valero of Catholic Voices said the number of electing cardinals is likely to fall by February.
He said that the pope usually appoints as many cardinals as it needs to increase the number of electors again to 120, and as many cardinals exceeding the age of 80 as he wants.
Consistorium next year will be significant because it will be the first after Francis was elected in March this year, Valero said.
At the moment, there is some focus on Europe Europe and, above all, Italy.
“It will be interesting to see if the new pope nominates cardinals from the rest of the world to restore balance,” he said.
Forty percent of Roman Catholics are in South America, but have only a slight number of cardinals.
It will also be the first choice of cardinals since Francis established the Council of Cardinals, a group of eight cardinals from around the world, tasked with finding ways to reform the Church.
In the past, the pope decided everything for himself.
“Now Francis has chosen these eight cardinals to help him,” Valero said.
He said it was "quitely possible" that these cardinals the pope would ask for advice.
But we haven’t been in this situation before – it’s absolutely new.
Valero said popes have traditionally exalted bishops from great places as cardinals, but Francis is "full of surprises - so we don't know who he's appointing."
GM withdraws some new U.S. light trucks to repair seat backrests
According to Friday's announcement from the U.S. auto safety authority, General Motors is withdrawing nearly 19,000 of its new (2014) Chevrolet Silverado and GMC Sierra light trucks to fix a malfunction with a manual tilting backrest.
For some of these vehicles, the tilting mechanism of the front seats may have a malfunction.
As a result, seat backrests aren't consistent with federal auto safety standards for head restraints.
Notifications placed on the website of the State Transport Safety Administration (NHTSA) say that "if the car gets an impact from behind, the headrest may not protect passengers properly, which increases the risk of injury."
Downloaded models were made between August 1 and September 10.
GM launched the delivery in June, and for this leading U.S. automaker, this is the most important launch of the vehicle from restructuring to bankruptcy in 2009.
GM has informed the owner of the van about the malfunction in the first half of October.
NHTSA was unable to review announcements to owners because of the government's 16-day malfunction, which weakened car sales growth in October.
Sales of Silverado and Sierra shipments, which have been redesigned for models in 2014, have risened to about 20 percent in the first 10 months of this year, GM said on Friday.
In October, GM selled 42,660 Silverado models and 16,503 Sierra models.
Shares on the New York Stock Exchange rose 1.4 per cent to US$37.47 on Friday afternoon.
Desperate scream by Obama's constituent
I voted for Obama twice – I shared the hope of a possible change
He says Obama's sincere efforts thwarted obstruction of the Republican Party
Obstructive policy can't excuse the woes of Obama's health care websites or drone strikes
Obama's 2008 memorium is a sad reminder of what might have happened
Nathaniel P. Morris is studying medicine at Harvard for the second year.
I've been reading a very sad book these days.
About this book, I thought that during the grim days of my second year at medical school, I would be encouraged and renewed in me with a pinch of hope.
It's called "The Audacity to Win," and it's memories of Barack Obama's 2008 presidential campaign.
In the evening, when I finish the assessments of the patients and go to bed, the book brings me back to a time when politics has inspired millions of people and your speeches have taken your breath away.
The election ended with an overwhelming superiority, and the television news forced us to reflect on the historical value of this moment.
My classmates wept with joy, and my parents kepted the newspaper they could find.
A young team of visionaries headed to the White House, and the nation was ready for change.
During taken into office in 2008, Obama had 82 percent support.
And this is where I'm closing the book.
Going today is a harsh awakening, like waking away from a dream.
It’s hard to be reminded of these optimistic days – they seem to be distant memories, a sad reminder of missed opportunities.
There have indeed been changes in the years after I dropped my first ballot.
It was definitely none of what I had imagined.
I recognize that Obama has achieved a number of great and diverse achievements – from the Affordable Care Act to the expulsion of our troops from Iraq and the end of politics “not to ask questions, not to say anything” to Osama bin Laden.
Moreover, I am convinced that party obstructions have reversed so many efforts to move our nation forward: among other things it is immigration reform, the possibility for the public to choose health care and the closure of a base in Guantánamo.
But after many situations where I have defended the Obama administration in front of colleagues and pals, in terms of the explanations they can provide, I have reached an absolute limit.
I found myself in a place of political hopelessness.
Obstructions of Republicans cannot explain consent to eavesdropping on foreign leaders or to airstrike drones that kill innocent children overseas.
It cannot be explained that the National Security Agency collects data on the private lives of Americans, nor that it persecutes those who will reveal the misconduct of the government.
They cannot be held accountable for the execution of Anwar al-Awlaki, a U.S. citizen, without a proper trial, or for curtailing public contributions and spending during presidential campaigns.
They can't justify the findings of a report saying the White House's efforts to silence the media are "the most aggressive ... from the Nixon administration."
And most recently, they can’t even excuse the inability to create more than 3 years after signinging the law on the available care of a simple website.
I don't know if something like this could have been expected.
Whether at the age of 18 I should have guessed that government power could be contrary to the political campaigns that preceded it.
The elected office obviously has no predictable course, and our public talks will shape an opposition political party and random events such as the Newton massacre.
However, all the examples I have given above appear to a large extent to be the result of the administration's own decision.
And that bothers me the most.
I elected Obama again in 2012, but not because I was thrilled with his candidacy.
Mitt Romney was a confusing and ungrinded alternative, and he seemed unable to consolidate his politics or position.
I had the feeling that a second term for Obama, freed from the pressure of the next election, would fulfill the hopes we had once heard about.
Still, the return to 2008 – as Obama’s support fell below 45% this week – is getting harder through this book.
It makes me remember a number of vows that have faded.
This week, I read a chapter in the book about how Obama suffered a crushing defeat with Clinton during the Pennsylvania primaries.
At an additional meeting during the campaign, he told his staff that he had to get back on pace and remain true to his mission.
“I want our magic back,” he said.
Let’s not forget who we are.
Five years have passed, Mr. President, and I totally agree with you.
The ideas expressed in this commentary are exclusively the views of Nathaniel Morris.
Clive Palmer argues that Prime Minister Tony Abbott is in a conflict of interest because of the parental leave programme
Billioner and MP Clive Palmer says Prime Minister Tony Abbott is in conflict of interest because of the parental leave program because his daughters can get pregnant and benefit from it.
The mining tycoon, who is waging a 6-million-dollar dispute over the payment of coal, made the claim as he tried to sweep off the table the question of whether he was in a conflict of interest.
Palmer's United Party can control up to four votes in the Senate, which play a crucial role in deciding whether coal taxes and mining activities should be levied.
But Mr Palmer argued that only ministers could be in a conflict of interest, adding that Abbott's daughters were in a position where they could have had a personal benefit from the policy.
"He's got into a big conflict of interest when it comes to paid parental leave, because if any of his daughters got pregnant, he'd be directly interested in whether she would get parental leave," Mr. Palmer said.
Two months after the election of the Electoral Commission, after the vote was converted, it officially declared Mr. Palmer the winner in the fight for the Fairfax district in the Sunshine Coast district thanks to a gain of 53 votes.
Mr Palmer called for a review of the election results to speed up the process.
Daughters of Tony Abbotta Frances and Bridget.
Should this election be decided two months after the polling stations close?
“We have to have a better system,” he said.
Why can’t we have a system where you can come, tap the details into your computer, vote immediately, and have results at half-past seven in the evening?
Mr Palmer has also criticised the use of pencils to designate ballots.
Is it that the results can be erased if someone doesn't?
In these days and at this time, the use of pencils seems unbelievable.
The Electoral Commission studied the possibilities of electronic voting and recently issued a joint contribution together with New Zealand to the discussion.
Mr Palmer (59) said that his political agenda also applies to Sunshine Coast airports and that he would take his new job "very seriously."
The public office is a service to the public.
“We do not strive for any reward except that we can serve society at this critical time,” he said.
Do you wash your hands?
Scientists have discovered that washing your hands will change your mood.
The experiment was attended by 98 people, divided into three groups.
The first two were tasked with solving an unsolvable task.
Of course, they both failed.
But the group, which washed its hands after the failure, had a much more optimistic impression than the one where its participants did not wash their hands.
The "washed hands" group also did better in the next test than the latter.
Nevertheless, those who washed their hands achieved worse results in other tests than those who did not wash them.
The results of the "washed" were at the level of the third group, whose participants did not record any previous failure.
According to psychologists, the results of the test indicate that physical purity removes really negative sensations after failure.
At the same time, however, the motivation decreases, the testers have to concentrate much more in further tests.
If we transferred the results of the research to Czechs, we would probably be the most optimistic after returning from the toilet.
After using the toilet, only one percent of Czechs and Czechs do not wash their hands, but it is already one-fifth along the way by public transport.
Hygiene is usually clinged also after work in the garden or in the workshop, when 85 percent of people wash their hands.
Caressing a peat motivates 78.7 percent of people to wash their hands.
A recent survey revealed that there is a difference in the perception of Czechs and Slovaks, to what extent it is necessary to wash your hands after shaking hands with a stranger.
In this situation, 28.8 percent of Czechs and 40.3 percent of Slovaks wash their hands.
The survey showed that both nations wash their hands about ten times a day.
"While it'd be appropriate, I'm afraid the reality is different and people are trying to do a bit better," commented the results of the survey skin doctor Klaudie Komárková.
RBS suspends two foreign currency traders
According to two people familiar with the situation, the Scottish Royal Bank (RBS) suspends two traders in its foreign currency division, which is another indication that a global investigation by control authorities regarding suspicious machinations with the currency market is becoming increasingly common.
Some of the world's largest banks, including UBS, Barclays, Deutsche Bank and RBS, have confirmed that they are collaborating with competent authorities on investigations into the world's largest financial markets, where they are converting US$5.3 trillion each day.
Two traders would be RBS's first employees suspended as part of an expanding investigation, which is a response to the LIBOR (London Interbank Interest Rate) scandal.
The bank declined to comment on the suspension but confirmed it had received a request for information from authorities this month.
"Our unfailing search on the matter continues, and we are fully cooperating with FCA and our other authorities," the bank said two weeks ago.
Last month, people who are close to the event said RBS handed over to the British authority of financial behaviour records of e-mails and instant messages sent from the accounts of the former trader.
This trader, Richard Usher, left RBS in 2010 and is believed to have been released from his current position as European foreign currency trading chairman with JPMorgan.
Rohan Ramchandani, Europe's chairman of trading for ready-to-money in Citi, went on holiday this week, while Matt Gardiner, a former head of currency trading at Barclays and UBS, was suspended by Standard Chartered this week.
Neither of these merchants has been charged with any offense.
Mr Usher's instant messaging group included bankers from Barclays and Citigroup, people initiated into the event said.
UBS said this week that it had taken action against some of its employees after the Swiss inspection body Finma announced it was investigating suspicious machinations in the foreign currency market in several Swiss banks.
Worldwide, at least six offices – the European Commission, Finma, the Swiss Antitrust Office Weko, FCA, the U.S. Department of Justice and the Hong Kong Monetary Office – investigate claims that these bankers have come together to move the currency market.
People familiar with the situation said HSBC, Citigroup, JPMorgan and Credit Suisse additionally launched an internal investigation or received a request for information from the competent authorities.
Banks are combing with years-old quick messages and emails to find instances of some offenses.
Reports of investigations alarmed traders in an area that was one of the largest profit-generating engines in investment bank closures in recent years, but which has been disrupted by the fact that small currency instability limited opportunities for speculators.
Some bankers tried to reduce the gravity of the situation when they argued that it was essentially impossible to tamper with a vast and high-liquid foreign currency market, but high-ranking traders say it wasn't necessarily true.
A high-ranking trader has said that despite the significant volume of daily trading with a foreign currency, fragmentation of liquidity between different trading platforms and banks increasing the use of their own internal platforms means that "you may start to have an impact on the market at fairly low prices."
The report came the same day Credit Suisse announced this week it dismissed a trader from its share trading department, which caused a loss of nearly US$6 million at the end of last year.
The bank immediately alerted the competent authorities and cooperates with their supervisory authorities.
"We are convinced that this trader acted alone and that this issue has been resolved," Credit Suisse said.
The number of HIV infections in Prague has exceeded 1,000
Doctors in Prague have registered more than 1,000 people with HIV.
From the beginning of monitoring in 1986 to the end of this year's third quarter, tests confirmed the infection in a total of 1005 people from the metropolis.
This is according to the statistics of the National Reference Laboratory for AIDS of the State Health Institute.
According to her, there have been 2 063 infected people across the Czech Republic so far.
In 396, immunity failure disease has already erupted.
198 people died.
The number of people infected in the Czech Republic has been increasing every year in the last decade.
In 2002, tests confirmed the infection in 50 people, five years later there were already 121.
In 2009 the examination revealed 156 new infections and in 2010 180.
Last year the number dropped to 153, but last year was a record.
According to the lab, there were 212.
"In my opinion, there is no doubt that the total number of newly diagnosed cases this year exceeds 200," says Miroslav Hlavatý, an expert on the issue of AIDS, who for years led Prague's House of Light for Patients.
From October to the end of the year in 2011, tests revealed the infection in 39 people, and last year at 47, Hlavatý said.
In September, doctors registered 21 new patients and three patients.
From the beginning of the follow-up to the end of September, HIV tests confirmed in 1710 men and 353 women.
An AIDS outbreak in 161 men and 37 women.
The most infected were in Prague.
If you really want to get to know a city, you have to go to the cemetery.
His visit reveals more than a walk through a packed center with monuments, churches and galleries, more than he would have divulged by bedeker.
Many people "read from graves."
Slowly, cemeteries roam and gain an intimate insight into local reality.
They find out what kind of people live here, what nationalities, what are their beliefs, how large and cohesive families belong, at what age they die here, how wealthy they are, how important their titles and professions were to them.
How they express their social status, their taste.
And from the condition of the graves, they will also find out how the bereaved take care of their dead.
Supreme Court backs Obama's health care bill
The U.S. U.S. Supreme Court has ruled that a health care law signed by Barack Obama is in line with the constitution, a major victory for the Obama administration.
By voice 5:4, the court ruled that an individual regulation of the Patient Protection and Affordability Act – ordering citizens to purchase health insurance by 2014, otherwise they are at risk of paying a fine – does not contradict the Constitution on the basis of government tax authority.
Court President John Roberts stood on the sidege of four more liberal members of the court, while Judges Scalia, Thomas, Alito and Kennedy were in opposition.
The court also supported the remainder parts of the 2700-page bill and further ruled that the legal requirement for health care, which orders states to extend the possibility of obtaining sickness insurance, otherwise there is a risk of losing all funding for sickness insurance from federal sources, not coercive and in violation of the Constitution.
The lawsuit was filed by 26 states and the National Federation of Independent Enterprises in order to block this Act.
During the 2012 primaries, the bill emphatically rejected each major Republican nominee, including presumed proposed candidate Mitt Romney.
There's no danger of jail for "Mugabe is a limp donkey", the court overturned the law
Until now, Zimbabweans have had to pay close attention to talking in public about the eighty-nine-year-old Mugabe, who has ruled the country since the early 1980s, and since the end of 1987.
According to Section 33 of the Criminal Code, a fine of 100 dollars to one year of imprisonment was threatened for disregarding the presidency.
"State prosecutors should not zealously accuse people who comment on the president in pubs or in other public places," representatives of the Constitutional Court said.
Nine judges unanimously agreed that the law undermines freedom of expression.
It is likely that the government will appeal against the court's decision, Britain's BBC reported.
A number of damaged Zimbabwans, among them Tendai Danga, who was arrested by police two years ago for allegedly insulting the president in an argument with a bar police officer, were loudly opposed to the law.
In May, an opposition activist in prison, frequenting Mugabe's swoll of "limp donkey," ended up in jail.
In August after, the prosecutor charged a twenty-six-year-old man because of his intention of using a poster with Robert Mugabe's face as toilet paper.
The court also declared it an unconstitutional law restricting the freedom of the media.
"The state cannot punish people whose statements later prove untrue," Deputy Justice Minister Luke Malala said.
Until now, Zimbabaw law has allowed a person who publishes false information to be sentenced to twenty years in prison.
"The existence of a law to criminalize someone as a false statement has a freezing effect on the right to express oneself freely," Malala added.
Despite Mugabe's ruling the country with a hard hand for more than 33 years, Zimbabwe adopted a new constitution with wider civic freedoms on the basis of a referendum in March.
She took small steps in the direction of the one-man country's dictatorship in 2009, when an agreement between Mugabo and the opposition carried Morgan Tsvangiraj into the prime minister's seat.
However, the country continues to be plagued by the economic crisis and the highest inflation in the world until 2008, which exceeded 100 000 percent.
Therefore, in 2009, the government completely abolished the national currency.
Today, in Zimbabwe, only the currencies of other states are paid.
An impoverished economy is at the expense of Mugabe's inconsiderate agricultural reform.
In 2000, he redistributed the land of white farmers among his supporters and war veterans.
However, most of them had no experience with running farms.
Central banks will keep swap lines permanently
Temporary and bilateral agreements between the European Central Bank (ECB), the U.S. Fed, the Bank of England and the central banks of Canada, Switzerland and Japan will become a permanent tool that will give banks access to global currencies whenever they need.
Banks said this in their joint statement.
"It's a very sensible move," commented Julian Callow, chief international economist at London-barclays.
"So far, all transitional instruments of this type have gradually become permanent, so it is, in my opinion, no surprise," adds Vojtěch Benda, chief economist at BH Securities.
Central bankers reinforce the bonds, which began to emerge in 2007, when the international credit market first stalled.
The central bank's agreements have allowed access to euros, dollars and other currencies to anyone who needed them.
The decision by central bankers comes at a time when the U.S. Fed is preparing to end its U.S. economic support program, which could drive out the price of money in the global market even higher.
By default, a swap contract is used to overcome a lack of liquidity of one currency in the current liquidity surplus of another currency.
It can be understood as a deposit of one currency and the borrowing of the currency of another.
The ECB is launching its third attempt to return confidence in European banks.
The EU has agreed on banking overseeing, the ECB will oversee 150 banks.
How Tipsport (not) deletes personal data.
More than a year ago, I registered on Tipsport.cz.
However, I did not finish it, because there was someone at the branch who could not complete the registration from the Internet.
And because I didn't want to go to another branch, the registration fell through, then I moved, and thanks to that, my address and ID card number changed.
When I wanted to complete the registration later, I was told that it was not possible, because I have a different OP number, address and that I simply cannot complete it and that I should request the deletion of the data and make a new registration.
Therefore, I did this and 24.10.2013 I wrote on technical support that I am requesting the deletion of all my personal data from the database according to the applicable data protection law.
On this, a long-daily technical support wrote to me that I should complete the registration at the branch.
In doing so, a marathon of e-mails for technical support began, where they finally, after the 4th urgency of 25.10., wrote that they deleted the personal data at my request.
Therefore, one would think that he could perform the new registration and here the problems first started to tell me that my username is already occupied (it has not been deleted - it is not personal data, but in the end they eliminated this).
So I used a different username and it jumped to me that my personal data has already been stored in order to complete the registration at the branch (how they may have stored something they claim to have erased?), so I contacted technical support again.
When asked how it was possible, they wrote me off that personal data had actually been deleted on 25.10. and to send an e-mail with that error.
I did this (about 15 o'clock yesterday) and they wrote to me today that I registered the same day (311.2013) at 13.22 and that's why I can't, because I used the same name and date of birth.
What about the fact that I have been contacting technical support for a week and dealing with them that it is still not possible.
And what about the fact that if it were true what it claims (as if it is not) no one could register with the same name or the same date of birth if this is already registered.
Just by the way in those 13.22 I actually registered, to the same name and date of birth, but I used a fake e-mail and a phone number and a world of wonder, a page appeared to me that they sent me a link to my e-mail to complete the registration.
But how is it possible if you can’t register for the same name and date of birth?
It’s probably because Tipsport ties registration to an e-mail and just doesn’t disable the phone number and the data.
So I have again communicated this to the technical support that they are simply lying (either intentionally or they don't know what they're writing), because what they're writing isn't true.
I've documented a few screens that the problem with registration was already before 31.10. so "false" registration simply does not affect this.
Tipsport wrote me an austere sentence for this - I'm sorry, but because you're registered again, we can't check your statement.
And he ended the communication.
Respectively, it repeats the same sentence that the registration binds to the name and date of birth.
They can't even accept the fact that the registration wasn't completed by clicking on that link in the e-mail, so it didn't even happen.
Therefore, if I summarize, Tipsport not only didn’t delete the data as it claims, but is still lying when it says otherwise.
Even worse is that it is easy to prove how they lie and try to make it seem that the fault is on the part of the customer.
By the way, after presenting this matter on my personal blog, things started to move a bit, and from the Technical Support came an e-mail that they are handing over the matter for verification and I will be informed (after a week of urgencies and arguing and warning of the same error).
