Secret wars: Syria, Libya and Iran.
Training for Obama on how to bomb Syria.
U.S. commandos in Libya.
TOP GUN in the Gulf.
The Israelis have demonstrated exactly what the bombing of Syria is supposed to look like.
Without unnecessary hype, threats and millions of declarations, they sent planes, which hit the target and returned.
Without being lulled by the official removal of chemical weapons in Syria.
They refuse to confirm the whole action and no longer comment on it.
Precisely the opposite of confused and big-mouthed, actions unsupprised by Obama's Syrian policies.
And the most satisfying thing about the whole attack was what the Israelis bombed.
Don't be fooled by the media by their claims that a "Syrian base" or a "Hezbolah van" has been bombed.
I recently wrote here about the fact that while U.S. assistance to secular rebels in the country remains only on a rhetorical level, Russia cheerfully continues to supply weapons to Syria.
And it was the fresh delivery of Russian missiles unloaded in the port of Latakiya that became the target of an Israeli airstrike.
A clear message.
And note, Israel once again bombed the Assad's regime, not insurgents, although according to popular internet theories it should be the other way around.
The U.S. government has honored 2 members of spacial units, probably the Delta Force.
They had no idea that they had distinguished themselves during an attack on the U.S. Consulate in Bengazi.
It is known the heroic death of two former SEAL members who defended the CIA campus in the city, in my opinion they were members of the secret CIA commandos, which are made up of former soldiers.
For a long time, there has been speculation about what the SEALs were doing at the consulate or even in a elsewhere located CIA residence.
It's clearer now.
In any case, the information that the CIA has its machine-gun nest-protected offices in Libya (just on one of such both SEALs perished after being hit by a mortar) is quite surprising.
But even more surprising is the information about those two previously unknown heroes.
They were parts of a U.S. commandos in Tripoli, and as soon as they got a report of an attack in Bengazi, they volunteered and, together with five private contractors, shot their way into the CIA compound in Bengazia.
The Obama administration is blunted by the fact that during that attack, the commandos were not deployed officially and completely, and that they simply hesitated what to do.
The commander of the squad, without orders, will probably release these volunteers.
In any case, the fact that there is not a one-off in the country, but permanently U.S. commandos, CIA agents and private contractors is quite interesting information.
This is due to the fact that until recently nothing like this has been officially acknowledged, and even now there is only talk of a kind of NATO advisory mission in Libya.
But it is a good thing that the U.S., although half-hearted, is trying to combat terrorism in Libya.
Although to solve the nationwide situation, it would take much greater help.
The last place in a secret and permanent conflict I want to write about today is the coast of Iran.
Not so long ago, the media almost praised the Iranian Air Force for prosecuting U.S. drones.
Little is known, however, how Americans effectively protect their drones.
Of course, if you happen to be able to discover your drone in airspace, there's nothing you can do about it.
However, if a drone is discovered by Iranians in an international space, the situation is quite different.
And so it happened that the Iranian F-4 Phantom chased an U.S
He tricked Phantom to visually verify his armaments, without Phantom registering him on the radar or simply noticing him, and then when he suddenly appeared with him and challenged the Iranian pilot to return home, he listened without grumbling.
Not surprisingly, Phantom is an American-made jet that remembers the war in Vietnam.
He can't compare to Raptor.
We want to show that Pilsen still knows how to win, Rajtoral promises
It's kind of a paradox.
While his team has not been doing well recently, defender or midfielder Viktorie Plzeň František Rajtoral takes the form that earned him a return to the national team after a year.
He is also doing well in shooting, he is full of activity, after the procedure he was ordered a penalty kick in a match in Teplice.
