The team carefully prepares for a difficult match with the North Bohemians, who play offensive football.
Everything is heading towards a match with Jablonec, the coach prepares us well, as always we draw a lot also from the video analysis of the opponent's game.
"I believe it will be an excellent match on Friday night," the Czech representative invites fans to Doosan Arena's stands.
Pilsen's last matches didn't work out the way she'd like, but defender Victoria refuses the word crisis.
That's the way it is in football, sometimes you have good form and sometimes worse.
But I wouldn't talk about the crisis in any way.
"I believe that we will win on Friday and it will be good again," the defender said.
The Victoria team will once again rely on the support of the fans.
"It's played Friday night and I'd like to invite our fans, who are great."
"I believe we'll repay them by winning," Rajtoral adds.
Jana Péťová: Step back.
So I decided to take a step back, but when I think about it, it’s actually not a step at all, but a big jump – a leap a few years back, to the memories, to my childhood.
It's a memory of what it was like not having any worries and just coming home from school and washing dishes, vacuuming and doing your homework.
Although these "duties", it was mostly just a theory, the wishes of the parents.
The practice was completely different - running from school, throwing the bag into a corner and running out.
Play ball games with other children, jumping a shot.
Then, during the series Helena and her boys, embroider with a chain stitch a festive tablecloth for Grandma's birthday.
And then, after several warnings, finally wash the dishes, vacuum, in the winter put the wood into the tiled stoves and do the tasks only after dark under the lamp (or rely on a classmate and instead of tasks write love verses addressed to school love).
In the morning to eat homemade strawberry marmalade (from real fruit, not those jams that are sold now, the contents of which did not lie next to the strawberries) and drink it with tea from dried herbs, which my mother and I loved to collect.
I also remember those magical evenings when there was grave silence in my room, when my sister and I were recording songs from the radio to the tapes, which were spinning loudly in an old tape recorder after my mother.
And then we rotated the tapes and played the same song over and over again, our favorite and toasted with a siphon.
Or were we on the phone through the table using cans that were tied to each other - and while we're on the phone to hear each other better, it was necessary to clean our ears thoroughly, at that time it was match + cotton wool.
The minimum wage was more than three times lower than it is now, and my parents were able to live from it in such a way that we didn't miss anything.
We didn't need any of those iPhones, iPads, not even cellphones and laptops or designer clothes.
The clothes were inherited from generation to generation, and the best "adhence" to the wardrobe was a batik T-shirt made using SAVA.
From the electronics, we were satisfied with my mother's cassetette and, at the most Walkman, which we shared with our sister and which, by the way, we used only on the train on a school trip anyway.
We found it more interesting to play with Aunt Hat Hop or Man, don't be mad, or as I've already stated to go "only" out.
When I was a little older we got a video player under the tree, surely my parents saved for it for a long time, but unfortunately it did not warm up for a long time.
My younger sister stuffed a strudel into it, supposedly cracking those tapes so beautifully, so she tried if he was sleeping the strudel.
Sadly, he was asleep, but it was his last bite.
Well, he's got this story to this day, poor thing, on his dad's plate all the time.
She says that when Lenka was a little girl, she stuffed the strudel into the video and thought she would play the recipe and didn't play it, that's why she still can't bake it.
I remember when we were all able to communicate with each other, communicate without a mobile phone, a computer.
My sister and I agreed with my granddaughter’s granddaughter with my granddaughter, when we will meet at my grandmother’s house again next summer – and that’s how it was, that’s how it worked.
We're together.
Promise yourself now that you will see each other in a year?!
I also remember the winter, when the whole village and I used to run outdoors, build snowmen, ride on a snoozy hill by the church on sacks stuffed with hay.
Then, together with the whole family, we had dinner and talked about the past day.
Of course, my sister and I used to hear the notorious phrase before each dinner: "Go wash your hands!"
Yes, this still applies today, but you certainly don't have soap tied to the tap in the lemon net.
After dinner, when my dad was "working" in the garage over a beer with Jarda from the village, my mother and I lay down on the floor, leaning our feet against the tiled stove, opening the door and staring at the flame and listening to the crackling of the wood pouring from the stoves.
We were getting closer - really - we didn't count how many friends we had on the social network, we knew how many friends we had, the real ones!
We communicated together - we spoke - we didn't write daily messages through various chalets, texts.
There was no such thing, and yet we knew much more about each other.
And that's how I could go on for a long time.
It's beautiful to remember, especially if I add these memories by looking at albums where there are neatly glued with black corners induced by black-and-wheel photos.
And in case I didn't know how old I was then, there's always a year of taking the picture from behind.
I see that even though the cuts according to the pot didn ́t suit us very much and we had pants after our cousin two numbers bigger, no one laughed at us before, it was so normal (not IN).
And I'd better go back one step forward - to reality.
By 2013 and I'll be waiting to see what the modern era will come up maybe someday for my kids.
What I know for sure is that for the younger teen, these memories are now at least "one hundred years behind monkeys" and I think they're missing a lot, that they won't experience this "pre-advocacy" time either.
I'd like them to!
Google, Samsung and Huawei sued over Nortel's patents
The group, which owns thousands of patents to the defunct Nortel company, filed a patent infringement lawsuit against mobile phone makers on Thursday, including Google, a company it overlipped at auction as part of Nortel's bankruptcy.
Rockstar, which bought Nortel's patents for US$4.5 billion, sued Samsung Electronics, HTCE, Huawei and four other companies for patent infringement in a U.S. circuit court in Texas.
Rockstar is owned jointly by Apple, Microsoft, Blackberry, Ericsson and Sony.
Google is accused of violating seven patents.
Patents include technology that helps connect terms searched on the Internet with relevant advertising, according to the lawsuit, which is the core of Google's search business.
Representatives of Samsung, Huawei, HTC and Rockstar were not immediately reached.
Samsung, Huawei and HTC produce phones that operate on Google Android's operating system, which competes heavily with mobile products from Apple and Microsoft.
In 2011, Google made a US$900 millione for Nortel’s patents.
Google increased its offer several times and eventually offered up to US$4.4 billion.
After Nortel was defeated by Rockstar for US$12.5 billion, Google acquired Motorola Mobility for US$12.5 billion, a business motivated in part by Motorola’s patent group.
“Despite its failure to obtain patents that are the subject of the dispute at auction, Google has infringed and continues to infringe patents.”
Rockstar seeks increased damages from Google because, according to it, there is an infringement of Google's patent intentionally, according to the lawsuit.
Miloslava Cejpková: November Smile
It came from the outside, 4 degrees Celsius that we're not used to.
At first she put down her smile, the one she kept on display as a defense against the would-be friendly surroundings.
This relieved her too much, because it was a mask that she decided not to put away in public.
He was sincere at home only in the days when she had grandkidren Elinka or Adíka around.
And, of course, when her sons were on the phone.
She remembered very well the precept from the endless courses of business skills.
Now they've finally really come in handy.
So she doesn't grieve those closest.
Then he flew to the corner.
It's like a disguise on a masquerade.
November, or another month, can't do that miracle for her to smile, like when they've been on a trip with someone who hasn't been here for almost three years.
Egypt puts first freely elected president into office
Muhammad Morsi has taken an inaugural oath, but his triumphant day will hardly end with political unrest in Egypt.
ISLAMIST Muhammad Morsi vowed a “new Egypt” when he was inaugurated as the country’s first freely elected president, after Hosni Mubarak, who had been deposed 16 months earlier.
At his time in office before the High Constitutional Court, Morsi also became the first freely elected Islamic president in the Arab world and the fifth Egyptian head of state since the overthrow of the monarchy about 60 years ago.
He took an oath in front of 18 judges dressed in black gowns in a mansion built overlooking the Nile, which resembles an ancient Egyptian temple.
"We're looking for a better tomorrow, a new Egypt and a second republic," Morsi said at a ceremony broadcast live on state television.
“Today, the Egyptian people have laid the foundations of a new life – absolute freedom, true democracy and stability,” said Morsi, a 60-year-old engineer educated in the U.S
When Morsi arrived shortly after 11 p.m. local time in a small column of cars, hundreds of soldiers and police were guarding the building.
Only a few hundred supporters gathered in front of the courthouse to glorify the president, and, unlike the presidential flamboyance of Mubarak's period, traffic was only briefly halted so that the motorcade could pass through a usually busy road connecting the city center with the suburbs in the south.
The personal prestige of Morsi, mockingly referred to as the Muslim Brotherhood’s non-charismatic “reserve tire”, has grown sharply since his victory and Friday’s speech, which aimed to present him not only as an Islamist candidate, but also of all those who want to bring to an end of rebellion against authoritarian Mubarak.
"Egypto today is a civil, national, constitutional and modern state," Morsi, dressed in a blue social suit with a red tie, told judges in a wood-paneled chamber where he took the oath.
Mursi later traveled to Cairo University, where he was due to deliver an inaugural speech.
While he stood at attention, he received an official welcome from a military band playing the national anthem.
The military commander of Field Marshal Hussein Tantawi was present.
His arrival was accompanied by chanting of the “army, and the people are one hand” from the mouths of hundreds of people gathered in the university’s main lecture hall.
The University of Cairo was founded in 1908 as a center of secular education and later in the 1970s became a bastion of Islamic student movements.
Morsi took a symbolic oath in Tahrir Square on Friday, at the site where the revolution that ended Mubarak's authoritarian rule last year, and solemnly vowed to once again seize the presidential powers taken from his office by the military council, which took them over from the ousted leader.
But by agreeing to make an official oath before the court, rather than before parliament, as is customary, he submitted to the will of the military, suggesting that the struggle for power would continue.
Morsi's speech in Tahrir Square was fraught with dramatic populist gestures.
There will probably be police specialists in the ghettos, they are supposed to ease tensions
Police specialists should work in ghettos in the Czech Republic in the future.
They'd spend up to half of their errands here.
They'd supervise order and solve problems.
Thanks to their knowledge of the environment, they should contribute to reducing tensions between people from impoverishements and residents.
In its proposal to reduce security risks, the Ministry of the Interior envisages this.
According to him, improving the situation in the country would be helped mainly by "accelerated provision of social housing".
The document was discussed by the government.
According to the analysis, there were around 300 poor houses and neighborhoods in the Czech Republic a few years ago.
There were 80,000 people living in them, mainly Roma.
Adults in ghettos were out of work, families were dependent on benefits, children ended up in "special" schools.
She was usury.
According to the experts of the troubled localities, in the meantimonily, it has increased by a
Relationships between their inhabitants and others are escalating.
Anti-Romani marches were held in several places.
People from around the country also joined the extremists.
According to the interior, it is difficult for the police in areas with ghettos to maintain security and order.
An essential condition is the "consistent application of equal access in law enforcement."
However, it is not possible to achieve this without detailed knowledge of the place and people.
The specialist would go to errands in the ghetto, where he would spend up to half of his service, the rest would serve in his district.
In a troubled location, he'd oversee order.
He'd also know the locals, so he could deal better with problems or violations.
"Thanks to established confidence, specialists will be more easily able to prevent a number of infringements," the interior ministry said.
Specialists in ghettos would receive surcharges, 500 to 1000 crowns a month.
The introduction of special police officers into problem areas is to be covered by Norwegian funds.
The budget is about 600,000 euros (now about 15.5 million crowns).
The Czech Republic pays 15 percent of the sum, funds 85 percent.
Preparations have started this year, now a detailed proposal is being processed.
The project is expected to last until 2016.
The authors were inspired by the neighbors.
This mechanism has been successfully applied for several years in Slovakia, where the problem with socially excluded localities is incomparably higher than in the Czech Republic.
"There are so-called Romani settlements here," the report's authors said.
How many specialists there should be in the Czech Republic is not yet clear.
The answer is supposed to give an analysis that will determine the places where a special police officer would be needed.
It will also depend on how much the police will be able to release annually after the end of the project.
According to the design of the project, selected police officers complete an internship in Slovakia first.
Five dozen members should go through this training every year.
From them will come the most suitable candidates.
There should also be special education for police officers who will work in ghettos.
According to the authors, mainly social housing would help to improve the situation.
It should motivate and lead to integration.
According to the ideas of the interior, it would have three categories.
The minimum standard would meet hostels with specified rules, such as the number of people per room.
The middle standard would be in smaller apartments.
A higher standard would have social dwellings that would be comparable to ordinary housing.
Tenants would have to carry out an integration plan to be drawn up by social workers.
They would have to adhere to accommodation rules, take care of their health, send children to schools, retrain and actively look for work.
If they tried to integrate, they would progress to a higher standard of housing.
According to the interior, the system of permeable housing should operate for at least 30 years, until the next generation "in a pro-integrated environment" grows.
The model of social housing is prepared by resorts for local development and work.
They're supposed to propose it by the end of the year
Karel Drábek: Coal Hill - one of our youngest volcanoes.
Coal Hill is a striking hill 3 km southeast of Bruntál.
Together with several others, it is a proof of volcanic activity in Lízký Jeseník at a time (from a geological point of view) recently.
The last eruptions here were at the beginning of the Quaternary.
When viewed from a greater distance, there is a conspicuous white-plastered baroque Pilgrimage Church of Our Lady of Help, built between 1755 and 1765.
He replaced the original wooden pilgrimage chapel.
The inn, which stood next to the church, did not behave.
Shortly after the completion of the church, an alley was planted from four rows of lip, about 1 km long.
The cross road on Uhlířské vrch is already the third version, the original stood here after 1916, the second, from 2001 it lasted only until 2004.
On fifteen stops there are wooden sculptures made by the local art carver František Nedomlel.
He used various types of trees from the Low Jeseník region.
Similarly, pedestals from rocks from the local region form an interesting geological exposition.
The name of the sponsor is also indicated at each stop.
Coal hill is the remainder of a young volcano.
Although the data from radiometric measurements of age varies somewhat, the last eruption here was about 1.5 million years ago.
It is therefore younger than Velký Roudný (aged 3.3 million years) and older than Venus volcano (aged 0.8 million years).
The volcano was born through an explosion.
In doing so, it ejected a significant amount of volcanic ash, along with fragments of rocks from the bedrock, which, by firing, acquired a reddish-brown color.
At the end of this phase, lava flowed to the surface - today it is a layer of porous basalt up to 13 meters powerful.
The second eruption ejected red, brown-red and, in places, black tuffs.
Their layer is powerful up to 50 meters and its part is accessible in the wall of the quarry.
There are conspicuous volcanic cougars large up to 1 meter. Even the second eruption was finished with a lava outburst.
At the end of the activity from the volcano, lava flowed, which today forms up to 50 m a mighty layer of slaggy basalt.
Volcanoes that alternate the ejection of volcanic ash with lava discharges are the most common type of volcanoes.
Because they are actually composed of layers, they were given the name stratovulcans.
The original volcanic cone was gradually erosive.
However, it was threatened only by the mining of rocks in the quarry on the south side.
The most intensive mining took place here between 1953 and 1962.
The tuffs were added to the concrete, which was then lighter and had better thermal insulation properties.
Mining managed under the pressure of experts and the public and thus preserves this unique place.
However, the problem is in porosity and the small cohesion of rocks, which quickly weather, decay and slide down the slope.
The quarry is disappearing quite quickly.
To make the situation even more complicated, a warm, south-facing slope is an ideal place for some rare species of plants and animals.
The correction is therefore problematic, but over time it will be necessary to select and maintain a characteristic geological profile.
It's nothing new, similar profiles are maintained in a number of important locations.
Uhlířský Hill can be reached by following the green tourist sign from Bruntál or from Slezský Kočov.
Automobilists can use an unpaid car park at the beginning of the linden alley, where even a short educational trail begins.
She moved from the villa to a block of flats.
Ivana grew up and spent most of her life in an apartment villa with a garden and swimming pool in one of Prague’s most beautiful villa neighborhoods in Ořechovka.
However, the original owners of the house got it back in restitution and the tenants had to move out with a small severance.
Ivana quickly searched for alternative housing for herself and her older mother at that time.
From acquaintances she got a tip for an apartment 3 + kk in an older panel house on the opposite side of Prague.
"At first it was a shock, but the view from the fourth floor of a large forest park and the dam thrilled me on the other side," Ivana recalls of the first impression.
At first, I thought it'd be a makeshift for about three years.
"Today we have lived here for more than thirteen years, and not only because of the gradual reconstruction, I wouldn't change," the owner adds.
At that time, the mother and daughter moved from a hundred-meter apartment to a smaller apartment with an area of 62 m2 and gave away almost all the equipment.
The action was not easy, things were stored with friends, and it took almost a year.
The new apartment was in pretty good condition, but necessary modifications had to take place, such as painting doors or replacing windows.
The previous owner demolished the core, but the owner was most bothered by the impractically designed and unnecessarily large hallway, and on the contrary a small bathroom.
By combining two rooms, a multifunctional living space was created.
The architects used plasterboard to define the individual zones and divide the room, which allowed for a new location of the lights.
The first intervention she took after a few years was the removal of a partition in the living room.
This changed the disposition to 2 + kk, but the room regained the airyness and opened a panoramic view of the surrounding greenery.
The next step to partially improving the standard was to replace the PVC floor with laminate.
Ivana didn't know what to do with the bathroom.
For years, I subscribed to housing magazines looking for inspiration.
I recently discovered an interesting realization in one of them.
On my recommendation, I approached the young architect Kamila Douděrová.
“It was actually the most important step in the future,” Ivana said.
Thanks to the reconstruction of the bathroom, it was also possible to adjust the layout of the hall.
The architect connected the space of the toilet and the bathroom and replaced the two doors with opening wings with one sliding, thus sparing space.
The bathroom is timeless and also sufficiently wheelchair accessible.
Quality tiles imitate sandstone, in the shower there is also a comfortable seat, at the closet a bidet shower.
The successful cooperation with Kamila Douděrová continued.
As part of How to Build a Dream, there was a quick but fundamental transformation of the living room, in which the architect Martin Čeněk also participated.
We had to get three functions into the space, which was created by combining two smaller rooms, dining, living and sleeping, without helping ourselves with the classical principles of optical division, for example, half-crossbars or Japanese walls.
"This, as a result, would violate the openness and uniformity of space," the architect explains.
It was originally able to separate the sleeping and living areas.
It was enough to turn two sofas with your back and put a custom-made duvet cabinet between them.
The dining nook is determined by shades of flooring and painting.
The architect used high-quality marmoleum on the floor in the kitchen and dining area, the sliding door revived with wallpaper with a pattern.
If necessary, the kitchenette is covered with sliding panels with wallpaper.
The new anti-allergic marmoleum floor has a number of advantages over the previous laminate: a softer non-slip surface without joints and better sound insulation.
The system of niches and ceilings made of plasterboard highlighted by paint is an example of how you can work with space in the panel house.
I left the colors to the architects, I wanted it to be joyful.
"I like orange, I had the same shade in the previous apartment," Ivana says.
The graphoglass cladding with the motif of blossoming magnolia is practical and creates a pronounced aesthetic element.
For a warm energetic tone, the architects chose white, which underlined the modern functional style.
In a small apartment managed to store the books, among other things, in the space under the window sills.
Everything is within reach, the lamps are comfortably controlled remotely.
In the remodeled interior, the contrast of the new with the older works perfectly, for example, with a decades-old crystal chandelier and with high-quality art objects that Ivana collects all her life.
Last came the kitchen.
The cabinets used to be located on both sides, and the owner here felt a bit like in a tunnel.
The architect detracted from their numbers and rearranged their deployments.
Furniture in a simple design is made to order, the white area softens a slightly pearly surface.
Thanks to the imaginative solution, the washing machine, dryer and dishwasher fit into a small space.
The appearance of a small kitchen revived the all-glass cladding of the walls above the worktop.
Unique graph glass with a digitally printed theme is attractive, in addition, it is excellently maintained.
Ivana chose the flowers of magnolias, which remind her of the favorite neighborhood in which she lived for years.
Now she seems to be happy here, too.
In the original bathroom there was a small bathtub and sink, in the new owner chose a neutral shade of cladding.
The large shower room has Ronal glass doors.
The task was to create from the given environment a clear simple space, in which functions and devices will be in harmony.
We unified the interior materially and in color and attempted its elegant, cozy, not too crowded appearance.
"Another condition of the assignment was a barrier-free solution with a minimum of obstacles and the possibility of easy movement around furniture and the availability of everyday items," adds Kamila Douděrová.
The Supreme Court has ruled: Nečas can be prosecuted.
The Supreme Court has ruled that former Prime Minister Petr Nečas can also be prosecuted for possible offences, which he may have committed before the August dissolution of the Chamber of Deputies.
It was protected only by procedural immunity, which applies to speeches in the House.
In the case of former ODS deputies Ivan Fuksa, Mark Šnajdr and Petr Tluchoř, on the contrary, decided that they could not be punished for possible offences until they gave up their parliamentary mandates last November, Petr Knötig, a spokesman for the Supreme Court, said.
With this decision, the court rejected a motion by prosecutor Ivo Ištvana, who wanted to continue criminal proceedings with MPs.
According to him, they accepted a bribe in the form of lucrative posts in state-owned companies for giving up the mandate and not causing problems in approving the laws.
On the contrary, it is still possible to prosecute the former prime minister, who offered them these posts.
An anti-corruption police officer was also arrested with Vít Bárta
Bárta's lawyer Oldřich Chudoba, who also represented him in the case of the alleged corruption of VV MPs, also did not want to comment on the case, but said: "Yes, I represent the client in this matter as well, but due to the protection of classified facts, I cannot comment further."
According to information Rights, the case is handled by the General Inspection of the Security Forces (GIBS).
News server tn.cz reported that Bárta could buy classified information from the police.
According to the news server prvnizpravy.cz, the case may have been related to the case of former police president Petr Lessy, whose appointment Bart allegedly sought.
However, the latest information Rights shows that the detention of Vít Bárta is unrelated to Petr Lessy.
Eva Kropáčová, spokeswoman for the police presidium, declined to comment on the matter, saying the case was being handled by the Prague city prosecutor's office.
"We will not comment on this question with regard to the protection of classified facts," Štěpánka Zenklová, spokeswoman for the Municipal Public Prosecutor's Office in Prague, told Právo on Friday.
It is precisely the local plaintiff who supervises Bárta’s case, and its words indirectly indicate that the file or criminal proceedings are conducted in the secrecy regime.
The reporter of the Novelties managed to reach the wife of Vít Bárta Kateřina Klasnová in the morning before their residence in Klárov, Prague.
She had just loaded her suitcases into the car and left.
She declined to comment on the event.
On Friday morning, Tomio Okamura, the leader of the Dawn movement, commented on Bárta's detention.
According to him, he has no idea what is involved in the case.
"Vít Bárta was one of the many candidates of many parties on our candidate, and as I have repeatedly said before the elections, he was not even a member of Tomio Okamura's Dawn of Direct Democracy," he added.
Vít Bárta, who ran unsuccessfully for Tomio Okamura’s Dawn of Direct Democracy movement in the past early elections to the Chamber of Deputies in the Pilsen Region, is known for his corruption case of bribery by MPs of Public Affairs.
In April 2012, he was sentenced to 18 months with a 30-month reprievement.
Subsequently, the verdict was changed and Bárta was acquitted.
I want a contract in the NHL.
The juice, the lust and the enthusiasm I still have
For Prospal, family has always come first, which is why he wants to fully submit to it at the age of 38.
He'll either get a job overseas and extend his career, or he'll definitely quit hockey.
"I want to play the role of daddy and husband."
"I'm really looking forward to it," says the hockey player, who is still waiting in vain for an offer.
Despite the fact that he's a well-proclaimed worker who'll never cheat anything.
Despite leaving his heart on the ice every time and being able to pull his teammates.
And also despite the fact that he was Columbus' most productive player last season.
"But everyone's desire to show them in me all the time," he declares determinedly.
He may have, reportedly the New York Rangers, who failed to enter the season and also have injured two supports - strikers Rick Nash and Ryan Callahan.
What does your arrival at Rangers look like?
It's just speculation.
As long as I don't have something on paper, there's no point in dealing with it.
I know there's an article published in the New York Post that they should sign me, but that's it.
It was no longer added that it really happened.
Unfortunately, I'd like to announce that I've got a contract, but that's just not the case.
You haven't even heard back from the club?
Such communication in the NHL takes place every day.
That's where it's about getting into a situation where the contract really comes.
When, for example, a player gets injured or someone can't do it and the crew doesn't trample.
There's a lot of different factors that have to come together for me to get the contract.
But there are similar circumstances in New York, isn't it?
Injured is Rick Nash with Ryan Callahan and the team didn't make it into the season, so there's a chance, don't you think?
There's always that chance, of course.
But the second thing is, you always need two sides for that.
I'd take a lot of offers today, but I haven't received one yet.
Unfortunately, this is the reality in which I have to live.
You're flying to America on Friday, and you're expecting something to click?
I've had those expectations since July 5, when the free-agent market opened up in the NHL, and still nothing.
Are you going to New York to be reminded?
I'm definitely not, I'm going to Tampa to see the kids and my wife.
I'm looking forward to the sun and when I see you again.
Do you have your program planned?
That's because I'm a father and my husband, and that's where it all comes down to it.
I'm no hockey player at the moment - I don't have a contract and I don't play games.
So I'm going to play the role of daddy and husband, and I'm really looking forward to it.
Vasek already has two hockey games on Sunday, it is a holiday for me and my whole family, when we can go to support our children in what they want to do.
Are you thinking of doing this in full-time quotes?
Sure, you can think about a lot of things.
This, too, can happen.
I tried it three years ago when I missed 50 games for knee surgery.
And I enjoyed it very much and very much.
Someday it'll get to such a stage that all the goals, wins and passes won't replace what you've got at home.
Because family and children, that's the meaning of life.
Let's have the job you want.
I had a job that was the most beautiful thing that could happen to me besides my family.
You've had a lot of offers from Europe: do you want to play the NHL just because it's the NHL, or it's because of your family that you want to be in America?
Since I'm a hockey player, sporting success comes first - in addition to my family.
The NHL is the best competition in the world.
This is where family life comes from.
My children, that is sorry, our children - mine and my wives - grew up in The States, started going to school there and so on.
And we'd very much like them to continue this trend.
Because what we're going to tell each other, there are options somewhere else in America.
That's the chance we're supposed to offer.
Are you planning to stay in America permanently?
We're going to practice it like we're going until now.
This means that in the summer we will be in the Czech Republic in Hluboká and around České Budějovice.
And in the winter again in Tampa or where our steps will take us.
It's perfect to be warm all the time.
I've been very lucky in my life.
It can be said that I went to meet him, it cost a lot of strength and renunciation, and it took away a lot of the things I missed.
But hockey offered me a lot of options in return, and that's one of them.
That my wife and I can choose where to live.
There's a lot of people in the world who crave this so they can choose what they're going to do and how they're going to live.
How long will you have the wife in your age to work, train hard and wait for an offer?
Yeah, that's a question I can't answer.
Today I still have it in me, the juice, the lust, the enthusiasm, the passion for the sport. The desire and motivation to prove to people that you still have in the NHL, that I'm still better than someone else.
But I can't say how much longer it'll stay there.
It can't be planned.
So you can say you're going to wait this season to see how it turns out, and then you'll decide what's next?
I'm at the stage where I'm going every day.
