CNN: Israel has bombed a military base in Syria.
The target should have been Russian missiles for Hezbollah.
Israeli military jets reportedly bombed a military base near the Syrian port of Latakia on Thursday.
Citing an unnamed U.S. government official, CNN reported.
The Israeli press widely reported on the explosion in an alleged missile depot, but the Israeli government did not confirm the attack.
A spokeswoman for the Israeli military declined to comment on the airstrike.
"We don't comment on foreign media reports," she said.
According to a U.S. government source, the target of an Israeli airstrike was missiles and related equipment that, in the opinion of the Israelis, could be transported to the militant Lebanese Hezbollah movement.
According to another U.S. security official, another U.S. security official reported that Russian SA-125 missiles were the target of an Israeli airstrike on a target on Syrian territory.
The second channel of Israeli television reported that the target of the attack was supposedly a highly modern Syrian air defense system.
According to the DPA, satellite images of the area of attack have shown a battery of Russian S-125 Neva missiles.
In addition, batteries of SA-3 missiles, including a command center with radar to track missiles and their targets, could be seen on them.
According to an Israeli television report, the recorded rockets have a 35-kilometre range and could carry warheads weighing 70 kilograms.
Israel has reportedly bombed shipments of missiles inside Syria at least twice this year, CNN and AP said.
The Israeli military has never responded to such reports, but has repeatedly stated that it will intervene any supply of weapons intended for Hezbollah or other groups identified as terrorist.
Is the internet the end of travel agencies?
Flight Centre seems to have successfully resisted this trend.
The company has updated its estimate of annual profit and expects record gains from holidaymakers in Australia and the UK.
The travel agency now expects its one-yield pre-tax profit to be between US$325 and US$340 million versus US$305 to US$315 million from the previous forecast.
If current developments are sustained, this will represent a 12 to 17 percent increase against US$290.4 million in record profit of 2011/12.
CEO Graham Turner said Flear Centre had generated an eight-percentage profit in the first half and the second-half began with sharp growth primarily in Australian and British travel outside business trips.
"As of today's date, our 10 countries are profitable and several are heading towards a record-breaking annual profit ahead of interest and taxation," he said.
This includes Australia and the United Kingdom, which are traditionally our biggest profit makers.
Business with recreational activities recovered during the second half in Australia, offsetting a slightly weaker domestic market with corporate travel.
Similarly, in the UK, Flight Centre fared well in the recreation area, while corporate clients spent less.
U.S. trade has recovered after a loss during a seasonally weaker half-year and is expected to bring a full-round profit for the third time in a row.
Flight Centre's shares were 3 cents higher yesterday at USD 38.20.
Greenpeace activists detained in Russia were transported to St. Petersburg
Environmental activists detained since September in Russia have been transported from Murmansk to St. Petersburg over a protest against planned oil drilling in the Arctic.
This was reported today by AFP, citing the international environmental organization Greenpeace.
At the same time, it claims that the original accusations of its activists from piracy have not yet been dropped.
"Greenpeace International has learned from diplomatic sources that thirty men and women, detained in Russia after nonviolent action against an oil rig in the Arctic, were transported from a correctional facility in Murmansk to a prison in St. Petersburg," the organization said in a statement.
The reasons for the relocation of the detained activists are not clear; according to DPA, it was a planned move.
Authorities initially accused environmentalists of piracy, for which they were facing up to 15 years in prison if convicted.
Last week, however, they announced that it would replace the charge with milder rioting, for which there is a risk of a lower sentence with an upper limit of seven years imprisonment.
Greenpeace, however, warned in a statement today that allegations of piracy had not yet been dropped by the authorities, as promised.
This means that the detainees are now charged with two offences.
A powerful Investigation Committee announced last week that it would replace allegations of piracy with allegations of rioting.
However, when thirty prisoners stood before the committee this week, allegations of piracy have not been dropped.
Instead, new charges have been filed against everyone," Greenpeace was quoted as saying.
In mid-September, three dozen members of the Greenpeace movement from 18 countries were arrested at Gazprom's oil rig.
Ecologists were on an Arctic Sunrise that sailed under the Dutch flag.
Since then, everyone has been held in custody, against whom they filed an appeal, but the court dismissed them all.
Environmentalists are complaining about the conditions of imprisonment.
Greenpeace CEO Kumi Naidoo said the move to St. Petersburg would make it easier for detainees and consular workers to meet.
However, there are no guarantees that the conditions in the new prison will be better than in Murmansk.
"In fact, they can be worse," he pointed out.
King Willem-Alexandro was assisted by one of the detained activists, Faiza Oulahsen.
In a handwritten letter published today by Algemeen Dagblad, she called on the monarch to talk about their situation during a planned meeting with Russian President Vladimir Putin.
