Schools should focus more on mathematics, spelling and grammar
It will be necessary to study at least one of Shakespear's plays, 19th-century novels, romantic poetry and contemporary British fiction from 1914 onwards.
The rehearsal will also include "hidden text" to motivate deeper reading;
Combination courses of English literature and language will be canceled.
From 2015, students will be required to have a language exam and will be encouraged to choose English literature as an elective course.
The Department of Education is due to issue a new curriculum for English and mathematics tomorrow – the first subjects to go through a radical change.
For other crucial subjects, changes are expected next year.
In a stand-alone step, the Ofqual office, which oversees graduation exams, reveals changes in the structure of the graduation exam, together with a new grading system and a smaller volume of projects for students.
Speaking over the summer, Education Secretary Michael Gove said there was "wide consensus when it comes to whether we need to reform our exam system in order to regain the confidence of society" and that there is a need to insist that the graduation exam be "more challenging, ambitious and stricter."
The study shows that English schools devote less time to mathematics – 116 hours a year or three hours a week – than most other countries.
By comparison, schools in Australia devote an average of 143 hours a year to mathematics and about 138 in Singapore.
Although there will be no official requirement to devote more time to mathematics, coalition sources said that a comprehensive mathematics graduation exam – along with a greater emphasis on this subject in the results rankings – is likely to encourage schools to provide extra teaching.
The curriculum will focus more on “real-world problems,” including financial mathematics.
Mayor Kajnar: I'm not a cancer in the CSSD
Before the elections, a long-smoldering dispute between the two wings fighting for influence within the ČSSD in Ostrava flared up fully.
Everything culminated when the mayor of Ostrava, Petr Kajnar, appealed to the trial over the undemocratic formation of the candidates.
Nevertheless, the actors did not want to comment too much on this, so as not to harm the party even more before the elections.
By now, however, the votes of the voters have been counted.
The leadership of the ČSSD in Ostrava called on the mayor of Ostrava on Monday evening to leave the party, and he will probably also push him to lose his position.
Petr Kajnar wants to keep fighting.
He described in an exclusive interview with Diary in an exclusive interview what is going out in disputes and who is on whose side.
How do you see the outcome of the election?
The CSSD was certainly counting on a much better result.
Has it been harmed in our country that disputes within the party were resolved in the media before the elections?
I can imagine that social democracy could have a better election result.
But on the other hand, it corresponds to the state in which the CSSD is now.
It's a party that has its internal problems, it's intrinsically ill, and people perceive that everything is wrong in it.
They made that clear in the election.
What are CSSD's internal diseases?
In my opinion, the ODS has been going through bankruptcy for some time, and such things are now taking place in social democracy.
It is that the lobbyists, influential people who previously expected to find their prebends at the ODS, have moved to the people in the ČSSD and have an influence on them.
It has to do with the fact that police go around the offices of the Ostrava municipality and collect computers.
It shows that the CSSD is not only composed of people who want the good of all others, but many of them think primarily about their own good.
ČSSD is divided into two irreconcilable camps in Prague, as can be seen in the current struggle for power between the Haškovecs and Sobotkovs.
This, too, has been prepared for a long time.
What happened before the elections in Ostrava was just a harbinger of what is happening now in Prague.
Hašek's wing gradually occupied positions, so that after the elections it could provoke a situation that will weaken Sobotka's wing.
One is in politics in a situation where one sometimes has to approach compromises.
However, everyone has an internal limit, when they can no longer compromise, and that is my case.
Initially, I didn’t want to run for the House, but when I perceived the whole thing inside the party, I agreed with Luboš Zaorálek that I would try to get on the regional candidate to strengthen Sobotka’s wing as mayor of Ostrava.
However, Hašek's wing gave it a stop.
Why are you on the side of Bohuslav Sobotka, and not Michal Hašek?
In my opinion, Slávek Sobotka is a person who always talks primarily about the ČSSD program and, to a certain extent, about visions for the party and for this country.
I have to admit, I didn't hear anything like that on the other side.
Michal Hašek is just fighting for power.
I don't see how a bureau that is a lower body than the Central Executive Committee can only start dismissing the party chairman.
That's the struggle for power, regardless of everything and against the party's statutes.
In my opinion, what Michal Hašek has now demonstrated in Prague ended his political career.
How did you perceive the fact that the Ostrava ČSSD leadership did not approve you for a candidate.
Political parties are based on a competition, who ranks as among their members.
This competition should, for the most part, be based on programmatic visions and the ability of those people to fulfill those visions.
Of course, there are also people who don’t have a lot of ability or vision in this competition, but a political party is an elevator to money that they wouldn’t get elsewhere.
The problem is when these people start to come to the fore thanks to the fact that without scruples they break the rules, they violate the party's statutes.
With their practices, they make the political parties mafia and, in my opinion, violate the laws of the Czech Republic.
To draw attention to this issue is more important to me that to take into account whether any Kajnar has a political future.
It is important that the parties begin to respect the statutes.
That is why, before the elections, you filed an application for a preliminary injunction against the form of a ČSSD candidate in the Moravian-Silesian Region?
The current district executive committee of the ČSSD in Ostrava consolidates its power by being able to abolish organizations that do not suit it, setting up new organizations on purpose, gradually throwing away inconvenient members of the party.
An example is that they wanted to exclude Richard Bednařík from the club of representatives, but he filed a lawsuit against it, and they then annulled the resolution on his exclusion.
This shows that seeking compliance with the party’s statutes through the courts is the last option, but at the moment when the other ways and the control bodies within the CSSD have ceased to function, this is the only option.
The effort of the leadership of the Ostrava ČSSD, headed by MP Adam Rykala, is to remove you as mayor.
It is an open secret that the mayor of Poruba, Lumír Palyza, is interested in this post.
That's right, Adam Rykala and Lumír Palyza are trying to do everything they can to dismiss me.
They promise people positions, get votes, and then consolidate their power in the party for those voices.
When I go back to the past, everything that took place around the football club Baník Ostrava and the purchase of the stadium Bazaly was just an excuse for them to attack me.
It was artificially staged so they could label me as a person who puts money where it doesn't belong.
At the same time, the city bought property that is strategically of great value.
So how do you see it next with your stay as mayor?
On Monday, the district committee called on me and other people to consider staying in the CSSD.
But I think it's like when a thief shouts: Catch a thief!
When we look at the results of my work over the last eleven years, I do not see anyone initiated and enforced, sometimes against members of the party, the emergence of so many developing things, such as industrial zones or the expansion of the science and technology park.
Still, everything becomes inconvenient.
I don't read it other than that I'm hindering others to break through to the places they desire.
Are you going to fight against these efforts?
Therefore, if this republic is supposed to work well, political parties must also work well.
It's hard to believe that any party will be able to manage the state effectively if it can't control itself.
So I don't think I'd leave the CSSD right now, because I don't think that the one who causes cancer of this party is not me, but these people who are trying to drive me out.
Olomouc will open a public rink in the historical centre
Pavel Spálený, the organizer of Olomouc Christmas markets and winter skating, said that preparatory work on the rink started on October 26 and an ice-making unit has been in operation since Wednesday.
The ice layer is already seven centimeters thick and by Friday morning the area will be ready for the first visitors.
The rink has an area of 375 square meters, the podium took 358 square meters.
Skaters and passers-by will also have two stalls with punch.
"We have expanded the floor area, enlarged the space in front of the stalls, and almost the entire area of the stage is covered so that skaters can move comfortably," Spálený described.
The streets of Olomouc are already commemorating the impending Christmas.
Last year, the ice rink was installed directly in front of the main entrance of the town hall on the Upper Square due to the repair.
The repair of Lower Square was completed at the end of the summer, so that the ice area could return to its original place.
For the first time, people were able to skate right in front of City Hall in 2009, when the rink was open from January to March.
The ice area then became an integral part of Christmas markets in the city.
Christmas markets, which include a rich cultural program, will be opened in the Upper Square a month before Christmas Eve.
