The brutal murder of a senior in the Zlín region will again be dealt with by the regional court
The regional court acquitted a twenty-five-year-old Slovak and a twenty-two-year-old Czech for lack of evidence.
The men denied the blame.
They face between 15 and 200 years in prison or an exceptional sentence.
Spokesman Petr Angyalossy confirmed that the High Court annulled the regional decision and returned the matter to him for consideration.
He declined to commentate on the reasons led to this, saying the decision had not yet been delivered to the parties.
According to the indictment, the youth ambushed a seventy-seven-year-old man at his home.
A senior has died a tormented death.
According to the indictment, the men strangled him, beating and kicking him all over his body, as well as with a metal object, apparently with a gas pistol, planted about three dozen wounds to his head.
The man suffered serious injuries, such as fractures of the cranial bone, swelling of the brain or bleeding in the brain, which he succumbed to on the spot.
The youths reportedly took about 3000 crowns from the house and jewelry worth about 14 thousand crowns.
According to the regional court, they were the only, in addition, indirect evidence against the defendant's odor traces, which the expert assessed as not fully established within the meaning of the legal guidelines.
The odour traces from the crime scene and on other cases coincided with the defendants in more than ten cases, but the police initially dealt with them as factual and not scent clues.
In addition, certain misconducts were found when dealing with them.
According to the Senate, the defendants' guilt was not proven without doubt.
However, the court acknowledged that police had very difficult conditions.
A dead senior with a broken head was found by relatives who thought he had apparently fallen and injured himself.
Even the emergency medical examiner did not draw attention to the violent deaths.
After a few days, the criminals were contacted by Olomouc doctors, who discovered signs of violent death during an autopsy.
Police were no longer able to work with all traces from the crime scene that relatives had cleaned up.
There was no DNA of the defendants at the scene.
The young Slovak lived in Uherskohradiště.
He is now serving a sentence of 8.5 years, which was finally upheld by the Olomouc High Court last year.
According to him, the man was the head of an organized group of six youths, which was responsible for the robbery of a jewellery store.
Robbery happened in October and November 2011, shortly after the murder of a senior in Slopný.
The younger of those accused of murdering a senior lives in Uherské Hradiště.
The court released him from custody in August after 11 months.
