Living legend Wayne Shorter closes next week Strings of Autumn
Saxophone great and jazz scene legend Wayne Shorter will make a dot for the 18th edition of the Strings of Autumn festival next Wednesday.
Shorter will perform on November 6 in the Grand Hall of Lucerna together with his quartet consisting of Danilo Perez, John Patitucci and Brian Blade.
In the second half of the evening he will join Shorter with a spontaneous jazz improvisation of PKF - Prague Philharmonic under the direction of Martin Kumžák.
Shorter's arrangements for the symphony orchestra will feature Pegasus from the new album Without A Net, as well as his older compositions Lotus and Prometheus Unbound.
Although he celebrated his 80th birthday in August this year, Shorter is still full of strength, which also proves that the Prague concert on the Strings of Autumn will start his world tour.
Wayne Shorter entered the world of music more than sixty years ago.
The list of performers he has collaborated with ranges from Miles Davis to Herbie Hancock, Joni Mitchell to Carlos Santana.
On his musical journey, he also met Czech musician Miroslav Vitouš and Viennese native Joe Zawinul, with whom in the legendary Weather Report for more than one decade they gave the direction of the new fusion style.
For the past thirteen years he has performed especially with the Wayne Shorter Quartet.
There will still be an after-party after the festival, which will take place on November 9 at the Roxy/NoD club.
In addition to charismatic singer Andrea Triana, the Manchester trio Go Go Penguin will be presented to the domestic audience with their jazz-influenced electronics and young jazz talent Ian Mikyska, who will bring an electro-jazz band Profound Bass inspired by dance styles.
Damks are said to prohibit UEFA from disclosing sentences for referees.
Dagmar Damková, chairwoman of the Football Association of the Football Association of the Czech Republic, has stopped publishing sentences for the judges.
UEFA's rules forbid it, she said in an interview with the daily Právo.
Otherwise, the FAČR would be in danger of punishment.
Damková also refused to side with the footballers of Pilsen, where she came from.
These speculations are really pointless.
It is true that I live in Pilsen, but the other members of the commission are from elsewhere, for example from Prague.
So in the case of voting, I can lose 1:6," Damková said in response to the criticism, which rebelled after Pilsen played twice as many penalties than other league teams.
Therefore, although in recent months the FAČR has ceased to publish sentences for referees, Damková assures that the judges pay for their mistakes.
"Believe me that if the committee finds out that the referee made a mistake, it will limit his delegation, no matter what match it is," Damková said.
The reason why sentences are not published, according to Damka, is a ban from the European Football Union.
"UEFA forbids the publication of such information and harshly punishes violations."
"We're very suspicious with us and we're looking for something behind every mistake."
"We published the restrictions, but I received two very strong warnings that if we don't stop with it, there will be harsh sanctions," Damkova said.
Other countries respect the ban.
"We were really an exception," she added.
If the disclosures continued, the commission could lose money from UEFA for the upbringing and development of referees.
"And they're very important to us."
"Without them, we wouldn't be able to fulfill the program to educate the referees," Damková added.
Barta's detention relates to Janoušek and Bém: Is he behind the escaping of wiretaps?
The president of Public Affairs Vít Bárta was arrested by the police on Thursday.
Together with him, Jan Petržílek, the head of anti-corruption police's anti-corruption police department, was also detained.
The detention is related to wiretaps by lobbyist Roman Janoušek and former mayor Pavel Bém (ODS).
The information about the wiretaps, which news server Novinky.cz came with, was confirmed by a trusted source to the Czech press office.
The wiretapped telephone calls of Janoušek and Bém, which were published last March by the daily Mladá fronta Dnes and in which the two men call each other "Mazánka" and "Kolibřík", show Janoušek's influence on the steps of the city under Bém's leadership.
The newspaper said at the time that it was acquired by the Security Information Service (BIS) in 2007, from which two years later it escaped to the ABL security agency.
That's where he owned Barta.
News server Novinky.cz pointed out that the detained criminal Petržílek worked for the BIS prior to joining the anti-corruption police.
Petržílek switched to the anti-corruption unit, which subsequently dealt with wiretaps, at the same time as they were published.
"With regard to the law on the protection of classified information, I cannot confirm or say anything at all," prosecutor Michal Muravský of the Municipal Prosecutor's Office in Prague said.
He also declined to confirm that he was supervising the case.
He doesn't want to comment on an inspection for the same reason.
Six out of ten companies are preparing for the new Civil Code
Six out of ten companies are intensively preparing for the impending effectiveness of the new Civil Code and the Business Corporations Act since 1 January 2014.
Only eight percent of the companies surveyed said they didn't address matters related to both key codes in their company at all.
This is according to a survey conducted by Ambruz & Dark Deloitte Legal, which surveyed the opinions of 108 Czech companies.
In the context of the impending effective date of the two key codes, it is only at the last minute that a number of managers are beginning to realize how they can have a significant impact on the business of their companies.
In our opinion, the biggest changes in private law over the last 50 years will affect all citizens and companies in the Czech Republic.
Due to laminating politicians, the business sector has long been uncertain about whether revolutionary changes will eventually occur or not.
"However, they have only a few weeks left, so that the transition to the new law will take place for their companies without complications," commented Ambruz & Dark Deloitte Legal partner Jan Spáčil.
61 percent of companies working intensively dealing with the new codes said that their companies have already taken all the necessary measures to be prepared in time.
Furthermore, 35 percent of companies from this number are preparing to take the necessary measures in the near future.
Against this, every fifth company has not yet dealt with recodifications, yet it has a specific idea of how to prepare for them.
A total of 11 percent have no opinion and are unable to assess the issue.
Only eight percent of companies admit that they haven't addressed matters related to recodifications yet.
The most important legal topic related to the new Civil Code is for the largest number of surveyed companies the conclusion of new contracts (21 percent), followed by the resolution of business conditions (16 percent) and changes in the Land Registry and in liability for damage (after ten percent).
One of the most important innovations of the whole recodification will take place in contracts, namely the unification of the regulation of contracts only within the framework of the new Civil Code.
The current parallel regulation in both the Civil and Commercial Code will expire, as a result of which the contractual law should become clearer for citizens and entrepreneurs.
"In the case of entrepreneurs, the new regulation will offer many innovations with an emphasis on greater freedom to organize relationships," said Ambruz & Dark Deloitte Legal attorney Lukáš Poddaný.
In connection with the Business Corporations Act, almost a quarter of companies consider the issue of increasing the liability of statutory bodies to be the most important issue.
This is followed by topics related to changes in convening and the course of the general meeting (15 percent) and the representation of the company (14 percent).
The new legislation entails a tightening of obligations and possible impacts for statutory bodies, especially when companies are at risk of bankruptcy or getting into it.
"In practice, this means that if the members of the board of directors and the executives of the companies do not act with the care of a proper manager and do not avert the impending decline of the company, they may lose the benefit earned for the performance of their duties only in the last two years," Spáčil added.
Pierre Rainero: I'm trying to make Cartier's style evolve as a living language
My job is to preserve the tradition while at the same time leading our brand to modern things so that it doesn't remain a dusty relic, Pierre Rainero, director of style at Cartier, explains in an interview with HN.
This French luxury house was built in 1847.
It travels through mud, the river and the jungle and provides free medical care
Dr. Georges Bwelle provides free medical care in the villages of Cameroon.
Bwelle and his team treat hundreds of patients almost every weekend
There aren't too many doctors in Western African countries; just one per 5,000 population.
Vote here or through your mobile phone.
Dr. Georges Bwelle is one of the top 10 candidates for CNN Hero of the Year 2013.
In the competition for the 2013 CNN Hero Prize, you can vote for him or any of the ten main candidates.
The winner will receive 250 000 dollars to continue his extraordinary work.
For 21 years, Georges Bwelle watched his father fall unconscious over and over again and drive to hospitals that weren't equipped enough to help him.
Jamef Bwelle was injured in a car accident near Cameroon capital Yaounde in 1981.
At first, he was treated only with a broken arm, but subsequently developed an infection that spread to the brain, creating a hematoma that had caused him problems all his life.
“There were no neurosurgeons in Cameroon,” says Georges Bwelle.
We'd have to take him out of Cameroon if we had the money.
Instead, Bwelle spent years transporting his father to overcrowded clinics and hospitals, where he was given such treatment that was simply available.
"It's not easy," Bwelle says.
You can leave the house at five in the morning, rush to the hospital to be there first, but you're not the first.
There's a lot of patients.
Some people die only because they have to wait.
Not much has changed since Bwelle's father died in 2002.
According to the World Health Organization, there is only one doctor per 5,000 inhabitants in Cameroon.
Just by comparison, the ratio in the United States is one doctor per 413 residents.
And even if they could visit Cameroonians, many wouldn't be able to afford it.
Two out of five people in this country live below the poverty line, and nearly three-quarters of the country's health care spending is private.
"The only problem they have is poverty," Bwelle says.
They can’t enjoy life because of poverty.
When Bwelle saw his father and many of his compatriots suffered, he decided to do something about it.
Dr. Georges Bwelle and his team of volunteers carried out 700 free operations last year.
He himself became a vascular surgeon at Yaounde Central Hospital.
And he founded the non-profile organization ASCOVIME, whose members visit rural areas over the weekend, where they provide free medical care.
Nearly 32,000 people have assisted with his group of volunteers since 2008.
Almost every Friday, he and up to 30 people are stuffed into vans, attached to the rooftops of medical supplies, and travel through rough terrain to villages that need help.
They're not always accompanied by happiness.
More than once, vehicles have to push through the river and mud.
But when they arrive, they will be greeted as real heroes: a feast, singing and dancing, and the best accommodation that the community can offer.
In these villages, free medical care is truly a reason to celebrate, and Bwelle – with his broad smile and unbridled energy – is very willing to engage in local merriment.
The next day, the team begins to treat hundreds of patients.
“We have 500 patients on every quest,” Bwelle says.
They come to the village from a 60-kilometer radius, on foot.
During each of these weekends, doctors provide different medical assistance.
Many are treated with malaria, tuberculosis, malnutrition, diabetes, parasitic diseases and sexually transmitted diseases.
Others may get crutches or donated glasses or a free birth certificate – a document required by the school, but which many wretched families simply can’t afford.
In the evening, the team performs simple operations under local anesthesia.
Surgeries are usually done in a school building, at town hall or at home; after surgery, the patient gets up and walks to the recovery site to make room for the next patient.
Using the lights from the generator, the operating room and the hygiene equipment Bwelle and his volunteers are working until the early hours of the morning.
It's exhausting, but village musicians usually help the team not to lose motivation.
"To stay up and continue to work, they play drums all night," Bwelle says.
On Sunday, the team will head back to the city, tired but proud of their work.
This group – consisting of Cameroonian doctors and foreign medical students – carried out 700 operations free of charge last year and realizes that their help can mean a lot to locals.
One man said he'd be able to work again after hernia surgery.
"That'll change the future for our family," the man said.
In addition to organizing these weekend clinics and the work of a surgeon at Bwelle Hospital, he also works at night for private medical clinics around Yaounde.
He says that it is precisely this second work that finances about 60% of his non-profit activities; the rest are covered by private contributions.
“I’m not sure when she’s asleep,” says Katie O’Malley, who studies medicine at Drexel University in Philadelphia and volunteers for Bwelle’s group for the second year.
He's always either in the hospital, or he's trying to raise money for an organization so he can do these campaigns.
For students of medicine and nursing services like O'Malley, who came from the United States or Europe to join Bwelle on his mission, it's a great practice they wouldn't get at home.
"To Dr. Bwell, we thoroughly washed our hands and removed the bloodstains, or held his tools," says O'Malley.
As a second-year medical student, you'll never experience it in America.
Students volunteers usually pay their way to Cameroon themselves and often arrive with donated medical supplies.
But as soon as they arrive in Yaounde, food, transportation and study are provided by Bwelle.
“No doubt he’s a hero,” O’Malley said.
This organization gives its life, and its desire to help the people of Cameroon is endless.
For Bwelle, a constant harness is not a burden.
Helping others to live more happily, fulfilling the promise he made to his father is something that brings him great joy.
"I'm so happy when I'm doing this job," Bwelle says.
And I'm thinking about my father.
I hope they can see what I'm doing.
To make people laugh, to ease their pain – that’s why I’m doing it.
Take a look at ASCOVIME's website and you'll find how you can help.
Minister Holcát has renewed the position of Principal Nurse for the Czech Republic
The head nurse for the Czech Republic should represent the interests of nurses, whose number in the Czech Republic exceeds 100 thousand.
"I consider it essential that this function is clearly defined and formalized, so that nurses have their representatives within the structure of the office - a representative to whom they can always turn if necessary," explained Martin Holcát, Minister of Health.
I appreciate the work of the nurses and I think that he deserves the field of nursing to be given proper attention by the Ministry.
In the exercise of this profession, not only high professionalism is needed, but also the human approach and personal beliefs.
"I will be happy, with all responsibility, to try to enforce those steps that can improve the position of nurses."
"We have excellent and handy nurses in our health care, but I would also like to appeal to those colleagues who do not help us too much with their approach to patients to carry out their calls with all their empathy and help us to improve the image of nurses in the eyes of the public," says Alena Šmídová.
Alena ŠmídovÁ, on the occasion of her entry into office, also stated that she wants to deal with various aspects of a nurse’s position and intends, among other things, to cooperate with professional associations, professional societies, educators, other non-medical professions and doctors.
"I fundamentally don't want to separate the profession of doctors and nurses, but on the contrary I see them standing next to each other, with everyone having their competences and together with other healthcare professionals forming a well-coordinated working team that provides health services for the benefit of the patient."
"I myself encounter a number of problems that occur in the profession of nurses, I perceive these difficulties and I intend to participate in solving them."
These include, for example, ensuring the simplification of nursing and medical documentation.
"I know from my own experience that some administrative tasks unnecessarily burden nurses and take away their time, which they could and should devote to patients," says Alena Šmídová.
Other areas he intends to focus on include the improvement of working conditions, finding ways for better mutual cooperation in the team and with the managements of health service providers, regulation of competencies, as well as employment issues such as compliance with the Labor Code or non-overloading of nurses by their insufficient number at a given time and place.
We often hear anonymous complaints, but out of fear of possible punishment, are not documented on a specific case where violations of the rules could occur.
"Where there is no plaintiff, there is not even a judge, and so in this case, the function of the Principal Sister of the Czech Republic could serve as a guarantee of maintaining the anonymity of such a complaint and have a specific case examined by the competent authorities," concludes Alena Šmídová.
Alena Šmídová graduated from the Secondary Medical School in Nurse (she graduated in 1978) and the Faculty of Philosophy of Charles University in Prague in the study programme Nursing - Pedagogy (she graduated in 1992).
Since 2010 he has been working as the head of the Department of Nursing and other non-medical disciplines and the recognition of qualifications at the Department of Education and Science of the Ministry of Education.
During her professional career she worked as a bedridger in the internal ward, she also worked as a teacher at the Secondary Medical School, where she was also a statutory deputy director for 8 years.
From 2004 to 2009 she worked as the head nurse of the Prison Service of the Czech Republic.
She has published several professional textbooks of nursing and articles in professional journals, devoted herself to lecture activities in the field of nursing.
She's not a member of any political party.
So I wouldn't get choked by hate
Writer Václav Kahuda returned to writing after twelve years.
After many years of reading, living but not writing, VÁCLAV KAHUDA (1965) publishes a new novel The Wind, Darkness, Presence.
That it will not be a slim volume suggests the number of pages comparable to his most famous work Houština (Petrov, 2001).
Another prominent Czech contemporary writer, Emil Hakl, testified for Salon Kahudu.
What's moved that you've written a novel after twelve years of silence?
I've done it - so I don't sway myself with hatred.
Seeing all the time new and new clones of that human foolish pettiness, young organisms affected by blind selfish efforts.
I don't even talk about the elders of us, faces ruined by helpless lushness, wolving souls, and vice versa-polary ideologues trying to sell their "product" with the expression of a small selling whore.
At the beginning of the millennium, I bought a small digital camera and filmed my jurodious friends, weirdos and hidden formations.
I've passed.
Sometimes I can feel my brains burning from life on this planet.
I prefer to go for walks and then in the evening I read books at the lamp.
You're talking about a sense of devastation, hatred.
Was there a turning point in the past that affected you, or you're just getting older, like all of us?
The turning point came a long time ago, at about ten years old.
It didn't have a name then.
Well, that's what I've been alive after.
There's still a quiet boy somewhere inside.
I see other solitary exercisers, each in their tornado anxiety.
It saved me to write books.
I'm taking part in that huge, thousand-year-long movement of the system of life on this planet - the birth of an incomprehensible future.
The immense, breathtaking complexity of life, when every stone is the direct heir to our presence.
We are the players of a sad and beautiful game, when the trial-and-mistake arises the next and next generation of beings, who in trillions of interactions per second create some superhuman, artificial intelligence.
Twelve years is a relatively long time.
Do you see your writing as an ongoing process, or did you start completely from scratch?
Self-talk, or rather, a dialogue conducted by the right and left half of the brain, sometimes floats to the surface and forms into a book.
Just as periods of peace are interwoven with quietly sleeping metastases of wars that may awaken one day - my book is twelve years of silence dressed in words.
I felt the mute avalanche of my life growing in my brain, and if I don't do anything, one day I'll be quietly overwhelmed.
It's a set of questions formulated by the brain and asks them to layers that are stored in darkness "under the surface of consciousness."
The neocortex, the gray cortex, asks older evolutionary formations, goes to the ancient rings of the cerebracy tree.
He speaks to furry beasts, asks snakes, searches for an answer in the flight of birds, raises his eyes to the clouds in the sky.
Yes, the moral law in me and the stellar sky above me.
Finally, the brain askes the world.
Relatively often you express yourself to the ego metaphorically - would you be able to define it biologically?
The brain preserves in its billion connections and electrochemicals all the memories, experiences and experiences that the person in question has experienced.
This forest logically and emotionally exposed and branching trees of information, memory registers, is used by the brain in its recognition, cognitive, process, is exposed to a constantly changing presence, in an unwavering effort trying to name and understand its surroundings and decode important messages.
Sometimes there are overwhelmed disorders.
By analogy - as if your PC "fell", from the speech emulator program and translator from foreign languages, you would get tangled first in the operating environment, for example Windows, and after several unsuccessancy reboots you ended up with a darkly green-blinking screen of Bios, in a basic system that manages functions and cooperation on the motherboard, between the processor and the operating memory, etc.
For the one who hasn't yet understood what we're talking about - I'm talking about a psychospiritual crisis.
Do you mention it often - why are you regularly interested in this type of regression?
'Cause it's a pit.
In the still functional mechanisms of our thinking, feeling and behavior - a rift appears.
Here it is good to quote Nietzsche: "So long he gazed into the abyss until the abyss looked into him."
A pure feeling - that a person is lost, if all hope disappears, mentally "dies" and a new quality begins.
And why do I care about it personally?
Responding with a parable.
Once one fell into the pool and drowned, running days, months and years, he was still drowning and reading, and those books taught him how to float - to live.
He became a "diver."
Perhaps someone's written sentence will help someone, as Hrabal's lyrics once helped me.
To what extent do you think about the reader, the particular person who will read your text?
The one about which nothing meaningful can be said, some call it consciousness, another super ego, but in my opinion Arthur Rimbaud best characterized it in a letter to a friend:
"I'm someone else - this reader participates directly in the process of formulating words and sentences."
From an early age, he read books of old Czech fairy tales, sailed in Captain Nemo's submarine, running around Stínadly in dark alleys.
In the end, behind all the branches of the authors and their message, it reads: "The soul of the bright worlds of other Karel Hynek Mácha".
And when the book's outside?
Do you care about the reader's opinion?
On the retro-reflection that gets to you through the people who read the book?
Of course, I care about the opinion of people who take three hundred crowns and are willing to read five hundred pages of text.
And then, for example, after years, they randomly contact me on the street and thank me for helping them with my book in some of their difficult life decisions.
Books are actually "lives" or, better said, "worlds."
They are encoded in letter characters, they carry the experience, the history of the regions of each language.
At any time, we can bring them to life if we let their sentences flow to the brain.
It's actually, in a way, something forbidden divine - awakening more and more life stories, small universes and temporary planets.
It's a fact whose DNA is laid down - according to old demonological books, we're committing witchcraft.
After the publication of the book, as is the case, a chain of author reading awaits you
I remember you used to love reading.
Are you happy to throw yourself into it again, or do you feel it differently after years?
Today, I prefer to just talk.
And what did I find out?
There are good intelligent people everywhere.
Their basic character - they're inconspicuous, they don't draw too much attention to each other, they're friendly radiants, you don't feel lonely with them.
I'll give you an example - in the town of Červený Kostelec there was (and I hope it's still alive) one curly bookseller (it's advisibly called Červenka), a merry friend and a good girl's bull.
During one drink, he presented me with individual articles of a string of readers of my book Houština.
I stared into their eyes, and then I was shown a crumbling, fed-up specimen of my book.
Embarrassingly, I put down the just-readed jitrnice and wept briefly.
In silence, we all stood there, swaying, tearing drunkenly with emotion.
Then the young blonde half-breed couldn't stand it, and with a roar he jumped out of the window into a snowdrift.
It was downstairs.
We ran out into the night's landscape, our happy rabies of the sensitives, the sore souls of the compatriots, and the sadness of sunken patriots - howled and pampered like werewolves until morning.
Each of your books is formally different than the previous ones.
Is this also true of the text Wind, darkness, presence?
It's a narrative.
It's a fairy tale about one land behind nine rivers and nine mountains.
Ivan Bunin, in his novel Suchodol, puts the words of an old Russian myth in the mouth of a folk healer: At sea, on the ocean, on the island of Bujanu.
There's a dog lying on it, a gray chest.
Horror, dread, mystery.
It's never happened, but we all know it from somewhere.
We may have been devils in hell a long time ago - what role we will play on this earth.
Do you intend to keep writing, or will you leave it again on a free tide of inspiration?
The answers are eternal - Otokar Březina.
Writing is a wonderful way of dialogue.
Something huge and unnamable, similar to the movement of continents on the planet, influences our desire from such a depth and in such breadth that it completely exceeds our horizon - and so, of course, we act precisely in the intentions of our programmed assignment.
We are rewarded with an influx of dopamine, a mixture of endorphins and other substances.
We're biological machines.
We want to mate, we die in the defense and acquisition of new personal territories.
Occasionally there's a disorder, and we radiate compassion.
That's when we have the right to call ourselves people.
That's the answer to your question - the biological robot is already full of various disturbances, standing on the coast of the ocean of the unconscious and catching the tides of warming wind into a leaky tarp made from a dirty handkerchief.
I have to write so I can be human.
Obama retreats from health care
After a wave of criticism, President Obama backed away yesterday from his offently repeated and ambitious promise that "if you like your health program, you can keep it."
Citing hundreds of thousands of notices of testimony from providers Republicans in recent days accused the president of deceiving the U.S. public.
Yesterday, Obama flinched from his original vow.
"The vast majority of people who have functioning health insurance can keep them," he said in a speech in Boston.
Considering what he called a "commotion in the media" about testimonies, Obama urged Americans who have received those announcements to search for new safeguards in the market.
Most people will be able to have a better, more comprehensive health care program at the same price or even cheaper than anticipated.
"You'll get a better offer," he said.
His administration argues that it shouldn't be a surprise that 5 percent of the population who have purchased insurance on their own may be forced to change the program because their insurance doesn't meet the new requirements given by the Affordable Care Act.
"I'm telling these Americans frankly: you deserve something better," Sebelius said in her testimony before Washington's Energy and Commerce Committee.
Sebelius, who oversees the implementation of the Affordable Care Act, said the launch of the internet market had gone "poorly" since October.
"I'm disappointed and angry like everyone else," she said.
I'll try to regain your trust.
Irritated Sebelius made this sentence, which was picked up by the microphone, yesterday, towards an adviser sitting behind her at yesterday's committee meeting, which took place after a tumultuouous exchange of views with Republican Billy Long, a Missouri representative concerned whether she, too, had to be involved in Obama's care program.
More than three hours during the hearing on Sebelius, he repeatedly questioned why the “architect” of the Affordabile Care Act didn’t voluntarily give up government-sponsored insurance, and didn’t buy the program through HealthCare.gov, which now imposes on millions of Americans.
Which billionaire are you going to be on the phone with next year?
Kellner, the owner of the PPF group, wants to buy an O2 operator.
Both companies have confirmed the negotiations, and according to various sources from the agencies and from behind the scenes, it is said that the hand in the sleeve is already in the sleeve.
The announcement of the transaction is expected to come in November, probably before the start of the LTE frequency auction, before November 11.
Kellner's competitor Karel Komárek owns the KKCG group.
Under Sazka Telecommunications, it was auctioned frequencies for LTE networks.
And to the general surprise, until then there was no talk about the company's desire to penetrate telecommunications.
But Sazka is now led by Robert Chvátal, a very experienced telecommunications man.
He started as the marketing boss of Radiomobil (now T-Mobile), later became the director of the Slovak T-Mobile, and most recently headed the Austrian branch of the same operator.
Sazka plays several cards at once.
She signed up for the auction, and now she's admitting she wants to run a virtual at Vodafone.
And it gives logic.
If he succeeds in the auction, he will be able to move existing customers to his network from his own virtual operator.
Vodafone probably wouldn't like it under normal circumstances, but according to the information of Hospodářské noviny, it can be part of the much closer cooperation of both companies.
Vodafone is currently suffering from the agreement of rival operators O2 and T-Mobile, who will completely share their networks.
Vodafone needs a partner.
And that could be Sazka.
If it succeeds in the tender on LTE networks (as a newcomer it can auction a specially designated spectrum), Vodafone does not have to go to the auction at all, or it will not mind auctioning only limited spectrum (there is not enough frequencies for everyone).
Vodeka can share networks with Sazka.
Vodake will release Sazka into its 2G/3G network (or the newly built LTE network at a frequency of 900 MHz), thus enabling the company to quickly start the network.
In the meantime, Sazka will start to build an expensive LTE network, which Vodafone will be able to use.
Both companies can be satisfied.
Vodafone could then spend the saved money on some fixed internet or cable TV provider.
And Kellner?
It would acquire a full-fledged operator with still decent profits and a wide range of services by purchasing the Czech and Slovak O2 (the Slovak Branch is the daughter of a Czech one).
But, on the other hand, it's a big moloch that's going to await restructuring.
In the auction, Kellner would acquire LTE networks already for O2 and very likely in conjunction with T-Mobile.
Both companies have already declared that they will share the networks.
Thus, the auction could finally end in success, but quite possibly not with the high amounts that the state budget is looking forward to.
And from next year, customers in the Czech Republic could choose between calls with Vodafone, T-Mobile, Kellner or Komárek.
Or two titans of Czech business against global giants.
Will Czech Television ever do without signing events?
It is not difficult to listen to a complaint that collects the documents of the castle club.
In the past, the traditional influence of Ivan Langer or Jaroslav Tvrdík has not been documented in this way, let alone to sign it.
True, the editors stood up for the leadership.
