Maybe we just haven't reached this stage of civilization yet, and this situation isn't with us.
But perhaps also in a country that thinks it is the most corrupt in the world, black-and-white stories about evil politicians and good journalists spend more comfortably than more complicated descriptions of a confusing situation in which the behavior of many imperfect human beings comes across.
JAN JANDOUREK: The curse of this country: presidents on the brink of power
It is almost a rule that at key moments of the country, the head of state has not been in such a way as to be able to fulfill his statesmanship obligations.
If T. Masaryk had been physically and mentally five years younger, he would have lived to see the end of his presidency, and the events surrounding Munich would have happened a little differently.
We probably wouldn't have won the war, but after a symbolic resistance, the air force could fly away, heavy weapons could be destroyed, and the government could flee to London right away and not fight laboriously for its legitimacy.
And most importantly, from that surrender, the nation wouldn't have hungover to this day.
Beneš was not a statesman and he was still in shape during Munich, but during the war it started to go downhill with him and in February 48 he behaved accordingly.
To be a little better, he could have gone into exile for the third time and undermined the legitimacy of the communists and their coups.
It's a pity to talk about Hácha.
They elected him, saying that "Mr. President will not get into a situation that he would make decisions."
But he had to, even though the big decision-making with a knife on his neck by Hitler in the chancellery probably wasn't.
