In what state of health is he?
In an unprecedented confession, John Kerry said U.S. espionage had "gone too far"
John Kerry hinted at a refinement U.S. defense stance about its surveillance programs and, in an unprecedented confession, said that in some cases espionage had "gone too far."
The Secretary of State also acknowledged that he, along with Barack Obama, was responsible for acting "autopilot" in view of an annoying revelations about NSA espionage activity that emerged thanks to whistleblower Edward Snowden.
The released information sparked a diplomatic storm between the U.S. government and its allies.
Mr. Kerry, through a video-link at an open government conference in London, said: "There is no doubt that the president and myself and the others in government actually learned about some of the things that were happening out of inertia, because the possibility, with reference to the Second World War, was a difficult period of the Cold War, and then, of course, 9/11."
Thus, he became the first high-ranking member of the U.S. government who admitted that U.S. espionage had crossed the line, but stressed that it had not violated anyone's rights.
He said: "In some cases it's gone too far."
And the president is determined to try to explain it to the people, and he is already conducting a thorough review so that no one feels there has been abuse.
I assure you there is no abuse of the rights of innocent persons in this process.
However, Mr Kerry insisted that the NSA strives for good and that its surveillance operations have saved many lives.
He added: "We are dealing with a new world in which people are willing to detonate themselves."
There is radical extremism in the world that is firmly determined to murder and blow up people and attack governments.
And what if you were able to intercept and stop it before it happens?
In fact, we prevented planes from falling, buildings didn't explode and people weren't executed, because we can already learn about these plans in advance.
The president of the Senate Subcommittee on European Affairs said Thursday that U.S. lawmakers were going to Europe to respond to fears from abroad regarding alleged American espionage and to convince Europeans the need to continue joining forces in the fight against terrorism.
Senator Chris Murphy of Connecticut said he had spoken with members of the European Parliament and others this week and that he was worried about their threats to stop participation in anti-terrorist organizations because they were disillusioned with surveillance by the National Security Agency (NSA).
It is really important for U.S. national security interests that Europe stay with us in the same ship, taking into account joint efforts in the fight against terrorism, Murphy, a member of the Democratic Party and presidioner of the Senate Subcommittee on Foreign Relations for European Affairs, said in an interview.
And I'm going to Europe to make it clear that we need to continue to work together to combat terrorism regardless of their rage over the NSA's programs.
Reports in the media that the NSA collected millions of phone records in Europe violated relations with some U.S. allies, even though the agency's chief said this week was inaccurate and reflected misunderstood metadata that NATO allies collected and shared with the United States,
Other revelations cited documents leaked by Snowden that the NSA tracked the mobile phone of German Chancellor Angela Merkel and up to 34 other world leaders.
The director of national intelligence, James Clapper, defended the espionagement of the allies as necessary, saying it was a normal matter from both sides.
In the midst of the turmoil, Murphy said his office was planning a parliamentary trip that should take place this year, and he hopes members of both parties and chambers will appear in the delegation.
In the coming days, the names of other legislators intending to participate are to be published.
He said there's still work to be worked out on
While Murphy said the goal of the trip was to help measure relationships, he added that "well-meaning educational measures" would also be prepared.
He said that European leaders must be honest with their own people when it comes to spying programs themselves have benefited for years.
"While we are adjusting our surveillance programmes to better protect the rights of Europeans, they, too, must realize that we are not the only ones conducting espionage here," Murphy said.
In the meantime, Mr. Kerry is heading to the Middle East and Poland this weekend to respond to the hostilities triggered by the U.S. strategy in Syria, Egypt and Iran, as well as U.S. surveillance activities.
The Australian has appealed against staying in a Thai prison
A twenty-one-year-old woman from Sydney, who was sentenced to a 15-day imprisonment on the island of Phuket when she falsely claimed to have been attacked by a taxi driver, appealed the verdict and was released on bail.
Stevie Rochelle Bamford was originally charged with perjury on June 15 by a provincial court on Phuket Island when she told Thai police that the taxi driver, along with two other men who allegedly detained her, assaulted her in the early hours of Sunday, June 10.
However, an industrial television footage later revealed that she returned safely to her hotel after breaking up with her Australian boyfriend.
Police from Phuket Island held talks with Bamford for two days before confessing to making up the incident.
She was held in a local police cell before the trial.
Bamford was sentenced to 15 days in prison at a low-security correctional facility on the outskirts of Phuket, rather than being placed in prison for adult women.
He is the daughter of former Australian league player Peter Tunks, who has appealed for help from the Ministry of Foreign Affairs in Canberra in his daughter's case.
Tunks told Sydney's Sunday Telegraph that the whole family had "extreme concerns" about their daughter's fate and wanted her back in Australia.
"Evidently, it's a challenging time, but we're hoping to come back safely as soon as possible," Tunks said.
Bamford appealed against the verdict and was dismissed on bail of 50,000 baht.
Reports from Australia say she had meanwhile spent a holiday in the Krabi recreation area in southern Thailand.
Legal sources in Thailand say Bamford was represented by a local lawyer in Phuket, but was warned that the appeal could lead to legal proceedings, which will increase her sentence and result in a placement for adults.
However, after the recent assassination of an Australian travel employee Michelle Smith in Phuket, Thailand may also be trying to correct a damaged image for tourists, which may have been behind the liberating verdict.
