Instead of a post-election candle post-election stew
October's elections, rather than the winners, brought a bunch of losers.
It is not only parties that did not get into the Chamber of Deputies (e.g. SPOZ), but also the previous government parties (ODS, TOP 09) and finally the ČSSD because of their internal split.
Politicians served us post-election stew over the weekend instead of the festive sirloin.
Andrej Babiš's movement, which promised voters to change the current political style of governance.
The fragmentation of the election result and the split in the winning party is playing into the political (from)will of President Miloš Zeman, regardless of the wishes of the voters, who did not elect the party that President Zeman has publicly supported into the Chamber of Deputies.
Although the president has lost a strategic lead over other politicians due to an unpleasant event, he still has a few ways to get him back - for example, by selecting a future prime minister.
While the ČSSD’s chances of winning the prime minister’s seat are moving away in my eyes, entrepreneur Andrej Babiš, on the contrary, is getting closer.
The post-election development is still miles away from the early and smooth creation of a stable government, but compared to the pre-election scenario of the left-winning CSSD-supported government, this development for business in the Czech Republic and therefore the economy is still better news.
Due to the loud resistance of ANO to get together with the ODS or TOP 09, it is still the most likely option of a coalition of ČSSD, ANO and KDU-ČSL (a total of 111 deputies).
Due to the split in the ČSSD, which has thus become an illegible and weak partner in the negotiations, the outcome of the coalition negotiations can be quite daunting for the ČSSD.
The minority government of the ČSSD, composed with "non-zemanites" at the Castle, is unlikely to pass.
Moreover, any government in which the CSSD will participate will be threatened by the unsatisfied ambitions of many Social Democrats, which may lead sooner or later to a further split in the party.
So I ask myself: "Aren't we going to the next early election?"
The next election would be more of a referendum on the strength of the Yes movement's electoral mandate.
ČSSD’s plans to introduce sector taxes, significantly increase income taxes or build state banks have come under the table.
On the contrary, the ČSSD, ANO, KDU-ČSL and Úsvit parties agree, for example, on promoting entrepreneurship, moderate fiscal policy (government deficit below 3% of GDP), on supporting the employment of disadvantaged people, reducing the lower VAT rate for selected goods.
Friction areas occur most in terms of social policy and the size of the state.
Yes, however, has the upper hand now, so the agenda of the most likely coalition of YES, ČSSD, KDU-ČSL would lean rather to the right.
Which, from my point of view, is ultimately a better outcome than a left-wing tax program for anything still moving in the economy.
